import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Mark",
  display: "Mark",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"The gospels are carefully considered historical proofs of scripture. When there is a man who comes to you and says, \"How do you know Jesus existed?\" Well, these eyewitness accounts would be an acclimation to that. There is a lot of debate about who wrote what book, but I am going to trust the research, study, and time dedicated by far smarter people than me to assume they are right to address the authors."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most people believe this gospel was written by John Mark, a man that was not an eyewitness to Christ, but became a disciple of Christ through the preaching and teaching of the first-century church (specifically through Peter and Paul's preaching, which can be further expounded in the book of Acts). This is the oldest gospel account written, and it is said to have been purposefully written to account for the facts of Jesus' true ministry. Apparently, Peter had been preaching a storm to some first-time hearers, and these people enjoyed his sermon and testimony so much that they asked for it in writing. That is where some of the questions come in."},{"kind":"para","text":"We assume that John Mark wrote it, and I won't take away from the evidence of that, but I want to claim Peter as the author of it. This is Peter's account of Jesus' ministry. Matthew's account is all about what Jesus said. We will see that Mark's account is all about what Jesus did. We can see throughout the book of Acts that Mark was Peter's right-hand man. His \"son in the faith,\" if you will. It seems that all of the Apostles had at least one man that would help serve the ministry with them, and Mark happened to be that man."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's sufficient to give some background understanding of Mark before going further, so we know his testimony is true. He is the cousin of Barnabas (a man that was extremely helpful to Paul's ministry in the beginning)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Aristarchus my fellowprisoner saluteth you, and Marcus, sister's son to Barnabas, (touching whom ye received commandments: if he come unto you, receive him;)","ref":"Colossians 4:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Mark was heavily influenced by Peter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when he had considered the thing, he came to the house of Mary the mother of John, whose surname was Mark; where many were gathered together praying. And as Peter knocked at the door of the gate, a damsel came to hearken, named Rhoda. And when she knew Peter's voice, she opened not the gate for gladness, but ran in, and told how Peter stood before the gate.","ref":"Acts 12:12-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter even calls him his son:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The church that is at Babylon, elected together with you, saluteth you; and so doth Marcus my son.","ref":"1 Peter 5:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"The writing style of this book is consistent with Mark's background and character (as well as Peter's accounts). We see that Peter only speaks primarily (in Acts particularly) about the life, death, resurrection, and ascension of Jesus over and over again. He doesn't deviate from that message. We will see Mark use the term \"immediately\" or \"straightway\" quite a lot throughout these pages of scripture. \"Immediately\" means \"straight, level, forward, upright, true, sincere, forthwith.\" \"Straightway\" means \"immediately\" or \"forthwith.\" It means Jesus did things with intention and clarity. There was no second thought about it; He simply moved to where He knew He was supposed to be next. When we read it in this context, we will think that Jesus' ministry was extremely hectic and constantly moving, but the other gospel accounts give us a deeper picture of Christ's heart and speed. He never did things in a hurry. In the eyes of Peter, however, everything seemed to be going by so quickly. It's another reason we have to believe it's Peter giving this account. He was a big part of Jesus' ministry dealings (including being involved with a few events that none of the other disciples were involved with (other than James and John))."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will also see (in this gospel account) that some of the embarrassing things that the other accounts give to Peter are omitted (or removed) from this gospel presentation. Peter has chosen not to talk about certain things either for continuity purposes (speeding along the events of Jesus' ministry) or out of a desire to keep his dirty laundry from being aired out as little as possible (unless he absolutely has to). The other writers don't seem to care though and just tell it like it is. We can look a little deeper into this through specific portions of these accounts, and I will try my best to compare it to other gospel accounts where it is not nearly as strongly recognized (or as in-depth) in this account."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our hope (and goal from what I can gather) in this gospel message is to get the overall picture of Jesus' ministry and purpose in a simple, quick, and painless account for the most simple-minded person to read. There is not much in this account that makes you wonder (some words perhaps that should be clarified a bit more), but it's all pretty straightforward as to who Jesus is and what He came here for."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"John The Baptizer","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mark hits the nail on the head here at once. First, he speaks about this gospel account being the \"beginning\" of the gospel. The other gospel accounts have not been written yet, so technically Mark is accurate in his assertion here about this one being the first. This is also the beginning of the message that he wants to preach. This is the method in which Peter preached to people. He doesn't start with the birth of Jesus, or even with the person of Jesus. Rather, he starts with John the Baptist (who we will see in a moment) and the initiation of Jesus' open ministry. This is how it came to be (at least in the eyes of Peter)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The second thing that Mark gets very clear (in this first verse) is that Jesus Christ is an important figure in this gospel account. He will be our main character. It might not seem like that in the next couple of verses, but Jesus is the theme of the entire Bible. Why is He our main character? Well (according to Mark), He is the Son of God. Mark starts this whole account off by proclaiming something extremely bold (especially towards the Jewish readers that would hear this message for the first time). The Gentiles are going to hear this message and be fascinated (I believe that is who this is primarily addressed to). Peter was not afraid to call Jesus who He really is."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see that Peter wasn't always like that (even in this gospel account), but eventually Peter becomes a bold preacher about the authority and proclamation of Jesus Christ being the Son of God. Now Peter is going to take that proclamation and prove it through the remainder of this book."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Verse two says that this was written in the “prophets.\" Plural. We know that since the beginning, God's Word has been gearing up for the coming of the Messiah. But there are actually two specific spots where this is referenced in the Old Testament and a prophecy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I send an Angel before thee, to keep thee in the way, and to bring thee into the place which I have prepared.\" Exodus 23:20 \"Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me: and the Lord, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple, even the messenger of the covenant, whom ye delight in: behold, he shall come, saith the LORD of hosts.","ref":"Malachi 3:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Verse three is specifically referenced from Isaiah:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the LORD, make straight in the desert a highway for our God.","ref":"Isaiah 40:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"There has been sent messenger after messenger to this Earth to warn and gear people towards the Lord, and they have all been rejected. Now we have John the Baptist (whom we will get to know a little bit better in a moment). He was a man that prepared the way for the Lord DURING the time of the Lord's Earthly ministry. He will also be rejected, as well as the Lord Himself. John spent his days \"crying\" (that is, “preaching with compassion”) towards the people in his day to come to the knowledge of salvation. He preaches a very specific message:"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He preaches a message about repentance and baptism. Baptism is not a salvation tactic (I think we should understand that very clearly right off the bat). We do not get baptized to be saved. That has never been a method of salvation (and we will look into that deeper as we go along, especially in Acts). He preached about baptism because he is \"preparing the way\" for something better than his water baptism. Baptism was a symbol of what was to come. Christ would bring something better."},{"kind":"para","text":"Repentance is the thing that hangs people up, however. Baptism is one topic saved throughout multiple facets of scripture (you can jump to the letters from Peter if you want to get a quick note on this), but repentance is something that must be established right here in this chapter of scripture in order for us to get a clear view of it. \"Repentance\" means \"a change of mind, as it appears to one who repents, of a purpose he has formed or of something he has done.” There are people who say that \"repentance is a work.” Repentance is not a work if it's simply just \"changing your mind\" according to Strong's Concordance. It's simply saying, \"You know what, I'm wrong, and I want to accept what is right.” However, there is a continual repentance after salvation."},{"kind":"para","text":"The best way I can describe repentance is in a twofold manner. The first is through salvation. In order for someone to be saved, they have to be willing to change their mind about their sin. They are certainly FOR their sin before Christ, but when they come to Christ they have a choice to make. Do they reject Him and continue their life of sin, or do they accept Him and repent from it? That is what I refer to as \"initial repentance.” That is the start of their salvation."},{"kind":"para","text":"After they take that step forward, they are then in a mode of what I call \"continual repentance.” They will be changing their minds and attitudes about literally everything they thought was good before if it rejects the Lord's grace and glory. That is the message that John is preaching, and he's preaching it because that is the lifestyle that one must live if they are going to genuinely follow the Lord in faith and believing. We cannot just have faith. We must actually FOLLOW God. We cannot follow Him without a repentant Spirit. Faith and repentance walk hand-in-hand. The book of James is full of representations of this."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Confessing their sins specifically to the Lord. They weren't confessing their sins to John the Baptist. Again, James gives clarity:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much.","ref":"James 5:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have a fault with someone, talk to that person. You can only sin against the Lord, and so you must go to God with your sin. They were trusting John's message about the Messiah, and about baptism, and about repentance, and they were being baptized right there in the Jordan River (a river that is picked up over and over again throughout Scripture). The Jordan is always a reference to new life in the Lord. Every time God's children cross that river, it depicts new life."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John seems like an interesting fellow. I had seen an ad recently on Instagram about this company who is making Christian protein bars. Their most famous flavor is (apparently) Locust's and Honey. They claim it's the most biblically blessed food you are going to eat. I wonder if they will make a \"manna\" flavored one next? You have to admit that this does not sound appealing at all. This is a very strange direction to take your business in, and I don't imagine that there would even be many Christians who would say, \"Hmm, that actually sounds like a good way to live.” We get this picture of John the Baptist and we (should anyway) immediately think, \"This guy seems a bit out there.” If people wouldn't listen to his message, they would come just to see how crazy he looks, that's for sure."}]},{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the essence of his message. He speaks about how glorifying God is, and how little he is in comparison. He talks about his purpose and method of baptism. Then (lastly) he talks about what his baptism represents. It represents the giving of the Holy Ghost! The other gospel accounts give a different description."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I indeed baptize you with water unto repentance: but he that cometh after me is mightier than I, whose shoes I am not worthy to bear: he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost, and with fire:","ref":"Matthew 3:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are two methods that a person will be baptized. There is a lot of debate as to what means what. Some say that \"fire baptism\" insinuates trials. I don't disagree with that idea. It very much COULD mean that. Others believe that he's talking about the choice between salvation and damnation. Christ is the deciding factor as to whether or not you will live in the Holy Ghost, or live in unquenchable fire."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whose fan is in his hand, and he will throughly purge his floor, and gather his wheat into the garner; but he will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire.","ref":"Matthew 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter seems to leave off the \"fire\" part in his account though. He is making it well known that the more prominent and important baptism to take is the baptism of the Holy Spirit. Water baptism is just a symbol (like circumcision). That's something we can talk more about later."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":11,"title":"Jesus Is Baptized","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that this was the indicating factor of initiating Jesus' Earthly ministry. I know Jesus was doing ministry things before this moment, but it was not openly shown until this very moment here. This was the moment that people started to see who Jesus was and what He was going to do."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Notice the “straightway.\" As soon as Jesus came out of the water, the Holy Spirit was evident to everyone around Him. They all were pretty confident at that point who this Jesus fellow was. The other accounts talk about John saying, \"Behold the Lamb of God who takes away the sins of the world.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The next day John seeth Jesus coming unto him, and saith, Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world.","ref":"John 1:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"This account does not mention this, however. Peter wants to address the Lord's ministry, and so this was the initiation of the gospel being evidentially presented for the first time to mankind. You might say, “Well, why did Jesus get baptized? Was He not already saved?” This further proves that Baptism does not have a saving work. Jesus is fully God even before this baptism. It was a testimony (just as it still is today). The purpose of this baptism was to show that He (Jesus Christ who is fully God) is also FULLY man. He will live a human life, not an extraterrestrial one."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"title":"Jesus Is Tempted","entries":[{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again we get that word “immediately.\" The other gospel accounts address the fact that there were some decisions being made between Christ's baptism and his wilderness trek. It seems strange that it says here he was in the wilderness for forty days."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, he was afterward an hungred.","ref":"Matthew 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"So which is it? Did He spend His forty days in the wilderness, or did He fast for forty days first and THEN go into the wilderness? Peter and Matthew must get their stories straight if we are going to believe the gospel message at all. Honestly, I think it's a viewpoint of the same thing. I believe they link together. Matthew gives more clarity compared to Mark (again, Peter seems to be cutting out a lot of deeper descriptions in his gospel account). They are both right. He fasted for forty days IN the wilderness, but it was in those forty days that Satan came to tempt Jesus. He was hungry AFTER, but He was tempted during. We also notice in this gospel account that there is no further detail about what Satan tried to tempt Jesus with. Thankfully, we have a deeper illustration in the book of Matthew. Again, though, this is all build-up for Jesus' ultimate ministry pursuit."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"title":"The Kingdom of God","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, there were a lot of other things happening between Jesus' wilderness trek and John's imprisonment. But this is where Jesus starts to make things public. This is where people start to take recognition of His work. John preached about the preparation of Jesus. Jesus preached about the preparation of God's reign."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The \"gospel\" simply means \"the good news!” The good news is that God's kingdom is \"at hand.” It's near! It's here! John's message and Jesus' message walk together."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And saying, Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.","ref":"Matthew 3:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The kingdom of Heaven is eternal. The kingdom of God is paternal. The kingdom of God is here for us to experience while we are alive in this flesh. The kingdom of Heaven is what we will experience when we are out of this flesh and living in the Spirit with the Lord. They both exist together, and He is the king of both."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"Fishers of Men","entries":[{"start":16,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems straightforward enough. This is how Peter and Andrew come to know the Lord. We read this account and say, “Well, they must have been fishing, and Jesus just so happens to walk by and invite them in the direction they are going.” They seem to just agree that it would be fun to follow this stranger to \"catch men.” Nothing strange or absurd about this at all."}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, nothing strange about four grown men deciding they want to follow this complete stranger to catch men. You’ll notice this is missing a very key context that Peter seems to think was not necessary to mention."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, that, as the people pressed upon him to hear the word of God, he stood by the lake of Gennesaret, And saw two ships standing by the lake: but the fishermen were gone out of them, and were washing their nets. And he entered into one of the ships, which was Simon's, and prayed him that he would thrust out a little from the land. And he sat down, and taught the people out of the ship. Now when he had left speaking, he said unto Simon, Launch out into the deep, and let down your nets for a draught. And Simon answering said unto him, Master, we have toiled all the night, and have taken nothing: nevertheless at thy word I will let down the net. And when they had this done, they inclosed a great multitude of fishes: and their net brake. And they beckoned unto their partners, which were in the other ship, that they should come and help them. And they came, and filled both the ships, so that they began to sink. When Simon Peter saw it, he fell down at Jesus' knees, saying, Depart from me; for I am a sinful man, O Lord. For he was astonished, and all that were with him, at the draught of the fishes which they had taken: And so was also James, and John, the sons of Zebedee, which were partners with Simon. And Jesus said unto Simon, Fear not; from henceforth thou shalt catch men. And when they had brought their ships to land, they forsook all, and followed him.","ref":"Luke 5:1-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why would Mark leave this whole scene out? Well, it was something that draws attention to Peter. Peter didn't want that (possibly because he is a bit embarrassed at the situation, but also because he wants Jesus to be the center, and he is getting that specific point across without bogging down the message with life circumstances). The thing that I find interesting is that the other gospel accounts give deeper clarity to personal lives, while Peter doesn't think it's all that important. I believe it's because he saw Christ's actions and continual ministry as the ultimate source of inspiration for him. The other writers see Jesus in different lights, but with all the same message of salvation."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":28,"title":"Controlling Demons","entries":[{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again we have that \"straightway\" word again. He gets these four disciples and goes over to a synagogue to start teaching. His preaching is so good that the people there were completely dumbfounded. He taught as if He was absolutely confident in His words. Others sweat when they teach."},{"kind":"para","text":"People have said that they like my teaching on more than one occasion. If I'm honest (and I try to be as transparent with my peers as much as possible), I am often terrified. I am worried about saying the wrong things or misinterpreting what is being read. I spend countless hours reading and digging in scripture so that it's not a wasted effort when we go to it in study, but it's still a worry for me that someone will ask me a question about something that I just didn't think of. Thankfully, the questions that people ask can be addressed through the simplicity of the gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus has perfect knowledge of all things. But He also knows that the answer is found in the work of Christ on the cross. These people didn't know that, but He knew what was going to come, and so it gave Him absolute authority and power behind His teaching to them. They could not deny what was being said because He is literally God's Word in the flesh."}]},{"start":23,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is always at least one in the midst with an unclean spirit. There's one who desires to destroy the testimony of the teacher. I'd like to say that it's simple to just go to the gospel and preach it if there are questions that come up, but there will be people who reject it with every fiber of their being, and will make a strong case against it (just talking over you and being mean). This man was a possessed man. He didn't like the message, and instead of just walking away, he starts shouting at Jesus (cries out) about how uncomfortable he is around Jesus' teaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's how you know your teaching is solid and biblical. If there are people who start to become a little less comfortable while they are sitting and listening to it. People that don't know the Lord will not be able to hear a message and feel good about themselves afterward. Not because Jesus was trying to convict people. Rather He was just telling the truth, and the truth makes people question their personal motives."}]},{"start":25,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus doesn't have to do much to put this spirit in its place. He just commands the spirit, and the problem is over with. All of these people witness it and are completely confused. They know this guy can teach with authority, but they didn't expect Him to control even the EVIL spirits with authority. They knew there was something different about Him, but they just couldn't seem to figure out what He was truly on about. They couldn't be convinced of their need for the Savior yet. They were just astounded at Jesus' miracles here."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, \"immediately\" something happened. Every time Jesus does something (according to Peter), something follows them immediately. Jesus was known everywhere because of this particular instance. This was the moment that people recognized the name of Jesus (according to Peter). There was a lot more happening behind the scenes leading up to this point, but we are getting down to the nitty-gritty in this book of the gospel. Honestly, I imagine that this is really how it would have felt to follow the Lord in the ministry. It probably wasn't this quick, but because of the amount of impact Jesus had, it seems like everything was just constantly going, and there was no real break for anyone."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"title":"Peter's Mother-In-Law","entries":[{"start":29,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Anon\" means “immediately.\" Maybe Mark thought, \"You know what... I think we have used the term ‘immediately' a little too much. Let me think of another way of saying it.” So anon is the word chosen. They tell Jesus \"immediately\" about Peter's mother-in-law and her fever. This is another reason we think this is Peter's account, because this is the only time this is mentioned about Peter's mother-in-law. Actually, we don't even know much about Peter's marriage other than this point here, and when Paul speaks about it later on."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Have we not power to lead about a sister, a wife, as well as other apostles, and as the brethren of the Lord, and Cephas?","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:5"}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So Mark goes right back to using the term “immediately.” Judging by the rest of this chapter, I'm sure there was more to this story than that. But Jesus seems to heal Peter's mother-in-law, and she was feeling so good that she started to minister to them. She likely just started preparing a meal and helping them to feel relaxed. What a glorious blessing this moment is here, though. It goes by so fast that it's easy to miss it."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":34,"title":"Multitudes Healed","entries":[{"start":32,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone from the city was coming to Him so that they could be free. That is what Jesus does for all of us, mind you. Whether you are possessed by a devil, or you are just bound to your sin, Christ can give you freedom if you come to Him. He has the authority to free you. Peter seems to find this facet of Christ's ministry the most interesting because he speaks primarily on this subject as we proceed."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":37,"title":"All Men Need Christ","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I love to pray. I love my Lord. I don't know if I have the ability to wake up before the sun to go and find a place to pray, however. Jesus took some time (the only time it seems He could possibly get) to spend with His Father. We should all be searching for such times. Maybe it doesn't have to be early in the morning, but it can certainly be late at night. Find a moment where you are able to get apart with the Lord and just sit with Him. Even if it's only for a few minutes."}]},{"start":36,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus cannot seem to catch a break. He was out by Himself to spend time with His Father, and now the disciples have found Him to bring Him back to the needs of the world. They were anticipating His miracles. They didn't really concern themselves with His time of worship; they just wanted His presence. We will notice that Jesus doesn't throw a fit about it. He doesn't get upset or tell them to hold on. Rather, He gets up and goes out to meet with the people that need Him. Who needed Him? All men."}]}]},{"start":38,"end":39,"title":"Jesus Preaches","entries":[{"start":38,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He was moving from town to town. He wanted to meet everyone right where they were. He didn't expect people to just come to Him; He took it upon Himself to go to them. That is a very important trait for a minister to have, mind you. I think there are a lot of poor ministers today who are waiting for the people to come to them. Hardly ever does that happen. Worse yet, we have plenty of people in our churches who are being neglected. The preachers are not going to the people to meet their needs, but waiting for the people to come to them. They must have missed this part of Jesus in the gospels..."}]}]},{"start":40,"end":45,"title":"A Leper Preaches","entries":[{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the Lord wills, He can do anything."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What an amazing verse. Jesus doesn't just heal this man. He openly says (out loud) \"I will…\" There's so much power in those two words from the Lord. It's not that He's just able to, it's that He is desirous to! He wants to make us clean if we are willing to come to Him for a cleansing. He has compassion on those who are searching for cleansing from the Lord. He loves those who are willing to humble themselves and admit how much they need Him. This man was sick and had no way of being healed except through the power of Jesus Christ. He knew that Jesus would use His power if He was willing, but the man could not force the Lord to do anything."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a good rule of thumb to have for all of us. Instead of demanding things from the Lord, we should be requesting things based on God's Will. We should be saying, \"If you will, Lord, you can do this or that.” If He is not willing, then we should be accepting of that as well. He is more willing if we are coming to Him out of a full leniency on His provision."}]},{"start":42,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He kept the law even in this cleansing matter. There was a way to deal with these situations, and even though Jesus had completely healed him, this man would be a testimony of Christ. He would be a testimony of Christ's power, but also of Christ's innocence. All of these people who are healed are not healed through the breaking of God's law. They were all examples of how Christ obeyed every single letter of God's law. Yet, it's through the law that the priests and religionists will try and condemn Jesus. They will misuse God's law even against their own conscience and knowledge. Jesus tells this man to keep this situation a secret. This is what the man does:"}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The man didn't stay quiet about it. I mean, can you blame him? If I were healed in such a miraculous manner, I would have a hard time keeping that to myself too. He was telling every person. Someone said, \"The Lord told him not to tell anybody, and he told everybody. He tells us to tell everybody, and we tell nobody.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"In all honesty, Jesus has healed us miraculously, and that should give us ample reason to tell the whole world. Yet we find reason to keep our mouths shut about it. Not this guy. He wanted them all to know. But it got to a point where Jesus had to start spending His time outside of the cities. That did not stop the people from going out of their way to find them. He still desired to meet with them, but it would require them to go a little out of the way to get there. They were so enamored by His miracles that they were willing to travel into the middle of nowhere just to speak to Him. Jesus didn't have to go anywhere or do anything because they are all starting to just simply come to Him, and He was willing to help and heal as much as possible. I imagine this was a lot of work for the Lord. He spent hours and days talking, meeting, teaching, and healing with people. There was not much time for anything else (not even rest)."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":44},{"start":45,"end":45}],"verses":{"1":"The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God;","2":"As it is written in the prophets, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee.","3":"The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.","4":"John did baptize in the wilderness, and preach the baptism of repentance for the remission of sins.","5":"And there went out unto him all the land of Judaea, and they of Jerusalem, and were all baptized of him in the river of Jordan, confessing their sins.","6":"And John was clothed with camel's hair, and with a girdle of a skin about his loins; and he did eat locusts and wild honey;","7":"And preached, saying, There cometh one mightier than I after me, the latchet of whose shoes I am not worthy to stoop down and unloose.","8":"I indeed have baptized you with water: but he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost.","9":"And it came to pass in those days, that Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee, and was baptized of John in Jordan.","10":"And straightway coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens opened, and the Spirit like a dove descending upon him:","11":"And there came a voice from heaven, saying, Thou art my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.","12":"And immediately the spirit driveth him into the wilderness.","13":"And he was there in the wilderness forty days, tempted of Satan; and was with the wild beasts; and the angels ministered unto him.","14":"Now after that John was put in prison, Jesus came into Galilee, preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God,","15":"And saying, The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand: repent ye, and believe the gospel.","16":"Now as he walked by the sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and Andrew his brother casting a net into the sea: for they were fishers.","17":"And Jesus said unto them, Come ye after me, and I will make you to become fishers of men.","18":"And straightway they forsook their nets, and followed him.","19":"And when he had gone a little farther thence, he saw James the son of Zebedee, and John his brother, who also were in the ship mending their nets.","20":"And straightway he called them: and they left their father Zebedee in the ship with the hired servants, and went after him.","21":"And they went into Capernaum; and straightway on the sabbath day he entered into the synagogue, and taught.","22":"And they were astonished at his doctrine: for he taught them as one that had authority, and not as the scribes.","23":"And there was in their synagogue a man with an unclean spirit; and he cried out,","24":"Saying, Let us alone; what have we to do with thee, thou Jesus of Nazareth? art thou come to destroy us? I know thee who thou art, the Holy One of God.","25":"And Jesus rebuked him, saying, Hold thy peace, and come out of him.","26":"And when the unclean spirit had torn him, and cried with a loud voice, he came out of him.","27":"And they were all amazed, insomuch that they questioned among themselves, saying, What thing is this? what new doctrine is this? for with authority commandeth he even the unclean spirits, and they do obey him.","28":"And immediately his fame spread abroad throughout all the region round about Galilee.","29":"And forthwith, when they were come out of the synagogue, they entered into the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John.","30":"But Simon's wife's mother lay sick of a fever, and anon they tell him of her.","31":"And he came and took her by the hand, and lifted her up; and immediately the fever left her, and she ministered unto them.","32":"And at even, when the sun did set, they brought unto him all that were diseased, and them that were possessed with devils.","33":"And all the city was gathered together at the door.","34":"And he healed many that were sick of divers diseases, and cast out many devils; and suffered not the devils to speak, because they knew him.","35":"And in the morning, rising up a great while before day, he went out, and departed into a solitary place, and there prayed.","36":"And Simon and they that were with him followed after him.","37":"And when they had found him, they said unto him, All men seek for thee.","38":"And he said unto them, Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also: for therefore came I forth.","39":"And he preached in their synagogues throughout all Galilee, and cast out devils.","40":"And there came a leper to him, beseeching him, and kneeling down to him, and saying unto him, If thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.","41":"And Jesus, moved with compassion, put forth his hand, and touched him, and saith unto him, I will; be thou clean.","42":"And as soon as he had spoken, immediately the leprosy departed from him, and he was cleansed.","43":"And he straitly charged him, and forthwith sent him away;","44":"And saith unto him, See thou say nothing to any man: but go thy way, shew thyself to the priest, and offer for thy cleansing those things which Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.","45":"But he went out, and began to publish it much, and to blaze abroad the matter, insomuch that Jesus could no more openly enter into the city, but was without in desert places: and they came to him from every quarter."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we got the introduction of the gospel message. We talked about Mark being the writer of this account, but Peter being the author of it. It seems like (at least to Peter) that everything in the ministry of Jesus Christ was fast paced. They went from one place to another, and helped one person after another, and it was becoming a lot for them to handle and deal with all at once. He spoke about a leper He helped heal, and told to keep quiet about it, and that man started telling everyone around him about the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that this healing message was widespread, and people from all around come to Jesus to see what He would do next. The problem is, they were not paying attention to the most important things about Christ, and instead wanted to see His miracles. We will take note of this throughout the rest of this account because it will be easy for us to forsake the best parts of scripture when all we see are the miracles Christ was performing."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we got the introduction of the gospel message. We talked about Mark being the writer of this account, but Peter being the author of it. It seems like (at least to Peter) that everything in the ministry of Jesus Christ was fast-paced. They went from one place to another, and helped one person after another, and it was becoming a lot for them to handle and deal with all at once. He spoke about a leper He helped heal, and told him to keep quiet about it, and that man started telling everyone around him about the Lord. In this chapter, we see that this healing message was widespread, and people from all around came to Jesus to see what He would do next. The problem is, they were not paying attention to the most important things about Christ, and instead wanted to see His miracles. We will take note of this throughout the rest of this account because it will be easy for us to forsake the best parts of scripture when all we see are the miracles Christ was performing."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"A Man With Palsy","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Jesus has made quite a name for Himself in a very short amount of time. In the other gospels, it seems like it takes much longer, but according to Peter, it seems everything escalated rather quickly. A few days later, He enters back into the city to be ambushed by a bunch of people. It seems like Capernaum was a favorite for Christ, because He keeps making His way back here."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So there are many people coming to Him for healings and physical matters, but what Jesus wanted to come to them for was for their spiritual matters. He starts to preach to them, but they were not there to hear His words; they were there to see His miracles. He desired to tell them the truth about eternity, not just heal people. That is why (I believe) He tells the Leper in our last chapter not to say anything. But the cat is out of the bag now, and so they are coming to Jesus because of the healing He had done before, and instead they are receiving words of wisdom (which the teachers of that day would not be happy to hear about)."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another example of people who hear about the miracle and want Jesus to heal rather than listen to His preaching. Except Jesus can use this opportunity to further prove the necessity of the gospel message. It will not just be about healing for this man, it will be about the message He was preaching. We might notice that it hasn't said what the message was that He was giving. All it says is that He \"preached the word unto them.\" Obviously He was preaching God's Word to these people to reveal the greatest miracle of healing out of all of it, and that was the miracle of having your sins forgiven. We know this to be a fact because of what He does with this man with palsy next."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, if I were this man who was sitting there with this problem, my first thought would not be, \"Oh Jesus, thank you so much for forgiving me.\" Rather, I would think to myself, \"That's great! But I still can't walk. What am I supposed to do about this?\" To be fair, they were interrupting Jesus' preaching, so He could likely say whatever He wanted to. There is nothing more annoying and suspicious about a man that stands up in the middle of a church service and interrupts it to ask a question that would be answered in a few minutes if they would just be patient and listen. I've actually seen this happen in a church before. They don't raise their hands, they don't wait for the service to be over. They have a question and they want it answered right away. So they stand up, ask their question, interrupt the preacher, and the preacher is forced to ignore them and continue the sermon. That person with the question gets mad and storms out thinking they are entitled to steal the spotlight away from the message of God's Word being spoken."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, these people knew that they had a friend who could not be healed any other way, so they were urgent in their matter. But it was still a bit of an overreach to force this kind of show for their friend who could be healed in a moment’s time rather than right in the middle of Christ giving the most important news of all. I digress though. I'll give them the benefit of the doubt and assume they had been trying to reach Him for a while, but the crowds were so much that they didn't have any other way but to just start tearing off the roof. They knew what Christ could do, and they believed He would do it if they could just get their friend close enough. Jesus doesn't heal Him right away though, He throws everyone for a loop and says, \"Your sins are forgiven.\" He even refers to him as a \"Son.\" All the religionists would know exactly what He was insinuating at this moment."}]},{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They weren't saying it loud enough for anyone to even hear them. They had talked amongst themselves about what Jesus had just proclaimed, but that was about the extent of that. They were missing the message of Jesus' preaching altogether. They completely neglected His greatest ministry, and because of it, they were willing to deny His acclamation of God in the flesh. He is God, mind you. He can forgive sin. This was not blasphemy, this was absolute truth."}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus says, \"If you don't want to believe that I can forgive sins, maybe you'll believe it when I tell this man to start walking again even though he was never able to walk before.\" He says, \"Which one would you guys consider to be easier?\" The people listening (including the religionists) would agree that telling the man that his sins are forgiven is far easier than getting this man to walk again. But the religionists thought of it as blasphemy that Jesus would even claim to be able to forgive sins. Jesus didn't even have to hear them say it; He could just tell what they were thinking by the way they carried themselves. He sees the heart of man."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I the LORD search the heart, I try the reins, even to give every man according to his ways, and according to the fruit of his doings.","ref":"Jeremiah 17:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"That should be another proof to them that He is God in the flesh. He could see into their hearts without them even saying anything. That should have brought them conviction, but they were fighting against the Spirit of the Lord every step of the way."}]},{"start":10,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What can you even say after something like this happens? Whether they believed that this man truly had palsy or not doesn't seem to matter. There were people who have seen this miracle today, and they were not about to question what they had just witnessed. I think it's important to point back to what Christ did this for, however. We can miss the message if we are not careful as well. They saw the miracles, and that was what kept them around. The preaching is what Christ wanted them to pay attention to."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is why I love the Gospel of John so much. It gives His sermons so clearly. Matthew does the same thing. Actually, that is all that the Gospel of Matthew focuses on. He makes sure to talk about the things that Jesus said rather than what Jesus did. Peter was more enamored by Jesus’ work and moving. The purpose of this miracle was to prove His most important work, and that was His salvation for their sins. That sad part is, everyone was amazed at this man walking again and seems to completely forget that He had just forgiven this man of his sins, which was a far bigger miracle."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"title":"The Call For Levi","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So He leaves, and He should have all their attention at this point. He doesn't go to the next place and ask for all their sick. He goes to the next place and continues His teaching. The sad part is, once again, these people are just hanging around and anticipating His miracle. They aren't really paying attention to His preaching. That is something we must realize as we read through this. The miracles were there to remind them of what was important in His teaching, and they were not paying attention to that."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is another reason I think it's Peter giving this account. In John's gospel, he is not shy about pointing out Peter's downfalls. We will notice through his (John's) gospel account that he makes mention of Jesus continuing to tell them about His death, and yet they are still surprised when it happens. Peter seems to be the only one that is downright rejectful of Jesus and His acclamation. He seems to be the only one that didn't pay attention to when Jesus talked about what would happen to Him. The other ones got it, but they didn't understand what it meant. Whereas Peter was so enamored by the work of the Lord that He failed to listen to the Word of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a major problem in our churches today, by the way. We get very much caught up in His Work that we neglect His most important consideration. Such a shame when we have all this great truth to read and accept."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Matthew didn't ask any questions or hesitate about following Jesus. I guarantee the rest of the disciples thought this was a strange thing for Jesus to do. This was a tax collector. They were literally robbing money from their own people. He was a Jew, and yet he was benefiting from taking the money from the other Jews (his brothers and family) to support himself by the rule of the Roman government. Matthew (in his gospel account) gives the same sort of story as well. They seem to match up with how willing he was to just leave his job and follow after the Lord. That is fascinating to me. I assume he had seen the miracles as well and could not question who Jesus was."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And as Jesus passed forth from thence, he saw a man, named Matthew, sitting at the receipt of custom: and he saith unto him, Follow me. And he arose, and followed him.","ref":"Matthew 9:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think Matthew was more enamored by what Jesus said, and it was the miracles that cemented in his desire to follow Him (but that's just my personal opinion)."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"title":"Publicans and Sinners","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting take. Matthew doesn't say it was HIS house that they were sitting at. He says:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, as Jesus sat at meat in the house, behold, many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his disciples.","ref":"Matthew 9:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"In whose house? Apparently in Matthew's house. We wouldn't know that unless Peter told us. He starts to follow Jesus and then says, \"Hey, you know what? Why don't we go to my house for a meal? We can get to know each other better and you can meet some of the people that I know!\" Jesus must think this is a great idea, and so they are seen hanging out with His disciples (which were few at this moment), and many \"publicans (that is Matthew's people) and sinners (that is worldly people (the non-religious folk)).\" It says \"there were many, and they followed him.\" They were all astounded by what Jesus was doing. I'm curious to know how many people showed up to Matthew’s house that day. I don't think there was much more than standing room after a while, but they were hoping to see more coming from Jesus."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it seems that Jesus had some religious nuts following Him as well. They come to Matthew’s house and instead of enjoying the presence of the Lord, they wonder how Jesus could sit with such disgraceful people. The religionists of the day thought themselves as something mighty. Honestly, the religious of our day think the same way. They think their good works and their holy robes make them the most important people in the room (even in the world) and really all it makes them is stubborn, proud, and hypocritical. They follow their own lusts and desires and reject the true calling of the Lord (which was to sit with publicans and sinners at this moment)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I'm thankful that Jesus doesn't just leave us hanging in our questions. Even this one (which seems a bit loaded) will be answered by the Lord. I don't know if these Pharisees meant any ill will in this question. Perhaps it was a genuine one (though not very likely). But if they were just confused about what Jesus was doing here (after His acclamation), they were just wondering if there was a method to Jesus' directives and ministry. His ministry should be obvious to everyone at this point, but it's not (and it never really will be until the first church takes off in the book of Acts)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice, though, that it was not Jesus that they directly asked. They knew He would have an answer to their question, but they go to the disciples and ask about it instead. They wanted to see if the disciples were following this man blindly or if they understood the purpose for what He was doing as well. I'm glad the disciples didn't answer, because I don't think they fully understood it either..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus interjects. He wants it to be understood that He came to call the lost into salvation. That has been His message from the beginning. It was His message when He dealt with the man with the palsy. It was what He displayed when He forgave that man of His sins first. They should understand that at this point, but they don't seem to understand it. Even after Jesus says this, they still don't get it. There will be some who understand it, but not many. That is just the truth of the gospel being shared. It's still true for us today. When they hear the truth, they try and add something to it, or just have no idea what it even means, so they just make up their own ideas about it."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":22,"title":"When to Fast?","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The disciples of John were the disciples of John the Baptist. He was teaching them things that they should learn about how to get closer to the Lord. He preached about Jesus too. These Pharisees don't seem to see who Jesus is still. They have heard the messages, but they just can't trust who He is (even though we have literally seen Him prove Himself and His own words over and over again). The question is legitimate though. Why did the Pharisees and John's disciples learn to fast, but Jesus isn't saying anything about it? Well (as we will learn), Jesus is the New covenant."},{"kind":"para","text":"The law is the Old covenant. The old covenant says do. The new covenant says done. As long as we have the Lord with us, we do not have to do a single thing to earn the Lord's favor. That has been the theme since the beginning. God has always been gearing His people for this specific covenant calling, but they all missed it because it became a tradition and a religion, rather than a desire and a relationship. They have missed the mark, which is why they need Jesus. They needed to spend time with the Lord. Their fasting was to give them that opportunity, and instead they only fasted as a means of religious practice, not relationship strengthening. John taught them the necessity to fast to grow closer to the Lord. Christ is not mentioning this necessity because they have the Lord right there with them."}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So is fasting necessary today? I believe it is. It has its place in our relationship with the Lord. It is not, however, a necessity to gain a relationship with God. Think of it like a sabbatical or a vacation with your spouse. It is a wise man's venture to take some time to be with your wife (especially if you have an anniversary coming up; this is a reminder to every man reading this today to check when your anniversary is coming and plan something today). If you don't take the time to spend with your wife, you will not strengthen your relationship with her. That's just blatant facts. You may still be married to her, but your relationship will not be strengthened unless you spend some time out together. That is a necessary way to build your marriage."},{"kind":"para","text":"The same is true with the Lord. A fast is taking a break from everything else to spend time with Him and grow your relationship with Him. We all need times like this in our lives. Sometimes they come up without us even realizing; other times, we need to plan for those times apart. Either way, don't ever forsake the opportunity to build your relationship with the Lord, because that is the most needful thing."},{"kind":"para","text":"At the moment, the disciples were physically spending time with God. They didn't need to fast to do it. There will be a time when Jesus will be gone, and that is when they will have to build their relationship with Him through a fast. Apparently, this is something that stuck out to Peter, and so he remembers it and has it written down."}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is further clarity of the new covenant versus the old. We don't need to attach our new life to the old one. It is not possible to do so. We must become completely new, altogether. Nothing old should cling to us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"You don't sew an old law to a new covenant. You don't pour a new spirit into your old lifestyle. That is not going to work. They don't agree with each other, they don't belong together, and they will not stick well. It has to be completely made new."},{"kind":"para","text":"I watched this one channel on YouTube about a guy that bought an old mansion in the middle of Texas. It was literally in the middle of nowhere, and his desire was to purchase it, fix it, and then resell it. Well, while they were demolishing some of the structure to fix it back up, they found more problems that caused them to break even more of the remaining structure. Basically, the more they tried to salvage what was there, the less they actually got to salvage. They had to nearly start from scratch rebuilding the whole house and couldn't save much of anything. They were completely in over their heads. Save what you like from your old life, but I guarantee it won't be salvageable in your new one. If you have Christ, you will find the old mildew structure you once thought was your life as nothing more than trash and worthless."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea doubtless, and I count all things but loss for the excellency of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord: for whom I have suffered the loss of all things, and do count them but dung, that I may win Christ,","ref":"Philippians 3:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"To have Christ is worth giving up everything that you thought was valuable to you. He gives us more value than anything else in this world."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":28,"title":"The Sabbath","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This obviously isn't corn. Before you get all \"KJV\" onlyist on me, there are some things that were just not translated quite accurately that need to be better addressed in modern English today. I don't know if you have ever had uncooked corn, but you would break your teeth trying to eat it. \"Corn\" means \"fields of grain.\" The Old English word didn't translate properly here. It was not as if they were going through the fields and just taking as much as possible. Rather, they were walking by, and they were hungry, so they grabbed a little grain to eat as a snack. That was all that was happening here. But that is not what the Pharisees think of it. Now, we are actually told in the law that they were allowed to literally eat from their neighbors’ crops, but they weren't allowed to store up the food."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When thou comest into thy neighbour's vineyard, then thou mayest eat grapes thy fill at thine own pleasure; but thou shalt not put any in thy vessel. When thou comest into the standing corn of thy neighbour, then thou mayest pluck the ears with thine hand; but thou shalt not move a sickle unto thy neighbour's standing corn.","ref":"Deuteronomy 23:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"They weren't storing it for later, they were eating it on the spot."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They apparently don't know the law. We just read it. If you were to compare the two instances, would you argue that they were breaking the law? I don't think I would. The Pharisees are just looking to try and pin something on Jesus and His disciples, and this was their way of doing it. They were bending the truth of God's Word to meet with their religious practices. This should sound quite familiar to us today. Except you can't argue with the Lord. We will see that every single person that argues with Jesus is put in their place rather quickly..."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting enough, Jesus doesn't go to the law to prove His point. He doesn't show that they were innocent. He could have easily quoted this particular law, but instead, he brings them to a story about David. According to the Pharisees’ version of the law, David would be just as guilty of breaking the law as the disciples are here. I believe Jesus doesn't mention the law specifically because He knows that they know they are just grasping at straws. They know they have nothing here. They are just being dramatic."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This would be even worse by their religious standards. David and his men ate holy bread. The disciples were eating grain that had nothing to do with the temple. These are very different situations. Actually, Jesus is painting a picture about David here that would put him into trouble. The priest would be in trouble too based on Jesus' story. They were both playing in sin for this cause. Yet, there were no consequences for either one of them for this action."},{"kind":"para","text":"The opposite is true, they were both relieved and strengthened. The only consequence that came was to the priest later on by Saul, but that was strictly Saul's fault. He did something evil to them in 1 Samuel, which could be read about in that book. Either way, it was not based on breaking the law that they were in danger. Rather, they were taking care of each other, and that was what the law was supposed to do by the way. It was supposed to bring God's children together for one common purpose (to follow the Lord, and live a life right by Him and by each other)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead, they took the law and made it selfish and cruel, trying to pin people against the Lord, and against living for Him. They are still doing it with Jesus and His disciples. The priest over David did the right thing, and they couldn't argue that. If the priest didn't do what he did, David would not have lived into his reign as king. The Israelites would have been a lost cause. God put everything and everyone into their proper place for that proper purpose."}]},{"start":27,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here is the truth that so many have a hard time with today. The Sabbath and the Lord's Day are quite different. Jesus makes mention of that right here. The Lord's Day is every day. The Sabbath day is one day. The Sabbath was made for YOU. It was there to give YOU some alleviation and rest. We have abused that, and we don't take that time to rest. You were not made for the Sabbath. You were made to work, but God gifts you with rest."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have spoken about this multiple times over throughout other books of scripture (and we will talk about it again in the future), but it's sufficient to repeat ourselves in important matters like this. People will tell you that you are worshipping the Lord on the wrong day. They want to limit your day of worship to a Saturday. Perhaps that is the day that God has gifted you with rest. That is not the day that God has gifted everyone with rest. Our Sabbath will be different depending on the purpose. The Lord's Day will always stay the same. The Lord’s day (in the New Covenant) is on Sunday. It is on the first day of the week, not the last. We are too prone to giving our Lord the last parts of everything. On the first day of the week is when we should be giving our God what is first. That makes the most sense."},{"kind":"para","text":"It also gives God the most glory. We should take that into consideration when it regards which day we should be worshipping the Lord on. This chapter ends on a bit of a cliffhanger. It's not a make it or break it ending, but it is one that has no end. The next chapter will actually pick up on the same day, and they go together in that sense. We will get a clearer story once we read the next chapter."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":28}],"verses":{"1":"And again he entered into Capernaum after some days; and it was noised that he was in the house.","2":"And straightway many were gathered together, insomuch that there was no room to receive them, no, not so much as about the door: and he preached the word unto them.","3":"And they come unto him, bringing one sick of the palsy, which was borne of four.","4":"And when they could not come nigh unto him for the press, they uncovered the roof where he was: and when they had broken it up, they let down the bed wherein the sick of the palsy lay.","5":"When Jesus saw their faith, he said unto the sick of the palsy, Son, thy sins be forgiven thee.","6":"But there was certain of the scribes sitting there, and reasoning in their hearts,","7":"Why doth this man thus speak blasphemies? who can forgive sins but God only?","8":"And immediately when Jesus perceived in his spirit that they so reasoned within themselves, he said unto them, Why reason ye these things in your hearts?","9":"Whether is it easier to say to the sick of the palsy, Thy sins be forgiven thee; or to say, Arise, and take up thy bed, and walk?","10":"But that ye may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins, (he saith to the sick of the palsy,)","11":"I say unto thee, Arise, and take up thy bed, and go thy way into thine house.","12":"And immediately he arose, took up the bed, and went forth before them all; insomuch that they were all amazed, and glorified God, saying, We never saw it on this fashion.","13":"And he went forth again by the sea side; and all the multitude resorted unto him, and he taught them.","14":"And as he passed by, he saw Levi the son of Alphaeus sitting at the receipt of custom, and said unto him, Follow me. And he arose and followed him.","15":"And it came to pass, that, as Jesus sat at meat in his house, many publicans and sinners sat also together with Jesus and his disciples: for there were many, and they followed him.","16":"And when the scribes and Pharisees saw him eat with publicans and sinners, they said unto his disciples, How is it that he eateth and drinketh with publicans and sinners?","17":"When Jesus heard it, he saith unto them, They that are whole have no need of the physician, but they that are sick: I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.","18":"And the disciples of John and of the Pharisees used to fast: and they come and say unto him, Why do the disciples of John and of the Pharisees fast, but thy disciples fast not?","19":"And Jesus said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them? as long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.","20":"But the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them, and then shall they fast in those days.","21":"No man also seweth a piece of new cloth on an old garment: else the new piece that filled it up taketh away from the old, and the rent is made worse.","22":"And no man putteth new wine into old bottles: else the new wine doth burst the bottles, and the wine is spilled, and the bottles will be marred: but new wine must be put into new bottles.","23":"And it came to pass, that he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day; and his disciples began, as they went, to pluck the ears of corn.","24":"And the Pharisees said unto him, Behold, why do they on the sabbath day that which is not lawful?","25":"And he said unto them, Have ye never read what David did, when he had need, and was an hungred, he, and they that were with him?","26":"How he went into the house of God in the days of Abiathar the high priest, and did eat the shewbread, which is not lawful to eat but for the priests, and gave also to them which were with him?","27":"And he said unto them, The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath:","28":"Therefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath."}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Jesus continues His ministry with preaching and healing, and has made a lot of enemies because of His continued love and directive to compassionately help people. The religionists of the day, however, did not like the idea of Jesus getting the glory for this. They liked their religion. Their religion made them money. Their religion kept people under their rule of thumb. Their religion kept people poor and required them to lean on them rather than on the Lord. Jesus was putting a stop to that by healing people who needed it. That made these poor people turn to Him rather than to their religious leaders who were leading them astray."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we continue where we left off on the Sabbath of our last chapter, and we see the religious continually try and destroy the reputation of Jesus and also devise a plan to get Jesus killed because of His continual love and support for the people who are poor and needy. It's pretty sick and twisted."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Jesus continues His ministry with preaching and healing, and has made a lot of enemies because of His continued love and directive to compassionately help people. The religionists of the day, however, did not like the idea of Jesus getting the glory for this. They liked their religion. Their religion made them money. Their religion kept people under their rule of thumb. Their religion kept people poor and required them to lean on them rather than on the Lord. Jesus was putting a stop to that by healing people who needed it. That made these poor people turn to Him rather than to their religious leaders who were leading them astray. In this chapter, we continue where we left off on the Sabbath of our last chapter, and we see the religious continually try and destroy the reputation of Jesus and also devise a plan to get Jesus killed because of His continual love and support for the people who are poor and needy. It's pretty sick and twisted."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"A Withered Heart","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As we have illustrated in our last chapter, the main emphasis that Jesus is placing on ministry is not the healing specifically. The emphasis is placed on the Word of God being preached. The promise of the Savior has been evidenced since the beginning of time, and the religious people of the day did not want to see it. They rejected the idea of this man being God and simply preaching a message about repentance and salvation. They wanted a war leader. They wanted someone who could free them of their physical ailments and persecution. They, instead, got Jesus who spoke to the multitudes about Him being the Christ and only healing people with physical issues."},{"kind":"para","text":"We just came off of our last chapter with the Pharisees trying to accuse the disciples of breaking the law of God by eating some grain as they passed by. We established how they were completely wrong about their assessment of the law, and they were trying to just find reasons to pin something on the disciples and on Jesus. Now Jesus is in THEIR domain. It was not supposed to be THEIRS but they are not willing to give it to the Lord. This is where their religious practices took place. It had nothing to do with helping people, serving people, or encouraging people in their walks with God. It had everything to do with looking religious, and taking money from the needy and hurting. They were basically running a criminal organization and calling it Godly."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is, sadly, how much of the modern church looks today. It is why most people have a sour taste about the church house. They have seen how it is all about money and trying to get as many people in the seats as possible, and so they shy away from church altogether thinking they are all the same. If I'm honest, most of them ARE the same. Nothing has changed from Jesus' ministry to today. The Pharisees are still alive and well in our church houses I'm sorry to say. That is just the truth. We must go to the source of ministry which was the primary example of Jesus Christ. He came to preach the word of God and bring people to Himself. May we be of the same mindset."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What an extremely sad picture being painted here. They didn't care about this man being withered. They didn't care that this man was dealing with a health problem. They just wanted to condemn Jesus for helping people out. They wanted the credit and glory for themselves. Instead of trying to help and serve this man, they were neglecting him, and then getting ready to pounce on Jesus if He did anything to love him. Again, this is not much different than our churches today..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus knows they are all watching. He knows they know who He is. He also knows that they are not using the law correctly, but they are using it religiously. If I may... That is the main problem of the church. We love God's law more than we love God Himself. We want to make ourselves FEEL good about keeping God's commandments, when all it does is reject God's goodness and grace for us. His law was not meant for us to hoard and bring us up to a level. Rather, the law was there to help us know how small and weak we are without the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore the law was our schoolmaster to bring us unto Christ, that we might be justified by faith. But after that faith is come, we are no longer under a schoolmaster.","ref":"Galatians 3:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"That was true for them in the Old Testament too. They just didn't want to live for the Lord. It was easier to live in sin and just dabble in religion. There are churches that have their confessional booths today. Those booths are literally ripe for you to live in sin and dabble in religion. They help you to continually live your life however you want because some man in a robe will tell you that he will get God to forgive you. That is not how it works, and Jesus will illustrate that in all FOUR of the gospel accounts. It will also be proven through every single one of Paul's letters, and PETER (himself) talks about how it is not by our works in his own letters to the churches. Though, I think Peter was under the impression that Godliness was works-based just like the Pharisees as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Actually, if Peter would have been serious about the Lord before Jesus came, he might have followed in the line of thinking as the Pharisees. I'm thankful that the Lord chose most of His disciples without any sort of religious affiliation (other than Simon the Zealot, but that could be talked about at a different time). Paul certainly followed the Pharisees’ thinking."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, an Hebrew of the Hebrews; as touching the law, a Pharisee;","ref":"Philippians 3:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"If Paul saw this particular thing taking place, he might have been just as upset. Jesus was doing something that they claimed goes against their law, specifically because it was done on the Sabbath day. It had nothing to do with the healing specifically, but everything to do with their religious practices. Though, I promise you, if Jesus had done this on any other day, they would have still found fault in it and would have been upset. They don't realize that He is Lord of the Sabbath as well. He can do whatever He wants with whatever day He wants because He is God."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They held their peace because the answer was obvious. They were waiting for Him to do something good so they could do something evil. That is the bottom line. Here's a very important message to consider in your faith walk (or perhaps trying to find your perfect church to join). Do the people in there try and stop you from doing Godly things to do their tradition or to a set of laws or beliefs? If so, then they are not followers of Christ. They might acclaim Christ as their Savior, but they are not true followers if they are serving their traditions more. Peter will recognize that later on in this book."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Howbeit in vain do they worship me, teaching for doctrines the commandments of men. For laying aside the commandment of God, ye hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things ye do. And he said unto them, Full well ye reject the commandment of God, that ye may keep your own tradition.","ref":"Mark 7:7-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"But I'm jumping ahead. They hold their peace here because they don't want to accept God's commands and love others. They want to love themselves."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of the evil looks he was getting and keeping him from having compassion on this man, it made him more bold to do what was right. Friend, that is how it should look in your faith. You will be looked down upon for nearly everything you desire to do for the Lord, but we should not allow that to sway us away from doing what we know to be absolutely right. Being sold out for the Lord will seem pretty lonely on most occasions; you can be sure of that. But it's always a safer and more rewarding bet."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have never regretted doing what the Lord has required of me, even though my own flesh and blood has rejected me for it. I would do it again in a heartbeat if the opportunity ever presented itself, and I wouldn't change a single thing if I'm following the Lord. I also want it to be understood that following the Lord is actually pretty straightforward. People have asked me, “Well, how do I know that it's the Lord that is directing me in this?\" Well, does it please Him? Is it good? Then it's the Lord. Satan will not have you do something that would please God, and he certainly doesn't want you to do good things that glorify the Lord. So you can make a safe bet that if it brings the Lord up to the top, it's the right thing to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"Can you explain to me how healing a withered hand on the Sabbath is a bad thing? If so, you are certainly not in tune with the Lord's Will and way. You will need to get that checked on appropriately before proceeding forward. These men would have to have a tender heart if they wanted to do their ministering appropriately, but we will see that their hearts are not tender. Their hearts are evil..."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These two acts of miracles are what will spiral into the ultimate death of Jesus Christ. Jesus did two very good things (both by the Lord's standards and the Earth’s standards), and the religious people hated Him for it. Worse yet, people still hate Him for His goodness today. They cannot read the accounts of Jesus and find anything worth pinning harsh punishment or judgement on Him for. They have tried, but they have all failed. It should be that the world would want a leader like this today, but they have followed suit with the Pharisees, trying to put an end to the goodness of God as quickly as possible. They like their personal religions. They make themselves feel good about their own evil deeds and self-righteousness. If they would turn to Jesus, they could be healed too. They don't think they need a healing, but they certainly do. Their hearts were more withered than this man's hand..."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":12,"title":"A Multitude Follows","entries":[{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The religious of the day might have hated Jesus for His healings, but the non-religious seem to be enamored by the Lord. People who had no thoughts prior to Christ were suddenly reconsidering what they might have known before. They probably grew up like me. They had a belief in a God, but they just didn't know any more about Him than that. Heck, I knew more about guardian angels than I did about my Savior, Jesus Christ. They knew more about the law of God than they did about the salvation of God. That is the religious of our day still. Jesus still had a multitude with Him, and they were all seeking to watch the Savior and His next move."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was having a bit of a hard time moving around. All this big, wide world of space, and people were still pushing against Him. There was no personal space or time spent to Himself. People need that. Even the Lord needed it. He needed some time to be with the Lord. He tells His disciples to prepare a ship so that way He has the ability to leave the multitude for a time and get to some others who have never heard His message yet."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Their goal was not to hear His Word. Their goal was to receive His healing. They were kind of abusing His power. That has been the theme with the children of God since the beginning of Scripture. God wants them to listen to His Word, and they want to abuse God's power. That's why it was such a problem for them to bring the Ark of the Covenant into battle the times they did. That was not how they were supposed to use God's presence."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's a crazy thought. The devils worshipped and recognized the Lord better than the religionists did. They couldn't even deny Christ. That should make us all very weary in our churches today. Does our worship of the Lord (Jesus Christ) look worse than the devils here that are mentioned? If so, we might be too religious. Are we looking for healing more than hearing? If so, we might be too faithless. Are we looking to simply follow the Lord without getting to know Him? If so, we might be too graceless. Are we looking for self-promotion, rather than serving others? If so, we might be too pretentious."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we want to get rid of all those titles of false belief and “isms,\" then we must reject everything we thought we knew and accept Christ alone. If our works and faith draw away from Jesus being the front and center, it is a bad belief that must be gotten rid of. Period. Otherwise, you cannot have Christ."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't want the demons to speak about their knowledge of Him because that would not give Him a better standing with the religious. If anything, it would have made it harder. He presumably made the demons mute and dumb. He was able to keep them quiet because they were not human beings. We might recognize that Jesus didn't keep humans from being dumb. He didn't stop humans from doing things or force humans to believe in Him. He just simply gave them the choice. The majority of the humans following Him just wanted to be healed of something. There were a few, however, that wanted to learn more from Him. There weren’t many, though."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":19,"title":"The Twelve Apostles","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we might get a bit of a dilemma here about my previous statement from the last verse. I just got finished saying He doesn't force human beings. Yet, we see He has called a certain and specific people to Him, and we are under the impression that they simply listened because He is controlling them. That is wrong. He knows exactly who will actually follow Him and who will not. He picks out twelve distinct people, and we will see who they are in a moment. We've already met a few, but we will get the full picture of the ones He specifically wants to be leaders with Him."}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He chooses twelve. That's an interesting number. There were twelve tribes of Israel. We get the blessings for the tribes in Deuteronomy. We see the land being split up for the tribes in Joshua. We are assured that the tribes are still prevalent during the time of Christ (though they are not widely spoken of)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew was a Levite. We know that to be true from our last chapter after he was called. It would seem like it was up in the air about where the other ones come from. We could all have our hunches, but the Lord knows exactly why He chose these twelve."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I find it interesting that Peter is always the one that is named first in every list that is given through the gospel accounts. I don't know if that is for a reason or not. It is highly unlikely that Peter was the first one to be called by the Lord. Perhaps that is the reason for this account given. It was Peter and Andrew that were called together, then John and James, but we don't get Andrew’s name until verse 18. I don't know the reason that Peter is always first."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"James and John receive the nickname \"The sons of thunder.\" No one is really sure why, but we presume that they were a bit of a loud bunch. They could have just been preachers with conviction, and it was evident in their thunderous voices that they were passionate about the Word of God. I don't really know, because we have a very different idea of John in 1, 2, 3 John and Revelation. It seems that he is extremely graceful and loving to the people he writes to. Maybe that's because he lost his thunder brother to a beheading? I don't really know."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are getting a list of names that Peter has not mentioned prior. We might assume these people just showed up randomly all of a sudden when Jesus was calling them apart. Obviously, there's more of a story than that. The other gospel accounts give a little more detail, but Peter wanted to address the twelve here so we are not confused about them later on. Especially the name of the last one..."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That seems to be quite abrupt. \"Oh, and there was Judas Iscariot who betrayed the Lord, but they went into a house.\" Hang on. If this is the first gospel message, shouldn't we know what that means first? Betrayed Him how? Well, that is what we are working towards in this gospel story. That is actually a really important part. That would be the final proof that Jesus Christ is God in the flesh, who takes away the sins of the world like John the Baptist described Him as. Judas was called, and he was called for a reason. They were all called for a reason."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"title":"Too Much Ministry","entries":[{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting verse. Every minister should take note. Again, we have gotten the idea that Peter saw the ministry of Christ as quick and hectic. It honestly was. It never seemed to stop in the three years He was ministering openly. It was so hectic (apparently) that His newly appointed Apostles thought it was too much for Him. They said, \"He is beside himself.\" They are saying, \"He's doing too much for the ministry. He can't even find time to eat.\" They were probably right, honestly. Jesus could handle that kind of pressure, but the disciples were having a hard time keeping up with it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice the transition of title here. They are no longer His twelve disciples, they were His twelve friends. These were people who were not just helping Him in His ministry work, they were people who were loving and caring for Him like good friends would do. Peter must have changed that title here after hearing Jesus say:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye are my friends, if ye do whatsoever I command you.","ref":"John 15:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"These twelve will be friends of the Lord. One will not be an apostle of the Lord, but he will still certainly be a friend (even if he makes a huge mistake later on). The jest of it is, Jesus' friends thought He was in over His head and needed some rest from all the hectic ministry that never seems to stop. That is true about ministry, honestly."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our church has gotten quite big lately. I never saw it coming. Because of it, our Pastor has a plate full, and there are a lot of things calling for his direction and attention. He cannot give his attention to everything, however, and so things start to go unnoticed. Jesus called these twelve to help Him alleviate the ministry needs. He did not do this so He can just let go of ministry altogether; He did this so that no one’s needs would be left behind. He wanted to make sure everyone's needs were met and the gospel would be expounded upon appropriately. That was His goal for calling these twelve apart."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":30,"title":"The Spirit of Satan","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is why He didn't want the demons saying anything about their acclamation of who He was before. They would have an excuse to say that it was Satan giving Jesus the power to do these things. \"Beelzebub\" means \"a name of Satan, the prince of evil spirits.\" They acclaim that the reason He is able to heal people and cast out demons was because He must be a demon Himself. That is an absurd belief, and Jesus will point out how silly that is in the next verse:"}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Parables\" means “comparing one thing to another.\" Instead of Jesus getting mad at them and trying to make them feel bad for the accusations (trying to prove Himself), He is giving them a story to help them understand who He is and how wrong they are. The question is a great one, by the way. How can Satan cast out Satan? I said it earlier: if it pleases God, it's the right thing to do. Satan won't do things that please the Lord. He will only do things that please himself. Satan won't desire to cast out Satan. He will only desire to gain more power and cast out the Savior."}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is evidenced in many facets. I've seen churches (specifically) divided against themselves. The problem isn't with the people on the outside attacking the church. The problem is with people on the inside attacking themselves. The church crumbles and falls apart when it's divided against itself. It's the same with your household. If you and your spouse are not getting along, the house will not stand very strong. It's a good little jingle to keep in your back pocket."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you are robbing a strong man, you better make sure he is not able to attack you first. He is called a \"strong man\" probably because he is strong. If Satan is going to rob himself, he will have to bind himself up. That would be good news for those that are desiring to resist the devil, but Satan isn't stupid enough to do that."}]},{"start":28,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a debated verse here. The worst sin a man can make is rejecting the Holy Ghost. If you are rejecting the salvation of the Lord, you cannot be forgiven of that sin. You will die in that sin and spend an eternity in Hell. Why is it unforgivable? Well, it's the only way you can be saved. You cannot have salvation without Christ and the Holy Ghost. You must receive His Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, the people Jesus is speaking to at this moment have no idea what receiving the \"Holy Ghost\" even means. The Holy Ghost had not come yet. He was in the person of Jesus Christ. He won't come until the day of Pentecost, which is found in the book of Acts. But they are all guilty, still, of rejecting the Lord. They are assuming He is Satan rather than believing His claim as the Christ and the Son of God. Again, the demons believe this better than the religionists."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The only Spirit that is clean in this entire story here is Jesus Christ's. His is the Holiest Spirit of them all. By rejecting the Lord, they are rejecting the Holiest Spirit they can obtain in that moment. They have the presence of the Holy Spirit, and yet they are not willing to recognize Him. I think if we measured spirits in this chapter, we would see that the spirits of everyone else were too evil to be considered godly, compared to Jesus' Spirit who healed, preached, and gave up all His own personal space and time to be with the needs of others."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":35,"title":"The Family of God","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's another debatable concept. According to some religions today (the Pharisees that they are), they claim that Jesus Christ didn't have any other brothers. He had other brothers, and they are standing here with His mom looking for Him. They want a personal conversation with Him, and He was nearly unreachable because of the amount of people who encircled Him. I'm sure James (the half-brother of Jesus) is looking down from Heaven and saying, \"How can they just outright reject the obvious proof of my existence?\" Mary is likely looking down too and saying, \"They have missed the whole message of Jesus. They have robbed Him of His glory by putting me in such a high place of reverence.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"There's a reason that Mary isn't mentioned much in the Gospel accounts. Whatever religions can do today to take away from Christ's finished work, I suppose. I mean, that is how they keep their doors open. They keep people coming back to do more for their faith so that it's never complete until the day they die. Even after that, they have family members trying to pray their loved ones into eternity..."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even the crowd knows that these are His family members. It is obviously not talking about His disciples. They were with Him. He had just made that clear. They were also called His \"friends\" now. He doesn't refer to them as His brothers (though they are when they are following Him). It's not until the church that we are truly brothers in the Lord. He will make mention of that right now:"}]},{"start":33,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are all blood-related in the Lord. His blood covers us and brings us into the family of God. The CHURCH (that is, born-again believers in the Lord) are His brothers and sisters and mother in the Lord. That is the point of the church. We are to be connected to the Lord in one family through His Spirit. If His Spirit is rejected, it's a great sin and eternal damnation. If it's accepted, you are His family."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":35}],"verses":{"1":"And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.","2":"And they watched him, whether he would heal him on the sabbath day; that they might accuse him.","3":"And he saith unto the man which had the withered hand, Stand forth.","4":"And he saith unto them, Is it lawful to do good on the sabbath days, or to do evil? to save life, or to kill? But they held their peace.","5":"And when he had looked round about on them with anger, being grieved for the hardness of their hearts, he saith unto the man, Stretch forth thine hand. And he stretched it out: and his hand was restored whole as the other.","6":"And the Pharisees went forth, and straightway took counsel with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him.","7":"But Jesus withdrew himself with his disciples to the sea: and a great multitude from Galilee followed him, and from Judaea,","8":"And from Jerusalem, and from Idumaea, and from beyond Jordan; and they about Tyre and Sidon, a great multitude, when they had heard what great things he did, came unto him.","9":"And he spake to his disciples, that a small ship should wait on him because of the multitude, lest they should throng him.","10":"For he had healed many; insomuch that they pressed upon him for to touch him, as many as had plagues.","11":"And unclean spirits, when they saw him, fell down before him, and cried, saying, Thou art the Son of God.","12":"And he straitly charged them that they should not make him known.","13":"And he goeth up into a mountain, and calleth unto him whom he would: and they came unto him.","14":"And he ordained twelve, that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach,","15":"And to have power to heal sicknesses, and to cast out devils:","16":"And Simon he surnamed Peter;","17":"And James the son of Zebedee, and John the brother of James; and he surnamed them Boanerges, which is, The sons of thunder:","18":"And Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus, and Simon the Canaanite,","19":"And Judas Iscariot, which also betrayed him: and they went into an house.","20":"And the multitude cometh together again, so that they could not so much as eat bread.","21":"And when his friends heard of it, they went out to lay hold on him: for they said, He is beside himself.","22":"And the scribes which came down from Jerusalem said, He hath Beelzebub, and by the prince of the devils casteth he out devils.","23":"And he called them unto him, and said unto them in parables, How can Satan cast out Satan?","24":"And if a kingdom be divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand.","25":"And if a house be divided against itself, that house cannot stand.","26":"And if Satan rise up against himself, and be divided, he cannot stand, but hath an end.","27":"No man can enter into a strong man's house, and spoil his goods, except he will first bind the strong man; and then he will spoil his house.","28":"Verily I say unto you, All sins shall be forgiven unto the sons of men, and blasphemies wherewith soever they shall blaspheme:","29":"But he that shall blaspheme against the Holy Ghost hath never forgiveness, but is in danger of eternal damnation.","30":"Because they said, He hath an unclean spirit.","31":"There came then his brethren and his mother, and, standing without, sent unto him, calling him.","32":"And the multitude sat about him, and they said unto him, Behold, thy mother and thy brethren without seek for thee.","33":"And he answered them, saying, Who is my mother, or my brethren?","34":"And he looked round about on them which sat about him, and said, Behold my mother and my brethren!","35":"For whosoever shall do the will of God, the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Jesus continued to preach and do His healings of specific people in specific areas for specific causes that would get the anti-Christ leaders attention. He did things to prove to the religionists that He was not there to encourage religion. Everything He has done has gone against the Pharisaical religious practices of His day. He heals a withered hand, gains a multitude of followers, labels out the twelve Apostles, gets bombarded with ministry (and pushes for healing and helping people), and illustrates the difference between the spirit of Satan vs. the family of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we finally get a bit of a deeper look at the things that Jesus was preaching about. We can (of course) get an even deeper look in the Book of Matthew, but this will be one of the few times that we get the idea of what He says in this book of Mark. He gives them a few parables, and then decides to cross the sea for more ministry."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Jesus continued to preach and do His healings of specific people in specific areas for specific causes that would get the anti-Christ leaders’ attention. He did things to prove to the religionists that He was not there to encourage religion. Everything He has done has gone against the Pharisaical religious practices of His day. He heals a withered hand, gains a multitude of followers, labels out the twelve Apostles, gets bombarded with ministry (and pushes for healing and helping people), and illustrates the difference between the spirit of Satan vs. the family of God. In this chapter, we finally get a bit of a deeper look at the things that Jesus was preaching about. We can (of course) get an even deeper look in the Book of Matthew, but this will be one of the few times that we get the idea of what He says in this book of Mark. He gives them a few parables, and then decides to cross the sea for more ministry."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"By The Sea","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Jesus liked to preach from a ship anytime the crowd was large, and He needed to amplify His voice. I don't know how useful that is to be honest. I feel like the water would make too much noise for the people to hear. The only assumption I can make was that He used the water to provide some sort of amplification. Or He used the ship in some way that gave His voice some carry."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the second time we have seen Him do this while He preaches. The first time being when He made the miracle happen with the multitude of fish and Peter, Andrew, James, and John all began to follow Him in the first chapter. Jesus taught a lot of lessons, but in this chapter He is going to use quite a few parables to illustrate some of the things that lead to His salvation. Again, that is the ultimate goal for Christ. He wants people to come to Him for salvation, not just for healing."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":9,"title":"Parable of the Sower","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was a master of parables. That was His doctrine. He used parables because people can understand stories better than information. That is something we should remember. People have complained before because I use too many stories when I teach scripture. The reason I do this is because it relates better to me and those that are listening. They may not understand a load of facts and Bible verses that you drop on them all at once, but they can certainly relate to a story."},{"kind":"para","text":"If someone (for instance) was telling me about how great quantum physics was, and they released a whole slew of information about it on me at once, I would have no idea what they were talking about, and would walk away from that conversation feeling dumber than when I entered. But if they gave a personal story (perhaps about why they love it so much, or how they started getting involved with it so deeply) the story would connect much better. I would understand their thought process and it might even give me a better ability to DESIRE to learn more about quantum physics."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what Jesus does with parables. He uses them to open up something deep about eternity, but it's something simple that is used here on Earth. This first one we are going to read is my favorite, and there is more depth to all of these parables in the book of Matthew."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus starts with some sort of seed that a \"sower\" went out to sow, so he can gather produce. We will find later on that the sower is the Christian. At this moment, the sower is Jesus Christ Himself. He is sowing seeds into the minds of people. His seed will fall \"by the way side\" and there will be fowls of the air that come and devour it up (the fowls being the world or even the religionists). The world will do whatever it takes to make sure you are not receiving the cultivating seed of the gospel if they have anything to say about it. They want you to trust THEM more, not the Lord. They want you to drown with THEM, not the factual written Word of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"It's funny, because they say they can be moral without a biblical framework. The Bible has written evidence of morality in God's people. They cannot be moral unless they follow the scriptures’ guidance. They will say morality has been easy for thousands of years before scripture, yet they can't give me a proof to back that off of. They want to give me \"proofs\" from the latest article, or from their own personal ideas of historical facts, but they can't give me journal records, or historical evidence that predates scripture. It's impossible."},{"kind":"para","text":"So what do they do next? Well, they try to devour the truth of God's Word from sinking into people's heads, because that is too much proof for them, and they hate that. Sadly, people will jump on the opportunity to confuse you when you have been blatantly told the truth about scripture."}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Some seeds fall onto a \"stony ground.\" It could be better translated to “hard-hearted ground.\" Maybe a \"prideful and arrogant heart.\" They don't look to actually believe the gospel. They might receive it at first, but they will run at the first sign of adversity. They are not willing to stand in the fight. They are not feeling particularly strong in their faith, and they don't fully believe in what they hear to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"I knew of a man and woman who were part of a Christian church for many years. They had gone to church for most of their lives, and they were faithful to what their church taught, and to the ministry they could be a part of. Some things were happening in leadership that led them to question their faith practices. They started getting involved less and less in the church, and one day a Jehovah's Witness knocked on their door. The woman answered, and was greeted very kindly. After a few minutes of false conversation, the woman finally said, \"I just can't do church anymore. I have not seen good things from church, and I feel like I'm always working to make sure I don't end up in Hell.\" and the Jehovah's Witness said, “Well, Hell isn't real. Scripture doesn't talk about Hell, it only talks about the grave. We will all die, but there is no Hell. Why would a loving God create a place like that for His own creation?\" The woman thought about this for a moment and said, \"Yeah! You're right! That doesn't make sense, does it? I never recognized that until now!\" She was tricked into rejecting the gospel message."},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason Jesus is saying the parables the way He is, is so that these people can understand His message before it's too late. There will be some who accept the seed, but then start to question some things in their faith and completely reject it because there was no actual root in the seriousness of their faith. Sadly, it's often the church that doesn't ground people. We are often to blame because we are not discipling and encouraging people like we should be. We leave them with a stony-hearted gospel presentation and see them flourish for a bit to suddenly vanish completely because we were not consistent in cultivating their heart for the gospel, rather than just the answer for the gospel. There are people who understand the gospel message well, but they reject it because they didn't have a graceful presentation of it."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Some people have the gospel message preached to them, and it disappears quicker than it can grow. They have too much of the world around them, and they can't do much of anything about it. They love the world too much. Why would they give up the world for Christ? Why give up their crutches for a Savior they don't know? They don't realize that this Savior can give them more than their vices and bandaids."},{"kind":"para","text":"This Savior can fill their hearts’ desires if they are willing to forsake the things that block them from Him. Perhaps the seed is there. Maybe it has been planted, but they just can't bear fruit. They are wayward Christians or closet Christians. Negative spirits in this world (that is, demonic spirits) have made it difficult for them to flourish and speak about their love of Christ without being harassed and hurt by others. Perhaps there are people in the church who have hurt them over their faith. That happens a lot more than anyone would like to admit..."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Word of God can fall into \"good ground.\" The Lord seems to be preparing some hearts for the reception of the gospel message. They, maybe, didn't know they were going to receive the Word of God with all readiness (some do, but not everyone), but God had been doing something in their lives to get them to a point where they were just ready to finally move forward into the truth of God's Word. I think we fall under the \"thirty\" mark in America. We have so many types and styles of preaching and religion in this country that a person can get very confused quite quickly. We have to do a lot of convincing from their own religion. I have no doubt that is what it was like those days too."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus had to deal with Pharisees who were so entrenched in their religious practices that they were more of the stony ground type or the wayside type. Others were of no religious affiliation other than to keep the Pharisees at bay and away from calling them a blasphemer and stoning them. Jesus, on the other hand, doesn't seem to care about if He is hurt for His message. He just says it like it is. You either accept the truth or you reject it (it's really your choice). These different types are all self-cultivated. They are all in line based on how prepared you have made yourself for the truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"Some people have altogether rejected the truth simply because they find the world’s \"wisdom\" easier to swallow. Christ's \"Wisdom\" makes them change who they are. The world's wisdom can mold into who YOU want to be. I'd rather have a standard and direction of a good, righteous man than to just try and form my own religious ideas on the matter. Atheists will say they are not religious yet preach a message to you about how there is no God. That is a religion. They are in the “fowls of the air” business. They like to go and devour the seed that was sown and has fallen by the wayside."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's an interesting verse after this parable. It's interesting because of what happens next. There's some context missing here, I think. I blame Peter, honestly. He was there for the previous messages, but it wasn't until these parables that Peter finally pays attention. The other messages are just a background thought to the miracles. The other messages, however, were probably pretty helpful to give context to what Jesus was speaking about here. He will give further clarity in a moment, but it seems that there were some that didn't have \"ears to hear.\" They heard the words, but they didn't hear the meaning."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":13,"title":"Presented By Heaven","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So there was a group of twelve, and a remnant around the twelve. I don't think it was the twelve (specifically) who are asking about the parable. I think the twelve also wondered what Jesus was talking about, though. There may have been one or two who assumed the message, but they were not sure. They had to understand why He gave this story. So they finally ask Him what He was even talking about."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They \"knew\" the mystery of the kingdom of God. I assume they knew it by the means of believing it, but they didn't know it by the means of understanding it, that was for sure. I suppose they understood that Jesus was their Savior, but they didn't understand HOW He was going to save them yet. The message will make much more sense after Christ dies, but for right now He is explaining how the gospel message works."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If they can get the understanding of this one parable, it will help them to understand every single parable after this. They must link it to the gospel message. The gospel message will be \"Jesus lived a perfect life, died a painful death (defeating sin and completing the law), and rose the third day (defeating death and the grave).\" That's the gospel message. They don't know that, but when they go to use their own parables and preach their own messages, they will be wise to understand these messages first. So Jesus will illustrate deeper about the importance of God's Word on the matter, and they can use this line of thinking for all future parables as well. They won't. But they COULD use this same method to understand everything else in the future."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":20,"title":"Sowing The Word","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Seed\" means \"Word.\" That is the definition that Jesus is giving."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Satan has many tactics to keep the Word from reaching your heart. He usually does it through selfish and prideful ambitions that this world is prone to fall for all the time. He uses hurts and pains from \"religious groups\" (perhaps in this instance from the Pharisees) to keep you from fully trusting in what God's Word says. Satan is good at keeping people from listening and accepting God's Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"We knew of one man (Lord bless his heart) who started coming to church about a year into me being there. He had some sort of autism and claimed a basic style of Tourette’s. The thing is, his Tourette’s were usually pretty dormant until church services started. He wouldn't curse, but he would randomly yell and stand up and walk around the church (in the main auditorium). We had tried to help him step outside when these things happened, but he was always persistent on staying inside the church building. People were mean to him because of his disorder. I tried to be as loving and as compassionate as possible, but he would not take that very well. Actually, he eventually claimed that he was \"in love\" with me. That's besides the point. Satan was using this gentleman to try and stop God's Word from being preached. He was a distraction every single time the gospel message was being presented. Satan uses the weakest of minds and the hardest of hearts to keep as many people as possible from receiving Christ and the Word of God. Sowers sow, Demons eat."}]},{"start":16,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Affliction tests genuine faith. If people run at the first sign of affliction, they are likely not true to their faith in the Lord. This does not mean that there are people who have left who were not saved. I think there are people who were genuinely saved and have fought a good fight in their church only to be backed into a space where they no longer have it in them to fight anymore. Those people were likely sown among the good ground thorns, which I will get into in a moment."},{"kind":"para","text":"Rather, this verse is speaking about fake Christians. They claim Christ, but they are nowhere to be found when there is a complication in the church or a tricky question that is asked to them. If they don't know the answer to something, they just simply roll over and give up. Their faith means literally nothing to them, and they would have no trouble leaving it in order to be at ease in their thoughts and mind. They seem nice and strong, but won't be trustworthy to anyone when real trial comes."}]},{"start":18,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The environment sometimes is inevitable, but how to use our surroundings for the Lord is what matters. Some people grow up in horrible situations and allow those \"past hurts\" to be an excuse for how they behave today. It's unfortunate when we allow this mindset to take over because we lose faith in what God has given to us. They allow the world to be stronger than God (not that the world is stronger), but it's all a personal choice. The real problem comes in when we allow the world to choke the Word of God. We might know it, we might be saved, but we are hiding under the sin of the world. We are not willing to be open about our Christianity, and so you just blend in with the rest of your surroundings."},{"kind":"para","text":"You are (perhaps) sown in good Godly ground, but the thorns of this world have choked the Word and have made it unfruitful. The roots are there, but the budding is not. Trials will come, and they might stay in the church, but they won't be a face of strength in adversity. They will likely be found hiding in the back somewhere, keeping quiet so they don't get noticed."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are some people who will receive the Word with open arms and a ready spirit. They will produce fruit from their reception, which will ultimately gain more fruit in the long run. It will help cultivate a \"Pay It Forward\" mentality. You've been given something good; now you can share that with others who will benefit from it. What I want to focus on is not so much the people receiving the Word. That is the focus, of course, but there's another aspect to this. I want to focus on the people who are GIVING the Word to others. Not only to hear the word (as most of the world has or will at one point in their life) but to receive it. To claim ownership of this property with good soil. His word is freely given to us. Fruit so good it has to be shared."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"O taste and see that the LORD is good: blessed is the man that trusteth in him.","ref":"Psalm 34:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"I'm not much of a farmer, but I can certainly respect the work they put in to produce good food for the people they are providing it for. Sometimes they are just out there for a job. Others are very much more caring with their work, and know that they are producing something that will ultimately be eaten by someone somewhere down the line. These people are aware that it will be sustenance for those that will eventually take hold of it and eat it. That is what the Word of God should be doing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, there are people who are giving God's Word only because they are commissioned to do it. They don't actually care about it reaching and producing valuable produce in the lives of those that they give it to. They are just concerned about ticking a box for their Christian walk. That is not going to be beneficial to the person giving the Word nor receiving it. Sowing seeds in callousness is dangerous for yourself more than to others. I think there is something spiritually wrong with someone who doesn't take careful consideration about what they are throwing out there. They could be sowing seeds of discord!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A false witness that speaketh lies, and he that soweth discord among brethren.","ref":"Proverbs 6:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you want to produce good fruit, you must sow with tears and compassion."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"They that sow in tears shall reap in joy. He that goeth forth and weepeth, bearing precious seed, shall doubtless come again with rejoicing, bringing his sheaves with him.","ref":"Psalm 126:5-6"}]}]},{"start":21,"end":25,"title":"A Candle Hidden","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A new parable to help illustrate the sowing parable. You don't light a candle to cover the light. What was the point of lighting the candle so you can see, and then putting it under something to keep it from being seen? That literally defeats the whole purpose of the candle. Rather, you want to light the candle to help illuminate the way. When you do this, it helps you see, and it helps you guide. The world is a dark place, and we need a lot more candles being lit by faithful Christians who hold the light of the Lord."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Word of God is not hidden. It is illuminated to those that desire to hear it. They must have the Lord in order to understand it, though. That is the purpose of the seed-sowing parable. If the seed is sown into good ground, they will understand the use of the parables and come to know Christ deeper."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He that is of God heareth God's words: ye therefore hear them not, because ye are not of God.","ref":"John 8:47"},{"kind":"para","text":"Eventually, the whole world will know the truth of God's Word, but not everyone will accept it. Once they know the Word of God, it will produce fruit that accounts for that. If not, then they have likely not understood the Word of God. The Word of God illuminates. When it's accepted, it doesn't produce the fruit of this Earth. It produces the fruit of light, which is the fruit of the Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law.","ref":"Galatians 5:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"If a Christian produces these fruits, it will not be by their own desire or doing. It will be through the Spirit of the Lord that it is evident in your life. YOU do not produce the fruit of light. God's Spirit does that. You cannot have a phony light shining either. You can only have true, genuine light. If anyone finds a light that is not genuine, it is likely not a light at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was trying to figure out an illustration of a fake light, I could not think of one. I asked my family and they gave me different ideas about what a fake light could be, but everything they gave me ideas about were about actual lights. \"The light section in Home Depot\" is just a display of lights, but they are genuine lights. They produce light. My daughter gave me the illustration of a drawing of light. That works perfectly. If you drew a lightbulb and then turned out the ceiling light, I don't think you are going to see too much using that picture. It's just a drawing of light, it is not the real thing. Only the Lord can produce genuine light. Everything else is just a cheap imitation drawing of what a real light looks like."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ brings this back up again. If you don't understand the first parable, it is likely that you won't understand this one, or the next one either."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a scary thought, and it should produce some boldness in us. If you understand these parables, you will have a greater responsibility to use these parables. Why? Well, because you have been given fruit, and you are then to give others fruit that they don't have. You are to give what you have been given. That is another rule of thumb in the Christian life. Life-gifted people should be life-giving people."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, Jesus is talking to people who are desiring to be healed from Him. These are not people here to just simply listen to Jesus' message. Peter obviously takes recognition of that, because this is one of the few rare times that Peter is actually paying attention to the message being presented. He has seen the miracle healings from a lot of people this far, and so have the people who are hearing this message. So there are people who are given the message, who now have the responsibility to GIVE the message to others."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then there are people who have not gotten the message who are now left with no healing for their sin, NOR with their ailments. It's not by their desire to be healed that Jesus is concerned about. It is by their FAITH to be healed, that Jesus wants to address. All of these people could be healed, without even going to Jesus. They just had to trust in who He is, and their FAITH will make them whole. They may not be healed from their diseases, or from their blindness, or pains physically, but they will be healed by their Spirit, and their sin Spiritually."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":29,"title":"The Kingdom of God","entries":[{"start":26,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have a lot of science to demonstrate the growth of a seed today. They could show you real video of it happening as well. But they still can't tell you HOW it works. There is no physical scientific process that can be analyzed and represented in the method. You might say, \"Well it's fertilized, that's how it works!\" Okay but where did the seed come from? \"Well it came from a tree.\" Okay where did the tree come from? \"It came from a seed...\" Okay but where did the first seed come from? \"...Atoms, of some sort, forming...\" There is no physical scientific explanation for this process. You have to go by faith somewhere. The Kingdom of God is all about faith building seeds being sown into good-maintained ground."}]},{"start":28,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's a process to the produce’s growth. If you reap too early, you will not have fruit that is ready to eat. If you reap too late, you will have inedible fruit. Fruit that will probably make you very sick and unhealthy. There is a maturation process that has to take place, and then you are able to harvest after the time has taken for it to grow properly. That is what the Kingdom of God looks like."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are all growing stalks of corn, mind you. If you are taken before you are fully grown in the faith, there is a good chance you will not be saved. It will be too early. You have not grounded yourself in truth or put your faith in Christ. You wouldn't understand it because you have not had the opportunity to do so."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are taken AFTER too much time has been given (and you were allowed the Word to settle in your brain), then you will not be saved either. You must be taken at the proper time (that time being in full understanding of Christ's sacrifice to you). That is the mature state. That is the proper time to be harvested. The Kingdom of God is looking for ripe Christians, not foolish young people or old grumpy people."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":32,"title":"Comparing God's Kingdom","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we think of God's kingdom, what do we think of? We might think of peace, unity, literally one mind, love. If we are trying to find a physical representation, what would we think of? Perhaps we would think of someone in authority (like a king or ruler). He has been talking about fruit and cultivating for quite some time, so perhaps He will use fruit as a proper illustration of God's kingdom? Let's read what He uses:"}]},{"start":31,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The only physical representation that best suited Jesus' comparison was... a mustard seed... That seems like a strange comparison. Mustard seeds are very small. When God's Word is planted, it might seem like a small thing to everyone else in the world. They will think it's not going to do much of anything because of how tiny they make it out to be. Little do they know it grows into something huge."},{"kind":"para","text":"The leaves of a mustard tree produce even more seeds that can grow into more trees. They produce a lot of little seeds on one leaf. That is a perfect illustration of God's Kingdom! He also uses the mustard seed as an illustration of our faith!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus said unto them, Because of your unbelief: for verily I say unto you, If ye have faith as a grain of mustard seed, ye shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.","ref":"Matthew 17:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Our faith (if we exercise it properly) will produce greater faith like a mustard seed will increase MORE seeds."}]}]},{"start":33,"end":34,"title":"Always in Parables","entries":[{"start":33,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I assume, after He said all His parables, the disciples continued to ask Him what they meant. They didn't have ears to hear, obviously. They were a little bit confused about how this applies to their lives. It won't really apply to them until after Christ has ascended, but they will all certainly remember the parables of Christ. He was preparing them for a future ministry, and it will be something that they will have to take seriously if they wanted to see fruit produced, light illuminated, and faith growing into strength, and glory for the Lord."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":41,"title":"In The Eye of the Storm","entries":[{"start":35,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He was heading back to the other side of the water after sending away the multitude, but it seems that there was a remnant of followers coming with Him. We assume He is in the ship with His disciples, and there were others who were followers with them. The twelve disciples stayed close to Jesus nearly every step of the way, and Jesus had chosen those twelve on purpose. They were likely the ones in the immediate ship with Him. It was a good thing that they were, otherwise this story might have ended a bit differently."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is likely that this crossing would have only taken them about an hour or two to get through on a normal day. It seems this day was not normal though, and there was a storm that came in. I assume they didn't know the storm was coming, or they knew it but didn't say anything to Jesus thinking they could probably outrun the storm."}]},{"start":37,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Think of that. If it only takes them about an hour to cross the Sea of Galilee, then Jesus fell asleep FAST. He is so comfortable in the presence and leading of the Lord that He can just fall asleep quickly and in a storm. This is not the last time they will be caught in the middle of a storm, by the way. I think it is important to note (as well) that this is in the same chapter that Christ just gave parables about the Kingdom. They should look at all of Christ's ministry as a parable now. Everything that Christ has done, and is about to do, is a parable of the Kingdom of God. That includes the process of this storm. His laying down during a storm is a parable. They will have to learn the lesson here. It was not that He didn't care if they perished (of course He cared, He has been preaching a message about His compassion for the lost for a while now), it was that THEY didn't care about their faith and trust in the Lord."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a parable too. This was literal, but it was a parable about the storms of life. That is the message that is preached in churches today, and it is a rightful message. If we have Christ in the middle of a storm, we have comfort through it. We can be confident that the Lord will get us through, or He will calm the storm. It might seem like it's never-ending. It might seem as if God is sleeping and doesn't care, but He certainly does, and He is paying attention even in His nap."},{"kind":"para","text":"His nap had a purpose, by the way. It was not because He was tired of dealing with the people's problems. It was because He is 100% man and needed some time for sleep. But it's also that He is 100% God that He is able to calm a storm!"}]},{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That's an odd question, isn't it? They were about to sink and be stuck in the middle of some heavy storm waters that would be nearly impossible to get out of. Christ had done some pretty great miracles before, but this was a new one. I don't think the question is asked here because He doesn't know why they were afraid. Rather, when Jesus asks questions, it is so they can question themselves. Why WERE they fearful? I could ask you today."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have the Lord, why do you feel justified to fear the storms in your life? Is it because you don't trust the Lord enough? Is it because you think that they are too difficult to handle? Why are you fearful? Why don't you have faith in the Lord? It's a genuine question. They were scared, and they didn't know what to do. Now they unlock a new fear. This situation brings a NEW type of fear:"}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is no ordinary manner of man! They should have understood that through all of the healings that He has done, plus the preaching He has been giving these people for however long it has been at this point. You'd think from all the other miracles alone that they would not be shocked by this situation. It seems that they either didn't understand, or they were just completely blind to it. They were fearful before about the storm, but now they are \"exceedingly\" fearful. He had just stopped a storm and it was quite an amazing thing to behold. At first they were afraid, they were petrified... Knowing they would never live without the Lord by their side… Now they are in awe of the Lord's power!"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it should be noted (by the way) that Jesus has the ability to stop any storm. He can stop the storms and trials in your life, and yet you don't see Him doing that very often. He lets the storm linger and grow, and you eventually are feeling defeated and withdrawn. He does it so we would come to Him. He could have stayed the storm before He took His nap this day. He could have kept the storm from ever coming in. However, He allowed the storm, and He showed Himself faithful to His people in the middle of it. He will do it again later on and the same problem will persist. They will get their eyes off of the power and leniency of Christ. That is our biggest problem still today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must come to Christ in the middle of the storm. We must hold onto Him and trust that He knows what is best. He will be there, but we just have to lay with Him and wait it out. Take a nap if you need to. It might seem like a joke but I'm serious. Sometimes it is good to take a nap in the middle of the storm."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":38},{"start":39,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":41}],"verses":{"1":"And he began again to teach by the sea side: and there was gathered unto him a great multitude, so that he entered into a ship, and sat in the sea; and the whole multitude was by the sea on the land.","2":"And he taught them many things by parables, and said unto them in his doctrine,","3":"Hearken; Behold, there went out a sower to sow:","4":"And it came to pass, as he sowed, some fell by the way side, and the fowls of the air came and devoured it up.","5":"And some fell on stony ground, where it had not much earth; and immediately it sprang up, because it had no depth of earth:","6":"But when the sun was up, it was scorched; and because it had no root, it withered away.","7":"And some fell among thorns, and the thorns grew up, and choked it, and it yielded no fruit.","8":"And other fell on good ground, and did yield fruit that sprang up and increased; and brought forth, some thirty, and some sixty, and some an hundred.","9":"And he said unto them, He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.","10":"And when he was alone, they that were about him with the twelve asked of him the parable.","11":"And he said unto them, Unto you it is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of God: but unto them that are without, all these things are done in parables:","12":"That seeing they may see, and not perceive; and hearing they may hear, and not understand; lest at any time they should be converted, and their sins should be forgiven them.","13":"And he said unto them, Know ye not this parable? and how then will ye know all parables?","14":"The sower soweth the word.","15":"And these are they by the way side, where the word is sown; but when they have heard, Satan cometh immediately, and taketh away the word that was sown in their hearts.","16":"And these are they likewise which are sown on stony ground; who, when they have heard the word, immediately receive it with gladness;","17":"And have no root in themselves, and so endure but for a time: afterward, when affliction or persecution ariseth for the word's sake, immediately they are offended.","18":"And these are they which are sown among thorns; such as hear the word,","19":"And the cares of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the lusts of other things entering in, choke the word, and it becometh unfruitful.","20":"And these are they which are sown on good ground; such as hear the word, and receive it, and bring forth fruit, some thirtyfold, some sixty, and some an hundred.","21":"And he said unto them, Is a candle brought to be put under a bushel, or under a bed? and not to be set on a candlestick?","22":"For there is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested; neither was any thing kept secret, but that it should come abroad.","23":"If any man have ears to hear, let him hear.","24":"And he said unto them, Take heed what ye hear: with what measure ye mete, it shall be measured to you: and unto you that hear shall more be given.","25":"For he that hath, to him shall be given: and he that hath not, from him shall be taken even that which he hath.","26":"And he said, So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground;","27":"And should sleep, and rise night and day, and the seed should spring and grow up, he knoweth not how.","28":"For the earth bringeth forth fruit of herself; first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear.","29":"But when the fruit is brought forth, immediately he putteth in the sickle, because the harvest is come.","30":"And he said, Whereunto shall we liken the kingdom of God? or with what comparison shall we compare it?","31":"It is like a grain of mustard seed, which, when it is sown in the earth, is less than all the seeds that be in the earth:","32":"But when it is sown, it groweth up, and becometh greater than all herbs, and shooteth out great branches; so that the fowls of the air may lodge under the shadow of it.","33":"And with many such parables spake he the word unto them, as they were able to hear it.","34":"But without a parable spake he not unto them: and when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples.","35":"And the same day, when the even was come, he saith unto them, Let us pass over unto the other side.","36":"And when they had sent away the multitude, they took him even as he was in the ship. And there were also with him other little ships.","37":"And there arose a great storm of wind, and the waves beat into the ship, so that it was now full.","38":"And he was in the hinder part of the ship, asleep on a pillow: and they awake him, and say unto him, Master, carest thou not that we perish?","39":"And he arose, and rebuked the wind, and said unto the sea, Peace, be still. And the wind ceased, and there was a great calm.","40":"And he said unto them, Why are ye so fearful? how is it that ye have no faith?","41":"And they feared exceedingly, and said one to another, What manner of man is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?"}},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Jesus was giving the people much needed teaching and preaching and He did it through the form of \"parables.\" That is stories that relate to the message He is trying to convey. He gave them very precise stories about who He was, and what He came to do and what we should be doing with the message He was giving us. He ended the chapter by proving His parables in stopping a storm and calming the sea."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get some pretty intense examples of miracles taking place with a man full of demons, a lady who hasn't stopped bleeding for twelve years, and a child who dies before Jesus can get to her. He proves His message and His salvation over and over again with a common thread between all of His miracles and healings that He performs and it is blatantly obvious in this chapter."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Jesus was giving the people much-needed teaching and preaching, and He did it through the form of \"parables.\" That is, stories that relate to the message He is trying to convey. He gave them very precise stories about who He was, what He came to do, and what we should be doing with the message He was giving us. He ended the chapter by proving His parables by stopping a storm and calming the sea. In this chapter, we get some pretty intense examples of miracles taking place with a man full of demons, a lady who hasn't stopped bleeding for twelve years, and a child who dies before Jesus can get to her. He proves His message and His salvation over and over again with a common thread between all of His miracles and healings that He performs, and it is blatantly obvious in this chapter."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"Legion Meets Christ","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This land belonged to the tribe of Gad. We have studied pretty extensively through the books of the law about how there were two tribes that chose to stay on the wrong side of the Jordan when the Israelites went to take the land. Gad was one of those tribes, and Jesus is now coming along to visit with them. He has not forgotten them. They are not living in a very prosperous land, by the way. They were not supposed to stay there. They were supposed to travel with the rest of the Israelites and inherit the promise. They helped the Israelites get in, but then went straight back to the land they had already accepted for themselves away from the rest of their ancestors. That, of course, was a problem, and it is still a problem when Jesus comes."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This man doesn't seem in his right mind. He literally lives in a graveyard, and it sounds like people have tried to tie him up because of how much of a lunatic he was. Perhaps that is why he lives by the tombs now. He was tied up several times, and they just could not keep him down. This demon was a strong one, even with literal chains holding him down. We will get more of his life story in a moment:"}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Nobody could tame him. He would either pick the locks or break the fetters. That is either really powerful or very smart. Demons are not stupid, by the way. They are spiritually stupid but intellectually pretty smart. Today, if you see someone who seems to be \"demon possessed,\" they are likely playing a part. It is not that they are demon-possessed, more than it is that they are mentally challenged. There are some people who are just very far gone from different ailments they have used to try and numb their pain (whether drugs or alcohol, it makes no difference). Demon possession is not an actual problem in their lives. There are (of course) demon-possessed people as well. They have an opportunity to come to the Lord and be saved, but they must be willing in order to be \"exorcized.\" In a moment, we will see how (on several accounts) that real exorcism works."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems he was only actually dangerous to himself. Demons are that way too. They usually don't attack other people, but they will attack the body they are indwelling. If you have a demon that is using someone to attack other people, it is likely that the actual PERSON is the one with that desire rather than the demon. The demon just feeds on that. The person that will receive the most damage is the person directly influenced by the demon. Contrary to popular horror movies today, they are not going to attack other people unless they are in direct danger from someone who has no power. But again, that is likely the actual person protecting himself rather than the demon protecting the person. The demon doesn't care about the person they are indwelling. They are just a vessel to use and keep from the Lord. But we will see when the Lord comes that this person actually has his own brain power in there when falling before the Lord in the next verse:"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was not the demon that worshiped Jesus, by the way. That is not what demons will do. It seems when Jesus shows up, the demons don't show themselves and hide away. So when Jesus comes to this man, the man is fully sober all of a sudden, which is why I think he is able to come and worship Him. He is assured (because of the demon's hiding) that this was not just some sort of regular man. This must have been a man of God, and he needed all the help he could get."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that this man knows Jesus. Perhaps he has heard of the fame of Christ from across the pond. Of course, at this time, Jesus had made quite a name that people would know because of the healing He was doing. It is evident (in a moment) that Jesus came across the water just for this one particular guy. He needed to change this guy’s life. I think this demon-possessed man was hoping that Jesus would come his way, but he was not certain he would. He would pray when the demon's were not controlling him, and other times he would just sit and soak in the land of the dead."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the life of most people on this Earth. We are praying for the Savior to visit us, but we never actually believe that He will. This man could have just believed in Christ from a distance, but he was not willing to acclaim Him until he sees Him with his own eyes."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus doesn't do much. He just comes up to him and says, \"Hey demon, come out of that man.\" And the man says, \"What have I to do with thee? Please don't torment me.\" Again, the spirit is not speaking for the man at this moment. It's just Jesus and the man here."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We see the demon and the man have agreed on their character now. He calls himself \"Legion.\" \"Legion\" means \"a body of soldiers whose number differed at different times.\" This means this man could have twelve personalities, or two personalities. Just depended upon the demon at the time. At this time, he had one personality, and it was just simply accepting the name and order of the spirits that were living in him."},{"kind":"para","text":"It makes you wonder how he got himself into this position. I do not imagine that he was just born demon-possessed. I suppose it's possible that his parents brought him up in this way, but I highly doubt it. He must have done something somewhere to accept such a terrible condition, and he was not sure what to do when it took over except to accept it."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't really know why they wouldn't want to be sent out of the country, other than they were comfortable here. Do demon's like familiarity? Perhaps it has something to do with the fact that they are in a non-promised land that makes them comfortable. I don't really know. It is likely that the demon meant that they didn't want to go to Hell. That is what we get from the other references in the gospels. Matthew's account says that they don't want to be \"tormented\" before they are supposed to."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, behold, they cried out, saying, What have we to do with thee, Jesus, thou Son of God? art thou come hither to torment us before the time?","ref":"Matthew 8:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then in Luke it directly relates to Hell."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they besought him that he would not command them to go out into the deep.","ref":"Luke 8:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"So it seems, these demons are not ready to go to Hell yet. We will see that Jesus (for some reason or another) accepts their request, and that is the part that many people start to question and pull other verses out of context. So are there demons in Hell at this present time? Yes. We are guaranteed that there are specific kinds of demons already there. Some are reserved for torment during the end times."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if God spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment;","ref":"2 Peter 2:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a controversial statement that (I have no doubt) will get people talking against me, but I'm throwing it out there with as much love and care as possible (because it is quite vital as we read along through scripture). I do not believe that God has cast any demons into Hell other than those that are reserved for this particular scene at the end of the world. I think these demons are specifically made for this specific cause and they are just stewing in Hell excited to leave and torment people physically (not just spiritually). The demons that are here on Earth are here to torment people mentally and spiritually (which ultimately (obviously) causes these people to harm themselves physically). The only other way to determine otherwise is to twist scripture out of context like 1 Peter:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For Christ also hath once suffered for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but quickened by the Spirit: By which also he went and preached unto the spirits in prison; Which sometime were disobedient, when once the longsuffering of God waited in the days of Noah, while the ark was a preparing, wherein few, that is, eight souls were saved by water.","ref":"1 Peter 3:18-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse gives a timeframe and an occasion. It speaks about using the Spirit of God to preach to the \"spirits in prison\" during the time of Noah. That same Spirit is the one that made Jesus alive. I will speak more about this in 1 Peter, but I want to make it clear that Jesus did not ascend into Hell to try and throw dirt in the wounds of those that rejected Him. There would be no point in doing that to those that are suffering in Hell, and there would certainly be no point in preaching that message to demons who cannot accept that message anyway. It would defeat the purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"But I don't believe there are demons in Hell yet (other than those that are reserved for the end times). So these demons won't be there either, and I believe they are still vagabonds walking along on this Earth. I think there are a lot of demons walking today. I think there are a lot of demon-possessed men who are tormented and just need the Savior. I hope we are people that can bring them to Him."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":17,"title":"The People Pray","entries":[{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now it's the demons that are speaking to Christ. It's interesting that they would rather be sent into pigs than go to Hell. They are not ready for that torment. They know what befalls them. You see, demons cannot be saved. We are saved through FAITH alone. Demons have already seen the face of God, and have already been in Heaven, so there is no re-entry for them. They are forced to live on Earth, and for some reason they want to live like men rather than like eternal beings."},{"kind":"para","text":"But they know Hell. They know what it's like to be there, and they would rather suffer death by pig drowning than to be cast into Hell for this time. It seems that Demons have to possess something. I think Demons (most of the time) possess little dogs. Every time I've been out soulwinning, I come across someone who asks further about our church or about our faith and I go to explain Jesus Christ. I do not dare to try and \"sway\" them into our church. I'd rather them know the Lord than step a single foot in our church corridors. Of course I would love for them to visit us, but the needful thing for them is the salvation of Christ. But it never fails that when I am presenting the gospel, and a little dog comes to the screen door of the house barking and yelling as loud as possible to make sure the message is not heard or received. We are then focused on the noise and complaints of this tiny vicious pup who thinks he can tear me apart. It's usually the smaller dogs (by the way) that are far greater used. They are usually much louder and more annoying. They are the Demon's favorite vessel."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this instance, Demon's were desiring to go to the vessels of pigs. They needed a place to go. It seems that Demons NEED to possess something. I don't always know if it has to be something living though. One time I was witnessing to a young lady that had been thinking about the Lord and salvation. She was not sure if she fully wanted to commit because there were things in this world that she actually enjoyed and she thought she would have to give up her entire lifestyle in order to accept Christ. I told her that her mind would change if she accepted Christ. There will be things she just will no longer accept. You do not have to give everything up to take Christ but you will when you accept Him. She said, \"I just don't know if I can truly believe that there is a God in Heaven that loves me enough to accept me the way I am.\" When she said that, there was a toy at the opposite end of the living room that started making some loud noises. It eventually turned off after a moment. I said, \"The Lord loves you and wants you to turn to Him, that is all. There is no ultimatum to that love, you just have to accept it.\" The toy started up again. \"Well how can there be proof, is there some sort of proof that there are spirits and the afterlife?\" the toy stopped. \"Yes, there is always proof around us...\" the toy started back up again, \"...we are just usually so invested in our own love for this world that we ignore the proof. It can even come through a toy that decides to be really annoying during one of the most important conversations you may ever have.\" The toy stopped. We sat for a moment and she got up going towards the toy, \"I don't know why this toy is doing this, it's not motion activated, and it's really starting to scare me.\" I don't think she kept that toy after that day to be honest."},{"kind":"para","text":"Demons will use anything and everything to keep people from Christ. It seems the Demons did not win this day, and they know they can't win so they ask for some more time here to torment people. Jesus gives them the time in the next verse."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Okay, so let's get the scope of this verse here. TWO THOUSAND pigs were drowned this day. That is either a lot of really dumb pigs, or a lot of very scared Demons. If I were the owner of those pigs, I would be very concerned about what had just taken place. In fact, we know they are concerned because we see them run in fear to tell other people about what they had just seen."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They all came to see Jesus the pig killer. They don't know that Jesus was actually freeing a man this day. They will know now."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were not afraid because of all the pigs that died this day. They were afraid because of this man who was a well-known demon-possessed man (who has obviously been locked up by these people multiple times) now sitting and \"in his right mind.\" No man can \"tame him,\" remember? They see Jesus doing something nobody else has ever been able to do with this man, and they don't know how to feel about it."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The pig farmers were able to do the math. \"At first, we were feeding the pigs, and then Jesus came and the possessed man begged him, and now all the pigs are dead and this man is normal. There must be some sort of connection!\" I imagine them saying to the city officials. There was an obvious connection. Let us note the way in which the Lord exorcises these demons. He does not do it through beating, or rituals, or strange superficial means. He simply leads the man to Himself, and tells the demons to leave. That is the theme of His ability to rid demons. That is the theme and example of what the disciples will end up being taught as well. Honestly, it is quite simple. The disciples will only run into one particular person that is not quite as simple to exorcise, but that was spoken about in Matthew."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then came the disciples to Jesus apart, and said, Why could not we cast him out?","ref":"Matthew 17:19"}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of being astounded and asking questions, it seems they were so scared that they just begged Jesus to leave. They didn't want to see any more. They didn't want to find out how they could prosper from Him. That kind of power was too scary for them. This goes to show how undone and terrible this man had been for so long, and they are completely terrified of Christ. I think they would have enjoyed this man being possessed more than him being in his right mind. They liked it better this way because they felt like their religious rights were the way to do things, and now this suddenly proves that their religious viewpoints were completely false because they don't work."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"title":"A Changed Mission","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Jesus obliges their desire for Him to leave. He turns and gets right back into his boat. Before he has the chance to leave, though, this man wants to follow Him. He doesn't know what to do. He knows that his own hometown will not accept him, and that he will be shunned by everyone, and Jesus has just changed his life. How can he go back to living amongst the dead when he is now alive? Jesus has spent a good portion of time asking people to \"follow Him,\" and this person is now asking if he can follow Him, and this is what Jesus says:"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Does this man have any friends left? Perhaps not yet. He will surely have friends now that he is saved. Jesus wants him to be a testimony here, seeing as Jesus cannot be. They told Jesus to leave, but did not say anything about this Legion fellow. He gets to go back and show the proof of a changed life. He was going to be a witness to his own community. Jesus might have been shunned by this society, but Jesus will still be preached whether they like it or not. And what better man to be a preacher than the one that they all deemed as a lost cause? This man will surely have more reach than Christ ever could because of the obvious nature of his changed life."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I want us to recognize a couple of things here that I think are very important to your preaching tactic. There are several people in whom I've met who say, \"Jesus never calls Himself God.\" Yet I can give several accounts of that. This is one of them. He tells the man to go back and preach about the good things that the LORD has done for him, and he turns around and preaches about the good things that JESUS has done for him. The man connected the Lord and Jesus together."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was evident that Jesus was referring to Himself, and He certainly doesn't reject the connection made by this man. We know this is accurate not because Jesus was there to see this man do it, but it seems the disciples made a trip back there at some point and got the testimony account of how this man's ministry was going. Jesus had made such an obvious change in this guy's life, and it made \"ALL men\" marvel. There was not a single person who was not affected by this man's testimony and preaching. I would love to hear this guy's gospel presentation. I imagine it's better than what you would hear most of these days behind the pulpit."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we have a lot of unchanged and calloused preachers trying to give sermons today. They are not affected at all by their own message. I don't think this man deviated much from his own testimony when he preached, and that was astounding people every time he preached it. The sermon doesn't have to be complicated, it just has to be amazingly true. It is amazing that Jesus would come to save such a seemingly insignificant man in society, and then turn around and leave. \"Decapolis\" means \"Ten cities.\" He was preaching to a vast land here. There were plenty of people who were marveling at this changed life. We get to marvel at it today as well."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":24,"title":"A Multitude of Difficulty","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Back to where He was again. Everyone was ready to meet up with Jesus again as soon as He came back to them. They were excited to see what He would do next. It seems He only went to the other side of the sea just to save this one poor soul, and start a whole preaching campaign through Him. What an amazing thought. He was not pressed on by crowds over there. He got a little bit of a break, honestly. Now it's back to the grind."},{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, His mission is to preach the gospel to everyone. He wants people to be saved, not just healed. The people around Him just wanted to see the miracles, but He was not here to try and impress them with miracles. He is BEGGING that they pay attention to His true mission, and was trying to use the healings as proof of His message. We will see that again in a moment."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, a ruler of the synagogue probably wouldn't be caught dead around Jesus at this particular juncture in time. There were people who were looking to try and pin something on Jesus for even being a light in their society. They wanted to try and make Jesus out to be a false prophet any chance they got. But this man was so worried about his little girl. He was pleading with Jesus to come save her. He needed a miracle, and he was willing to lose his entire reputation to get it from Jesus. If Jesus is who He says He is, then He would have no problem healing this little girl. The problem at this point was the number of people;"}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is trying to get to this girl and help her, but there were far too many people to get through. They were making it quite difficult because they all wanted to see Jesus' next miracle. If He was truly going to try and save a little girl and heal her, they wanted to see that too. This all has a point and purpose, as we will see in a moment. We are focused on the dire necessity to see a little girl healed, and yet we are stopped for a moment in this next portion before He has the chance to get there."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":34,"title":"A Woman With Blood","entries":[{"start":25,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So let's evaluate for a moment. We are assured that people were touching Jesus left and right (we will dive deeper into that in a moment), and yet this woman reaches over and touches Jesus' garment. Just a piece of His clothing. She doesn't even touch Jesus Himself, just a corner of cloth. She had been dealing with some sort of bleeding for TWELVE years, and had seen all sorts of doctors and healers and nothing worked to rid her of her health concern. She had literally spent all of her money to be healed and nothing else was working. Jesus is coming into town, and she thinks, \"He's the healer! He's done it to others, why can't He heal me too? I'm sure if I just touch Him it will be all over! I just have to get close enough!\" And so she does, and this is the result:"}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was an immediate relief. She was healed. What healed her though? That is the real question. It seems that every other occurrence that healings took place, Jesus was directly involved with it. He had to purposely and intentionally be directly healing someone for it to actually happen. Yet, in this instance, we see Him heal someone without a direct involvement in it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is going to open up how everyone else is healed before and after this. It is not by Jesus touching the people, or intentionally healing the people. It is through the same means as the demon-possessed man. It will be the same for the little girl that He is on His way to heal as well."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Virtue\" means “strength and power.\" It seems when He does a miracle of healing, it takes some of His own strength to do it. It was not as if He was losing power whenever He did it; it was that He felt the power being used when it took place. The question is funny though because we know that EVERYONE was touching His clothes. That is what His astute disciples point out as well in the next verse:"}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is another reason we believe it is Peter giving account here because in Luke we get a little bit of a deeper scope into what actually happened here."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus said, Who touched me? When all denied, Peter and they that were with him said, Master, the multitude throng thee and press thee, and sayest thou, Who touched me?","ref":"Luke 8:45"},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of Peter admitting it was him that said it, he just says, \"All of us wondered this same question.\" Perhaps they all did, but it was only Peter that asked this smart-aleck question. This would honestly be my question too if Jesus asked this. I'd say, \"Who should we talk about first?\" There was a whole multitude of people to blame for touching Jesus. But there was only one person seeking healing. The rest were just waiting for Him to do a miracle, but there's an obvious reason they do not receive healing, or any power from the Lord. This lady will prove how you are supposed to come to Jesus."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He knew there was ONE specific person that He had to place His attention on. He knows that there was a woman who needed the healing. I assume He knew the woman as well. He likely knew her name and everything, but He is wanting to point out something significant that everyone else is ignoring because they just see Jesus as a miracle worker, rather than for what He ultimately came for."}]},{"start":33,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was not just His power that healed her. It was her faith that was able to get power from Him. It is the truth of every single healing that has taken place so far (even the possessed man at the beginning of this chapter). FAITH made her whole. She was healed because she came to Him in faith, not because she was just trying something out. She had no other choice but to trust that Jesus was a genuine healer. She HAD to believe that He was truly the Son of God. Jesus wanted to point that out right here in front of everyone, including this Jairus fellow (who was probably very concerned about his own daughter at this moment and needed Jesus to hurry along). Jesus seems to be taking a bit of time to get to this little girl on the brink of death. Jairus is just hoping that it's not too late, but he is seeing that FAITH is what saves a person, not just presence. They are ALL seeing that it is faith that saves, yet the majority of this crowd will seem to forget and forsake that."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":43,"title":"Jairus' Daughter","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was too late for Jairus. I am curious to know how Jairus felt at this moment. I assume there was some sort of fear that came across his face because of what Jesus says next to him:"}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus reminds him of faith. Jesus can heal. He has that power. He has exerted that power several times over, and that is why Jairus came to Him to begin with. He was worried at the prospect that his daughter was dead, and Jesus assures him that faith is the salvation. That is the reminder to you too today. Faith is the salvation. His grace is a proven gift of your faith."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His three inner circle. This is another proof that we believe it was Peter that is giving this account. There were only three people that saw this, and John makes it clear about who is writing his gospel account. James didn't write an account, so it has to leave Peter as the only other source of this information."}]},{"start":38,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These people didn't really care about this child. They cared about being recognized by this synagogue ruler. They just wanted recognition. They were getting it in all the wrong ways. So Jesus tests their faith and dedication, and this is their reaction:"}]},{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They LAUGHED at Him. They suddenly turned their mourning to laughing at something that wasn't even funny. If I truly thought someone I supposedly cared about was dead, nothing would make me laugh. Not even a very funny joke. I would be too genuinely distraught that I wouldn't find humor or joy in anything. These people had no trouble turning to laughter at Jesus. They didn't laugh because of His acclamation, they were laughing because of His presence. They thought He was ridiculous and giving Jairus and this family false hope. So He just scoots these people out and takes a moment with just this family and the three disciples that were allowed to go with Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we are assured that this little girl is not sleeping. That was what they were laughing at. They all knew she was dead. They thought Jesus was playing some sort of silly game with this family, and they liked the entertainment. So they are shooed out because of it. They will not get to see the miracle that is about to be performed because of their lack of faith."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Talitha\" means \"damsel.\" \"Cumi\" means \"arise.\" Those are the literal words He is saying here. He is literally saying it out loud. It was not a secret held from them in any way. His power was out in the open. He simply had to speak it. He could have spoken it from the street if He wanted to. It didn't matter where He was. He, however, wants to build the faith of people who are a bit skeptical."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have no doubt that Jairus was feeling a bit skeptical. He has been told by his own superiors and friends who are in the temple that this Jesus is someone to watch out for. He was told not to be around this man. Yet, here he is, doing something that is unthinkable to the rest of the spiritual leaders. They did not want to give Jesus the time of day, but Jairus was far too concerned about his little girl. He loved her too much to reject the Savior."},{"kind":"para","text":"It baffles me that there are parents that claim to love their children yet try and turn them away from salvation in the Lord. They claim to love their kids, and don't encourage them to accept Christ. They are given an open opportunity for it, and they reject it and then tell their kids that religion is all bad and made up and out for profit. They are right in a sense. Religion is mostly out for profit. That is what these religious leaders were after. If they could not profit from it, they would just accuse the person of being a false witness. That is what they were doing to Jesus. He was stealing their income, not just the people's faith. They didn't like that He was doing it for free… But this man didn't care about profit. He cared about his little girl. He was willing to give up everything (including his title and authority in the temple) to heal his child. That is literally what he is seen doing here."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were \"astonished.\" \"Astonished\" means \"throw into wonderment.\" They were completely confused about how this could take place. Even if their daughter was not dead (yet just sleeping), they saw her more sick than was capable of being healed, and yet it took Jesus just simply SAYING it to heal this child instantly. Mind you, the lady that touched Jesus had faith that made her whole. It was not just about touching Jesus. If she truly believed that Jesus could heal her, it would have been enough for her to be healed. She touched him though, and that solidified her faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"This little girl didn't touch Jesus at all, and she is instantly healed. Not just healed, but brought back to life. It was certain she was dead. Jesus said she was \"sleeping.\" That was not to say that she was just resting for now. She was certainly dead. She just wasn't going to stay dead. That was what Jesus was getting at. She was in a dream state with the Lord. She was in eternity, but brought back from that for this miracle. This will not be the first time a miracle like this takes place either, and it will be astonishing every single time we read about it."}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He says, \"Don't tell anyone.\" How can they (as parents) ever keep this a secret? This would be nearly impossible to keep quiet about. Their daughter was dead and raised back to life! That is a miracle worth sharing! Jesus knows that it won't be kept secret. Maybe it did for a time, but eventually they would have no choice but to just let it out and share this amazingly good news with everyone around him."},{"kind":"para","text":"I love the fact that Jesus tells them to feed her. She likely hadn't eaten in days, and was probably starved. She needed some food. When I'm sick, the last thing I want to do is eat. This further proves that Jesus didn't just bring her back to life, He completely healed her. She was hungry, and her parents are there to provide her with some sustenance again! They were probably excited about taking care of her. They likely thought they would never get that opportunity again after what they had just experienced. It must have been a relief to know that they still had the time and opportunity to love and nurse their little girl."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":43}],"verses":{"1":"And they came over unto the other side of the sea, into the country of the Gadarenes.","2":"And when he was come out of the ship, immediately there met him out of the tombs a man with an unclean spirit,","3":"Who had his dwelling among the tombs; and no man could bind him, no, not with chains:","4":"Because that he had been often bound with fetters and chains, and the chains had been plucked asunder by him, and the fetters broken in pieces: neither could any man tame him.","5":"And always, night and day, he was in the mountains, and in the tombs, crying, and cutting himself with stones.","6":"But when he saw Jesus afar off, he ran and worshipped him,","7":"And cried with a loud voice, and said, What have I to do with thee, Jesus, thou Son of the most high God? I adjure thee by God, that thou torment me not.","8":"For he said unto him, Come out of the man, thou unclean spirit.","9":"And he asked him, What is thy name? And he answered, saying, My name is Legion: for we are many.","10":"And he besought him much that he would not send them away out of the country.","11":"Now there was there nigh unto the mountains a great herd of swine feeding.","12":"And all the devils besought him, saying, Send us into the swine, that we may enter into them.","13":"And forthwith Jesus gave them leave. And the unclean spirits went out, and entered into the swine: and the herd ran violently down a steep place into the sea, (they were about two thousand;) and were choked in the sea.","14":"And they that fed the swine fled, and told it in the city, and in the country. And they went out to see what it was that was done.","15":"And they come to Jesus, and see him that was possessed with the devil, and had the legion, sitting, and clothed, and in his right mind: and they were afraid.","16":"And they that saw it told them how it befell to him that was possessed with the devil, and also concerning the swine.","17":"And they began to pray him to depart out of their coasts.","18":"And when he was come into the ship, he that had been possessed with the devil prayed him that he might be with him.","19":"Howbeit Jesus suffered him not, but saith unto him, Go home to thy friends, and tell them how great things the Lord hath done for thee, and hath had compassion on thee.","20":"And he departed, and began to publish in Decapolis how great things Jesus had done for him: and all men did marvel.","21":"And when Jesus was passed over again by ship unto the other side, much people gathered unto him: and he was nigh unto the sea.","22":"And, behold, there cometh one of the rulers of the synagogue, Jairus by name; and when he saw him, he fell at his feet,","23":"And besought him greatly, saying, My little daughter lieth at the point of death: I pray thee, come and lay thy hands on her, that she may be healed; and she shall live.","24":"And Jesus went with him; and much people followed him, and thronged him.","25":"And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,","26":"And had suffered many things of many physicians, and had spent all that she had, and was nothing bettered, but rather grew worse,","27":"When she had heard of Jesus, came in the press behind, and touched his garment.","28":"For she said, If I may touch but his clothes, I shall be whole.","29":"And straightway the fountain of her blood was dried up; and she felt in her body that she was healed of that plague.","30":"And Jesus, immediately knowing in himself that virtue had gone out of him, turned him about in the press, and said, Who touched my clothes?","31":"And his disciples said unto him, Thou seest the multitude thronging thee, and sayest thou, Who touched me?","32":"And he looked round about to see her that had done this thing.","33":"But the woman fearing and trembling, knowing what was done in her, came and fell down before him, and told him all the truth.","34":"And he said unto her, Daughter, thy faith hath made thee whole; go in peace, and be whole of thy plague.","35":"While he yet spake, there came from the ruler of the synagogue's house certain which said, Thy daughter is dead: why troublest thou the Master any further?","36":"As soon as Jesus heard the word that was spoken, he saith unto the ruler of the synagogue, Be not afraid, only believe.","37":"And he suffered no man to follow him, save Peter, and James, and John the brother of James.","38":"And he cometh to the house of the ruler of the synagogue, and seeth the tumult, and them that wept and wailed greatly.","39":"And when he was come in, he saith unto them, Why make ye this ado, and weep? the damsel is not dead, but sleepeth.","40":"And they laughed him to scorn. But when he had put them all out, he taketh the father and the mother of the damsel, and them that were with him, and entereth in where the damsel was lying.","41":"And he took the damsel by the hand, and said unto her, Talitha cumi; which is, being interpreted, Damsel, I say unto thee, arise.","42":"And straightway the damsel arose, and walked; for she was of the age of twelve years. And they were astonished with a great astonishment.","43":"And he charged them straitly that no man should know it; and commanded that something should be given her to eat."}},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 06"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our previous chapter, Jesus did some miraculous healing to a man possessed with many devils, a lady that had twelve years worth of a blood problem, and a little girl who had died before Jesus could get there. We spoke about the common thread between all of these healings, and it was not that Jesus was present with them. It was their faith that made them whole. Only by faith was Jesus willing to heal. Without faith, there was no reason for Jesus to do His work. All He wants is the faith of the people."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we will see how the lack of faith of his own people gets to Him, and how desirious He is to reach as many as possible with the gospel message. He will do whatever it takes to make sure that everyone is invited into the kingdom, and He will send His own people (who are following Him) to do it so that everyone has the chance to hear and come to Him. He is preparing the disciples for the future ministry they will pursue after He has gone away and left them on their own to continue the church."}],"previously":["In our previous chapter, Jesus performed some miraculous healings. He healed a man possessed with many devils, a lady who had suffered from a blood problem for twelve years, and a little girl who had died before Jesus could reach her. We discussed the common thread between all of these healings, and it was not that Jesus was present with them. It was their faith that made them whole. Only by faith was Jesus willing to heal. Without faith, there was no reason for Jesus to do His work. All He wants is the faith of the people. In this chapter, we will see how the lack of faith of His own people affects Him, and how desirous He is to reach as many as possible with the gospel message. He will do whatever it takes to ensure that everyone is invited into the kingdom, and He will send His own people (who are following Him) to do it so that everyone has the chance to hear and come to Him. He is preparing the disciples for the future ministry they will pursue after He has gone away and left them on their own to continue the church."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Rejected By His Own","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus goes home to Nazareth with His disciples. I imagine Jesus had a real burden for these people. We will see that His own people are not very attuned to His claims, though. They know Him better than anyone else because He grew up with these people. Sadly, the people you are closest to are the hardest people to reach. They are the most stubborn about your message. They are trying to convince themselves that they know you better than you know your spiritual nature. No one, however, can know your Spirit better than you know your Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus lived a perfect life, even from His youth, so they had nothing they could hold against Him specifically. His parents and brothers, on the other hand, were not perfect. So they will use them as an example of sin rather than Him specifically."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of being enamored by His words, they are offended by Him. He is giving them life-changing words of wisdom. He is offering them salvation, and they are not happy about it. They use His seemingly dismal job title against Him. Carpenters were important, but they were not rich men. They were usually quite poor if you were just a carpenter or mason worker. They thought, “How could a carpenter know anything? He is no scholar; He is just making things up!”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then they look to His family and say, “Mary? We know Mary. She is the one who was pregnant before she married, right? She was the one claiming that she was pregnant and a virgin at the same time! She is a heretic as well! She has no grounds to stand on and claim anything about Jesus, so we can’t trust her obviously biased opinions.” Then they look at the half-brothers of the Lord. They say, “James, Joses, Juda, and Simon? They are all a bunch of sinners too! So are His sisters! They are pretty rambunctious ne’er-do-wells. They have no affirmative testimony for this Jesus fellow’s acclamations either. In fact, they don’t even agree with His teachings! Jesus must be a liar! Why does He think He’s something so special?”"},{"kind":"para","text":"They were right. Jesus must stand alone by His own testimony. I guarantee that if you look at my background, you will not find a righteous young man who always kept the best and most glorious company. I was a terrible child, a teenage teenager to be sure. The worst of the worst of sinners, and my parents were just as bad. My dad was an agnostic, my mom an unsaved woman, my brother a troublemaker. It would be evident to anyone who looks at my history that I am not a great example of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, though, I have turned that whole life around. Yet, the people who knew me before my salvation would claim that I am not saved because of the life I lived before. They think that people don’t truly change. Yet, here I am, rejecting those things I once thought were good and useful. They still won’t believe it though, and I’ve been saved for ten years (as of this writing)."},{"kind":"para","text":"They couldn’t say anything about Jesus’ background, however. They could only accuse the people He grew up with. Jesus was a very different story. This is the sad reality that takes place after they are seen disgusted in Christ."}]},{"start":4,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Provenly... Over and over again through scripture (what they claimed to know for sure) was that prophets were mistreated only by their own people. Any other nation or people would take what a prophet is saying and see if it's true. Their own people, however, would reject and deny anything a prophet said, and then try to ignore the problems when they come. Jesus is warning them about what it will be like without salvation, and they don't want to hear it because it's His own people. Everyone else (especially the Samaritans) would gladly take it."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He went to the people that were treated as less than to try and teach, but they just did not feel concerned about it. It was pretty strange to Jesus. He tries to tell them all the truth, and they just don't care. I think it is pretty difficult to amaze Jesus. How can you amaze the man that has seen it all? There are some things, however, that we will see that actually amaze Jesus. This is one of them. He is amazed that they are not willing to believe. I think the stupidity of mankind today still amazes Him. So what Jesus does next is brilliant. If He can't reach His own people, then He will find a way to reach them."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":13,"title":"Two and Two","entries":[{"start":7,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The premise of this was that they were going to be taken care of when they returned. If Jesus cannot personally reach these people (and at this point, there is a multitude of people that need to be met with), then He will send out His disciples to take some of the load off His shoulders. He teaches them how to draw out demons and gives them a simple explanation of the Gospel. His goal is to reach people with salvation, not just with healings. He sends them two by two so they don’t go alone. That is an important thing to note."},{"kind":"para","text":"If your pastor or ministry worker sends you out to work by yourself, be somewhat wary and cautious. We should be going with others to help lighten the load from us. Sometimes it makes it easier (as well) to have more to say and have an extra person in prayer for us while we do the speaking (vice versa). They were in this as an urgency, not as a vacation. They needed to move fast and gather the people for ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the need, by the way. The need was MINISTRY. That is still the need today. Reject the message all you want, just don’t reject the kindness of God’s people to serve you and bless you. They were carrying a light load and a clear message."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's a very important message to any and all Christians who are going to go out and minister. Don't be afraid to hang around. If people are welcoming to you, take that into consideration and take your time talking to them. That is what each of us needs to learn. We are so much in a hurry to just give out an invite and move on that we miss out on the most needful part of door knocking, which is the ministry part. Being there for the person you are trying to talk to is more important than just delivering a message. Jesus demonstrates that from the very beginning of this book. He didn't just preach; He ministered. That was the takeaway for Peter as well. He lived what He preached."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"People who don’t accept you, which is far more often than those who do, must simply be left behind. Just walk away. Don’t stick around. Sadly, their fate will be worse than those in Sodom and Gomorrah. Why? Well, because they have the promise of the salvation of God right in front of them and they are willing to reject it. Sodom and Gomorrah had plenty of time to change, but they just slowly got worse and worse until God had to do something about it. These people in the time of Christ are openly rejecting the salvation of the Lord with it right in front of them."},{"kind":"para","text":"The prospect is simple, and the instructions are clear. That part (for the soul winner) is easy. We know what we are supposed to talk about and do. We just need the courage to get up and do it. I think it is another reason that Jesus sends them out in twos. He wants us to push and encourage each other to get out of our comfort zones."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Can I just point out that it was all TWELVE of them that are doing this? That includes Judas Iscariot (which, if you didn't know, is the one that is going to betray Jesus later on). He had the power to minister just like the rest of the disciples. Jesus taught them all and gave them the ability to do what He was doing. We all can preach; there is no excuse to avoid it. We may not all have the ability to \"cast out many devils, or anoint the sick with oil, or heal them,” but you certainly have the ability to preach. Help them to turn from their sins and towards the Lord. That is what John the Baptist preached too. We will find that John was killed around this time for this same exact ministry in the coming verses…"}]}]},{"start":14,"end":20,"title":"The Resurrection of John","entries":[{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Herod felt guilty, obviously. We learn in this chapter that Herod was actually afraid of John. But there is a gift to Herodias' daughter to kill John. That will be obvious in a moment. They have heard about what Jesus is doing, and instead of understanding Him as the Christ, they think He is someone from the past that has come back from the dead. Herod thought it was John the Baptist. Others said, \"There's no way it can be John! It has to be Elijah coming back after he was previously translated.\" They were told in the Old Testament that Elijah was supposed to come back."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD:","ref":"Malachi 4:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Little did they know that this was speaking about John the Baptist. So, really, they were talking about the same person. Except John died, and Elijah was just taken up. These were probably Sadducees that said it was Elijah. They didn't believe in a literal resurrection."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Philip is Herodias' first husband, before she remarried to Antipas (who has a lot more power and authority). Antipas will become a problem. This whole family is a problem. John points out their sin, and Herodias isn't happy about it. It is interesting how that works. Just as a warning to anyone reading today, if you point out someone else's sin, they probably won't like it. That is just the nature of fallen man. They don't like it when they are confronted for their wrongdoings, because they don't like to be thought of as someone that is wrong."}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Herod knew there was something special about John. He did not want to kill him; he wanted to learn from John. Something comes up that causes him to have to kill John, and he is grieved with the prospect greatly. Obviously, it grieves him so much that he is afraid that John has likely come back from the dead to make him miserable. John likely preached a message about resurrection to Herod, and so he believed that John was telling the truth and coming to haunt him. Rather, it will be the Lord Himself coming to haunt Herod in a hope for change and repentance."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":29,"title":"John Beheaded","entries":[{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Herodias’ daughter had the privilege of getting whatever she wanted. You might say, “Well, not whatever; there had to be some sort of stipulations. Obviously, Herod could just say no to the death of John the Baptist.” We will see that he has sealed his fate in the next verse."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He would gladly even give her HALF the kingdom. That's a lot of authority for her to do whatever she wants. She is excited about the prospect, and so she takes a bit to think about it. She wants it to be something good. She was sent to please Herod so that this would take place (her mother had hoped that it would come to this), and now she has to give him a request for something up to half the kingdom. Instead of just acclaiming power, she goes back to her ungodly mother."}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He could have rejected her. He was infatuated by his flesh and by her beauty rather than doing what was right. He had heard the message of John the Baptist over and over again and was interested, but the words were thrown on stony ground. They were starting to bud, but they were never able to take root. So, he followed his fleshly desire, rather than putting his foot down (as a ruler, mind you) and simply saying, \"No. John is a good man, and I cannot do this for you.\" He would rather follow his flesh than follow the truth and grace of the Lord. John was a good preacher to him, and he is going to drive himself and the rest of his family into the ground because of this move right here."}]},{"start":27,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I can’t imagine the feelings of the disciples here. John is the first martyr, killed because of his faith. He was in prison for so long, but this was finally his release. Sadly, it was not a release into the outside world to minister to people. It was a brutal death out of nothing. All the disciples will experience something similar in their own faith stories (some even more brutal and painful)."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was just the start of their ministry. If this didn’t turn them away from what they were about to partake in, then they will only grow in faith from here. It will have its rocky moments (that is to be sure), but this will be a still image that will be engrained into their ministry walks for the rest of their lives. Jesus was burdened about this too, but we can save that for a different discussion."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":34,"title":"Gathering Together","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I want to focus on this here because I see a growing theme in the church that is not aligning properly with scripture. The pastor does not send leaders out to minister and then wash his hands of ever having to be involved with that part of the church again. That is the wrong way to do it. We must take the example of Jesus here. He sent them out, and they came back to give an account of what they had done and taught. He wanted to be sure that it was up to par with what He had given them to do. He would know if they were lying, but there was no reason for them to lie about it because He gave them a very simple task. In all honesty, soulwinning is not that hard. It's scary, but it's not difficult. It just needs to be done in prayer, boldness, and unity. We should all be doing it together."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ministry is a lot. There is not much time to rest. Jesus commands it here though. He does not say, \"If you feel like you need to rest, come here and do it.\" Rather He commands, \"Go over here where there are no people, and rest because you will need your energy for more ministry later on.\" If you cannot find a time for resting in the Lord, you will never minister properly for Him."}]},{"start":32,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are robbing the disciples of the privilege of rest. They are not being sensitive to them. They were all in need. I do feel bad for my pastor. I pray that he gets rest. I don’t see him taking it often. The problem is, we see a lot of needs get swept into the background. That has happened at every church I’ve been a part of. We do not have a close relationship with the ministers of our church because there is too much calling for their attention. There is not much time for rest. I try to honor that, even at the risk of being left behind and forgotten about."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, that is part of my ministry. It has been for a long time. I am busy ministering, and there is not much time to be ministered to. It is a hard thing to feel forsaken, rejected, and then turn around and pour more out to people, but that is the literal life of a minister. It’s not right, and it’s something that needs to be worked on, and we will see how Jesus works on it throughout the gospel accounts. Here’s how Jesus thinks about it when he sees the people preventing the disciples from rest:"}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He had compassion on them. It didn't grow his wearisome, it grew His Mini…stry...some (I tried to make it rhyme but it didn’t work)… The more people that were hurting, the more compassion He had to reach them and heal them. People were not a burden to Him. People grow to be a bit of a burden to us."},{"kind":"para","text":"If I'm being honest, there is something that tends to drag on my Spirit when someone comes to me with the same sins over and over again even though I have counseled them through the same scriptures over and over again with the same end results over and over again. But to Jesus... He saw their sins, and needs, and necessities, and it grew His desire to be in their midst and serve them. He literally LOVED to serve the people. So He gives the disciples a break by going out there and serving the people. He is capable of doing it, by the way. He is 100% God. But He is also 100% man. There would need to be times to Himself. He will find those times, but it won't be often enough."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":44,"title":"Feeding 5,000","entries":[{"start":35,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people didn’t want to leave. I think the disciples wanted to get rest, which is why they asked Jesus to send the multitudes away. I’m sure they were also concerned about Jesus getting rest too. Perhaps they actually wanted these people to get some rest, as they had been going nonstop with them as well. If the ministry was hectic for them, it was surely hectic for the people following."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is funny. They said, “Hey Jesus, maybe you should send everyone away so they can eat. We can just rest here and wait for them to get back.” And Jesus says, “How about this, YOU give them some food.” Of course, that would be impossible for the disciples to do. They ask about how they are going to get that much food to feed that many people. Jesus does this on purpose because He likes to use parables to show the power of the Lord and His salvation. These people were likely very hungry."},{"kind":"para","text":"The disciples were not wrong about this. But what they needed to understand was that there is a far more important meal that they should be partaking in, and it was not just the food that can fill their stomachs. They needed to pay attention to the spiritual feeding that Christ has been giving to them. But He will perform a miracle here that will (prayerfully) help them see that Jesus was the King and Lord of provision. He is the Savior through and through. Even before His death on the cross, they can receive provision and salvation through Him."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"By the way, Jesus didn't need to ask. He knew how many loaves they had. He needed them to be certain that there was not enough food for everyone, because what Jesus was about to do was nothing short of a miracle."}]},{"start":39,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are assured that there were thousands here. We will see that in a moment. But there was not nearly enough fish and bread for the twelve, let alone any after that. Yet there is a multitude of people who needed to eat. He multiplied this small amount of food in front of everyone. Every single person didn't just eat. There is a difference between eating and being filled. It says they were \"filled.\" I could break up a small piece of bread between a few hundred people, and everyone would likely have a small bite of something. But that is not what happened here. It says they were all FULL. Then the real miracle happens next:"}]},{"start":43,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were twelve baskets leftover, completely full of bread and fish. Jesus is the provider! He fed thousands with little to no food, and they must have been astounded by this amazing feat. I'm positive the disciples were amazed (whether the rest of the people were or weren't isn't really said). There had to be a few who saw this miracle and were completely confused about how this happened. It happened because the Lord is the provider. The disciples must depend on Him."}]}]},{"start":45,"end":52,"title":"Walk on Water","entries":[{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus fed them first, and we get that word \"straightway\" again. He is back into rapid succession. This is happening immediately after He had just fed over five thousand people. He sends the people away, tells His disciples to get into a boat, and lets them know that He will see them in a bit."}]},{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is getting His time away for a moment. Again, we see that He has some time of rest. He has to make it happen, but it eventually comes to pass. He has time to be with His Father and just sit and pray. He is also using this opportunity to prepare for the next amazing miracle."}]},{"start":47,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Jesus was watching them for a while before He decided to walk out towards them. The disciples seem to be rowing against the current, and they aren't going very fast. They must have been rowing quite hard. Jesus was able to walk faster than they were rowing. He almost walks right by them; they were having such a hard time. I don't know if it was stormy, but it was certainly windy. The waves were not working in their favor. It is clear, though, that they were in deep waters and there was no way for them to get to shore outside of the ship except to swim. Yet Jesus walks right over to them."}]},{"start":49,"end":51,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are missing some context here. Yet again, Peter has left an embarrassing (and important) moment out of this account. He has left out the part where he asks Jesus if he (Peter) can walk out to Him. Jesus gives him the ability to do it, and Peter starts off strong and suddenly starts to sink when he pays attention to the waves and not on Christ. Thankfully, Matthew doesn't shy away from it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Peter answered him and said, Lord, if it be thou, bid me come unto thee on the water. And he said, Come. And when Peter was come down out of the ship, he walked on the water, to go to Jesus. But when he saw the wind boisterous, he was afraid; and beginning to sink, he cried, saying, Lord, save me.","ref":"Matthew 14:28-30"},{"kind":"para","text":"That should be everyone's cry, by the way. But this is another example of how we can believe it's Peter giving this account. Whatever doesn't embarrass Peter."}]},{"start":52,"end":52,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an even funnier verse to me, knowing that there is more to this story that is not told here. Peter points it back to everyone else and says, \"They were all amazed only because they were so hard-hearted that they forgot about the five thousand people he had just fed.\" And yet it's Peter who loses faith in the water. But we won't talk about that part."}]}]},{"start":53,"end":56,"title":"Coming To Gennesaret","entries":[{"start":53,"end":53,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This might seem like a small thing to the reader, but I think it's important that we note where they have landed. They were originally planning to go to Capernaum on this trip. Instead, they end up a little further south at Gennesaret. It is here that King Antipas is ruling. He is the one that desired John the Baptist's death. I have no doubt that this is the reason that Jesus comes to this particular stretch of land. He is not going to let this king’s evil reign rule over the opportunity to heal and prove who Jesus is."}]},{"start":54,"end":55,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was nothing that Antipas was going to do to stop Jesus from serving these people. They all knew who Jesus was and what He was capable of doing. Mind you, He is there to preach the same message as John the Baptist. We are assured of that in the gospel of John."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this gospel account, we are focused on His ministry miracles, but the message is always there as the focal point. It should never be in the background. That is what it was in the life of Peter, though. The message was in the background. He purposely sprinkles it in as a reminder because he wants us to understand how he was focused on the wrong thing, and how easy it is for us (who are reading this account) to focus on the wrong thing too."},{"kind":"para","text":"He (Jesus) is preaching the message of the gospel and repentance, and the king was likely to hear about it and bring back to memory the preaching of John the Baptist. This will certainly rub him the wrong way. This is why he was spoken about briefly earlier in this chapter. It will be Antipas who has a role in the crucifixion of Christ as well. This guy is just evil."}]},{"start":56,"end":56,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was still healing though, and if they decided to reject the message, they could not reject His obvious miracles. The Lord was doing a work whether there were evil rulers or not. Whether the leaders accepted Him or not, God was going to do what He came to do, and that was to save those that needed salvation. That is everyone, by the way. We all need the Savior. Sadly, there will be many who reject Him. But the opportunity is open to all, and His work will continue forward no matter how many voices you try to silence to rid the world of victory in Jesus."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":38},{"start":39,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":44},{"start":45,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":46},{"start":47,"end":48},{"start":49,"end":51},{"start":52,"end":52},{"start":53,"end":53},{"start":54,"end":55},{"start":56,"end":56}],"verses":{"1":"And he went out from thence, and came into his own country; and his disciples follow him.","2":"And when the sabbath day was come, he began to teach in the synagogue: and many hearing him were astonished, saying, From whence hath this man these things? and what wisdom is this which is given unto him, that even such mighty works are wrought by his hands?","3":"Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, the brother of James, and Joses, and of Juda, and Simon? and are not his sisters here with us? And they were offended at him.","4":"But Jesus, said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.","5":"And he could there do no mighty work, save that he laid his hands upon a few sick folk, and healed them.","6":"And he marvelled because of their unbelief. And he went round about the villages, teaching.","7":"And he called unto him the twelve, and began to send them forth by two and two; and gave them power over unclean spirits;","8":"And commanded them that they should take nothing for their journey, save a staff only; no scrip, no bread, no money in their purse:","9":"But be shod with sandals; and not put on two coats.","10":"And he said unto them, In what place soever ye enter into an house, there abide till ye depart from that place.","11":"And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when ye depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrha in the day of judgment, than for that city.","12":"And they went out, and preached that men should repent.","13":"And they cast out many devils, and anointed with oil many that were sick, and healed them.","14":"And king Herod heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad:) and he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.","15":"Others said, That it is Elias. And others said, That it is a prophet, or as one of the prophets.","16":"But when Herod heard thereof, he said, It is John, whom I beheaded: he is risen from the dead.","17":"For Herod himself had sent forth and laid hold upon John, and bound him in prison for Herodias' sake, his brother Philip's wife: for he had married her.","18":"For John had said unto Herod, It is not lawful for thee to have thy brother's wife.","19":"Therefore Herodias had a quarrel against him, and would have killed him; but she could not:","20":"For Herod feared John, knowing that he was a just man and an holy, and observed him; and when he heard him, he did many things, and heard him gladly.","21":"And when a convenient day was come, that Herod on his birthday made a supper to his lords, high captains, and chief estates of Galilee;","22":"And when the daughter of the said Herodias came in, and danced, and pleased Herod and them that sat with him, the king said unto the damsel, Ask of me whatsoever thou wilt, and I will give it thee.","23":"And he sware unto her, Whatsoever thou shalt ask of me, I will give it thee, unto the half of my kingdom.","24":"And she went forth, and said unto her mother, What shall I ask? And she said, The head of John the Baptist.","25":"And she came in straightway with haste unto the king, and asked, saying, I will that thou give me by and by in a charger the head of John the Baptist.","26":"And the king was exceeding sorry; yet for his oath's sake, and for their sakes which sat with him, he would not reject her.","27":"And immediately the king sent an executioner, and commanded his head to be brought: and he went and beheaded him in the prison,","28":"And brought his head in a charger, and gave it to the damsel: and the damsel gave it to her mother.","29":"And when his disciples heard of it, they came and took up his corpse, and laid it in a tomb.","30":"And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Jesus, and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.","31":"And he said unto them, Come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while: for there were many coming and going, and they had no leisure so much as to eat.","32":"And they departed into a desert place by ship privately.","33":"And the people saw them departing, and many knew him, and ran afoot thither out of all cities, and outwent them, and came together unto him.","34":"And Jesus, when he came out, saw much people, and was moved with compassion toward them, because they were as sheep not having a shepherd: and he began to teach them many things.","35":"And when the day was now far spent, his disciples came unto him, and said, This is a desert place, and now the time is far passed:","36":"Send them away, that they may go into the country round about, and into the villages, and buy themselves bread: for they have nothing to eat.","37":"He answered and said unto them, Give ye them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?","38":"He saith unto them, How many loaves have ye? go and see. And when they knew, they say, Five, and two fishes.","39":"And he commanded them to make all sit down by companies upon the green grass.","40":"And they sat down in ranks, by hundreds, and by fifties.","41":"And when he had taken the five loaves and the two fishes, he looked up to heaven, and blessed, and brake the loaves, and gave them to his disciples to set before them; and the two fishes divided he among them all.","42":"And they did all eat, and were filled.","43":"And they took up twelve baskets full of the fragments, and of the fishes.","44":"And they that did eat of the loaves were about five thousand men.","45":"And straightway he constrained his disciples to get into the ship, and to go to the other side before unto Bethsaida, while he sent away the people.","46":"And when he had sent them away, he departed into a mountain to pray.","47":"And when even was come, the ship was in the midst of the sea, and he alone on the land.","48":"And he saw them toiling in rowing; for the wind was contrary unto them: and about the fourth watch of the night he cometh unto them, walking upon the sea, and would have passed by them.","49":"But when they saw him walking upon the sea, they supposed it had been a spirit, and cried out:","50":"For they all saw him, and were troubled. And immediately he talked with them, and saith unto them, Be of good cheer: it is I; be not afraid.","51":"And he went up unto them into the ship; and the wind ceased: and they were sore amazed in themselves beyond measure, and wondered.","52":"For they considered not the miracle of the loaves: for their heart was hardened.","53":"And when they had passed over, they came into the land of Gennesaret, and drew to the shore.","54":"And when they were come out of the ship, straightway they knew him,","55":"And ran through that whole region round about, and began to carry about in beds those that were sick, where they heard he was.","56":"And whithersoever he entered, into villages, or cities, or country, they laid the sick in the streets, and besought him that they might touch if it were but the border of his garment: and as many as touched him were made whole."}},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 07"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the Lord continue His ministry, and He was led to minister to His own hometown (which end up rejecting Him because they knew Him growing up). He takes the lead and sends His disciples out (two by two) to reach more people seeing as He couldn't go to everyone and be everywhere all at once. This would allow His disciples to hopefully reach some of His own hometown as well. If they weren't going to take Jesus' Words for it, they could take the disciples word. Then we get news about the death of John the Baptist, and the scare that Herod has because of Jesus' ministry. He is under the impression that John has somehow come back from the dead. Good news comes back to Jesus from the disciples after they were sent out to preach, and Jesus does an amazing miracle of feeding five thousand people with just a few loaves of bread and some fish. He then is seen walking on water, and we end the chapter off by Jesus healing more of the sick and needy."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see some of the religious cults come to Christ to try and pin something on Him and His disciples. They are trying to make a fool out of them, but ultimately they just amek a fool of themselves. We then see Jesus continue to do more healing, and lead more people into salvation, and an increase in faith."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we see the Lord continue His ministry. He was led to minister to His own hometown, which ends up rejecting Him because they knew Him growing up. Taking the lead, He sends His disciples out two by two to reach more people, as He couldn’t go to everyone and be everywhere all at once. This would allow His disciples to hopefully reach some of His own hometown as well. If they weren’t going to take Jesus’ words for it, they could take the disciples’ word. Then we get news about the death of John the Baptist and the scare that Herod has because of Jesus’ ministry. He is under the impression that John has somehow come back from the dead. Good news comes back to Jesus from the disciples after they were sent out to preach, and Jesus does an amazing miracle of feeding five thousand people with just a few loaves of bread and some fish. He then is seen walking on water, and we end the chapter off by Jesus healing more of the sick and needy. In this chapter, we see some of the religious cults come to Christ to try and pin something on Him and His disciples. They are trying to make a fool out of them, but ultimately they just make a fool of themselves. We then see Jesus continue to do more healing and lead more people into salvation, resulting in an increase in faith."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"The Scribes and Pharisees","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it’s sufficient (at this point) to make mention of these two groups. The Pharisees were not a bad group in themselves. We could probably say that about a great deal of groups if I could be honest. They were not bad about what they believed; it just became a religious rite rather than a spiritual relationship. They started to do their work because it’s what they knew was important. They did it to keep a good “standing” with the Lord, rather than because it pleases the Lord. They did it to maintain their salvation, rather than because they were saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lutheran Church started the same. Even the Catholic Church, honestly. They had the right reasons at the beginning and fell away from those reasons rather quickly because it became a work rather than a relationship. The scribes as well. Ezra was a scribe. It is said that he was the first scribe. He wrote out the Word of the Lord and made copies of it for others to read. That doesn’t sound like a bad thing, does it? Well, it became a bad thing during the time of Christ because they were living by the law, not by the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, their service to the law of God separated them from a relationship with God. That is the big problem with religions and isms today as well. They are not serving the Lord because they love the Lord and know the Lord, but rather because they feel like it is necessary. They have read their scriptures with the wrong tone. They have read it out of judgment and turmoil, rather than grace and peace. Grace and peace is the theme of Paul’s letters, by the way. But we can talk about that later."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Scribes and Pharisees are coming to Jesus, not because they want to know Jesus better, but only because they didn’t agree with Jesus’ acclamations about His Father. Anytime these religious people come to Christ, it usually leaves them either feeling more undone or more prideful. It’s never left on a high note."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If the disciples had eaten with clean hands, the scribes and Pharisees would have found fault. That is what religions often look for—your faults, your flaws, what needs to be fixed. However, that is not what God’s children are called to do. We are called to live a life that pleases the Lord and to tell people about the Lord they are pleasing. If others are not doing that, it is not our place to hate them and bash them because of it. It is simply our place to tell the truth and live for the Lord. Let the Lord deal with the rest."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are not to be out looking for the faults and flaws in people. That will never end. We will be just as disgraceful and hypocritical as these Scribes and Pharisees depicted here. They are ignoring God’s working in order to point fingers at God’s people. That is the definition of religion today."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The key problem word here is “tradition.” Washing your hands is a good thing. I think it’s important to wash your hands before a meal. I tell my daughter to wash her hands as well before dinner. I want her hands to be clean from the dirt and grime of the day (especially because she touches things that are not the cleanest)."},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s not what the scribes and Pharisees were concerned about, though. They didn’t care about germs. They cared about keeping the law of their fathers. They wanted to hold onto tradition. They were willing to run down their own family and friends if it meant keeping to the traditions of their past. How tiring and burdensome it must be to constantly live like you have to walk on eggshells for the Lord. One slip-up, and you are in doom of God’s wrath. Another flaw of religion today."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is providing background on their seemingly useless tradition. The scribes and Pharisees considered themselves special because of their “cleanliness.” They believed that cleaning the outside was sufficient to be accepted by their religious elders. They thought that if you looked good and presentable outwardly, the inside was also good and presentable. Of course, that is not the case."},{"kind":"para","text":"If I eat a bowl of cereal and then rinse the outside of the bowl, it does not make the bowl good to use again. I knew of a man who had an old coffee cup that he carried around everywhere. Supposedly, he would take it to Jack-in-the-Box to have it refilled. He would tell the lady inside to just dump out the old coffee and fill it up with new coffee. Well, that’s all great and fine, but when she dumped out the old, she could tell that the cup had never been cleaned. There was moldy old coffee stuck in there still. Perhaps he cleaned the outside of the cup, but he never touched the inside. The cup (from the outside) looked presentable. The moment you opened it, however, it was a different story."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is how many of us are. We are presentable when we go to church, but if we spent some time with you, I’m willing to bet all sorts of things will start to open up, and we will see your true nature. We will see that the inside of you has not been cleaned by the Lord. Only the Lord can reach those parts of you."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":13,"title":"Hypocritical Observation","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I mean, at least they were asking a question. They didn’t ask it, though, because they were curious. They asked it because they were trying to entice a fight about traditions and religious sacrament. That is all they wanted to do. We will see that’s what they wanted by the way in which Jesus responds to them in the next verse. The question seems harmless here, but it was not a harmless question by any means. They were genuinely upset about the disciples’ code of conduct compared to the traditions they held so tightly."},{"kind":"para","text":"By the way, I think it’s important to realize that Christ replaces your traditions. If you place your traditions over Christ, you will recognize (over and over again) through scripture that Christ does not approve of this. He never has, and He never will. Your relationship with Him should triumph over your passed-down traditions and religion."}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The problem was the same as when Isaiah first addressed it. He spoke during a time when Israel was flourishing in their sin, living completely and utterly against the Lord. God warned them about capture and captivity if they didn’t turn. Unfortunately, they did not turn and consequently fell into captivity. They performed all the right “religious works” and offered all the right “sacrifices,” but they did so not because they loved the Lord, but because it was a religious tradition."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord had commanded them to do these things, so it was not as if they were practicing pagan worship. Rather, they were doing it because it was common and passed down rituals. This is the problem with religious traditions today. People no longer take communion because they love the Lord and want to remember Him; they do it because it’s a religious ritual. They no longer baptize because they love the Lord and identify with Christ; they do it because it’s a religious ritual. They no longer give (time or money) to their church because they love the Lord and want to see Him grow His church (build their own faiths in Him); they do it because it’s a religious ritual. They no longer hold church services and listen to the scriptures being read and preached on because they love the Lord and want to learn more about His Word; they do it because it’s a religious ritual. They no longer serve at the church because they love people and they love the Lord and want to grow that love for Him; they do it because it’s a religious ritual. They no longer go door to door sharing the gospel because they know it’s the truth and that is what gives people eternal life; they do it because it’s a religious ritual. They no longer pray for people, or for their loved ones, because they genuinely want to see the Lord work in the lives of others, or because they feel like God is actually accessible and listening; they do it because it’s a religious ritual."},{"kind":"para","text":"Throw any sort of religious activity into this sequence, and you will find that everything a religion does has nothing to do with growing your relationship and dependency on the Lord, and everything to do with keeping up with traditions (separating your heart from loving God to loving your traditions). That is what Christ is talking about here. He is sickened by it, and He makes it well known."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ hates that these people are rejecting the Lord to chase after their own traditions. If you were to ask the Pharisees at this time if they kept the commandments, they would all claim that they did. They would claim they were living perfectly before the Lord. They would say, \"I am a perfect specimen. I am the greatest example of a godly man.\" Except Christ knows they are not. He will point it out right now."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses commands us to honor our father and mother, yet there was a religious practice that literally cut ties with one’s own parents. It was called “Corban.” People would give their possessions to the temple, which would relieve them of their connection and commitment to serve and honor their own parents. This practice was quite diabolical, as it went against the very command of Moses by creating this tradition."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another religious problem today is the creation of rules to benefit one’s own religious rites. I hesitate to pick on the Catholic Church, but they are likely the most well-known for these types of issues. They create all sorts of strange sacraments to fit their religious ideologies, such as holy water, guardian angels, praying to saints, purgatory, sprinkle baptism, and confessionals. These types of religious traditions are made up and hold no Biblical weight for honoring and pleasing the Lord. They cling to these traditions more than they cling to the finished work of Christ on the cross or the Words of Moses."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Pharisees clung to the words of Moses with everything they had, but they also didn’t want to continuously support their parents for the rest of their lives. So, they made a means to separate themselves and reject the law of Moses. They probably thought they were okay with doing it too, because it didn’t really tie in with any law. However, Christ points out that it does, and they are all guilty of breaking it to keep to their unruly and unwelcome religious traditions."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They literally rob God's law and Word by making these traditions final. You know what traditions are good for? Sympathizing with your sin. They are only good for you to make an excuse to sin because the Word of the Lord goes against it, and you know it."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":23,"title":"Evil Hearts","entries":[{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Religion is not some sort of coat you can throw on to help you with your personal lifestyle. Rather, it's a fake front for a deeper longing that will never be filled without that relationship aspect with the Lord I was talking about earlier. That is what we all truly need. Make your outside look as pretty and accepting as possible, but it's on the inside that the Lord takes recognition. You can act the part and fool all the people on this planet, but you can never fool the Lord."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"According to Matthew, it was Peter that asked this question alone. The other disciples seem to be silent about what they had heard."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then answered Peter and said unto him, Declare unto us this parable.","ref":"Matthew 15:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's strange that they needed help understanding this parable. It seems pretty straightforward. Maybe they were thinking too deeply about it. Maybe they just had trouble connecting the dots. I don't really know, but I feel like it should have been something they understood pretty easily. In fact, it seems that Jesus thought this was a pretty simple one for them to understand as well, judging by what He says next:"}]},{"start":18,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Someone once said, “The heart of the problem is the problem of the heart.” Over and over again, as we’ve mentioned numerous times already, Jesus has been speaking about spiritual issues. He wants to heal the spiritual heart of man, not the physical heart alone. All of His healings were examples of a spiritual necessity. They kept missing it. The Pharisees certainly missed it because they were so focused on the physical washing that they didn’t realize their own necessity for spiritual cleansing. They were stuck in their phony traditions and fake worship of a god they just made up for themselves to continue to live in their sins and traditions. It is what comes out of the heart that is the problem, not what goes into the stomach."},{"kind":"para","text":"You can know exactly what a person’s heart is like because Christ gives a detailed list here. I, perhaps, don’t know who you are (reading this today). But I know for a certainty that THIS is your heart: “…evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders, thefts, covetousness, wickedness, deceit, lasciviousness, an evil eye, blasphemy, pride, foolishness…” It’s okay to admit it. It’s my heart too. That is why we need the Lord. We need Him to continuously cleanse our hearts. He will always be working on our hearts as long as we are living because we are a continuous work in progress. Praise the Lord that He doesn’t give up on us though. Praise God that He finds reason to cleanse our hearts out. Not just clean us up outwardly, but clean up our hearts inwardly."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":30,"title":"Crumbs To Dogs","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was too well known at this point to hide Himself. I don’t think He was actually trying to “hide”; I just think that He wasn’t really asking for people to know where He was. He had a lot on His plate and needed some time for rest, but that was seemingly not going to come any time soon. Ministry was starting to get quite hectic."}]},{"start":25,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a Gentile woman, through and through. Gentiles did not like Jews, and Jews did not like Gentiles. However, she trusted that Jesus was capable of doing some actual healing, as opposed to the religious leaders of that land. It might seem as if Jesus is being pretty harsh to this lady here if we are not careful to read this appropriately. He is being a bit harsh if I’m being honest. In Matthew’s gospel account, he says that the woman is being obnoxious. She was likely causing a great scene. He says this after a bit of this woman continuously pursuing the Lord. If Christ had said that to me (as a Gentile), I would have likely been offended. But not this lady. She has a different countenance altogether."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She agrees with His interpretation of her. She agrees that she is unworthy to ask anything from the Lord. She is nothing in this world, but the Lord is everything. She knows that for an absolute truth, and she knows that He is testing her faith."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was a little more than this that the Lord said (according to Matthew):"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Jesus answered and said unto her, O woman, great is thy faith: be it unto thee even as thou wilt. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.","ref":"Matthew 15:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"...great is thy faith...\" is an indication that the Lord wanted the world to see that this woman (a Gentile, let's not forget) had a far greater faith in the Lord than the Pharisees (who were Jewish, may we remember) did in the salvation of Christ. The Lord healed the woman's daughter because of her faith. It was her faith in the Lord alone that provided this miracle. It was not her work. It was not her pleading. It was not her demanding. It was her faith. It was tested and proven right in front of the entire Jewish assembly there."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite a miracle, isn’t it? Her daughter didn’t even have to be present with the Lord to be healed. It only took this woman to come to the Lord on behalf of her daughter to receive the blessing. She came by faith. That is the theme. Faith alone is what causes the blessings to occur. But it’s not the blessings that are the gift here. It’s the salvation message that is the real gift. May we not forget that because of the blessings."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a life lesson for all of us. Don’t let the Lord’s blessings overshadow the Lord’s salvation for you. Blessings are great, and they are a reason for us to worship the Lord. But just the fact that you are saved is a blessing enough to worship even if the Lord gave you nothing else for the rest of your life."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":37,"title":"Doing All Things Well","entries":[{"start":31,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This man was able to speak from the Lord, seemingly requesting it from the Father. Now, we have seen the Lord heal without having to touch, spit, or say much of anything. He simply heals. It seems He heals in different ways to different people for different reasons. Why He chose this way for this person, I cannot say. I believe it was more about the people around that person than it was for the person Himself. It was not about Him changing up His tactics; it was about getting the attention of those who would likely reject or deny Him. He can heal with any means possible. Even His own spit and touch can heal a man who cannot see, or cannot talk, or cannot even hear. He is more than capable of healing with any and all means necessary to prove His salvation message."},{"kind":"para","text":"It should be well noted that the Lord saves by His Work. He saved the whole world by His finished work on the Cross of Calvary. He also saved by His Word. He saved by speaking salvation into existence and living as the Word of God made flesh. He saved by touch as well. He touches the hearts of every single person who would come to Him in faith and receive their healing. He is able to save by all three factors even still today."}]},{"start":35,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know why (at this point) that the Lord charges anyone to keep their mouth shut. This person, specifically, had probably kept his mouth shut for nearly his entire life. He wasn't about to keep it shut any longer now that he was capable of speaking properly."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ has done all things well. The world will try to find some occasion to criticize the Lord for this means or that means. They might attempt to attribute some ungodly character trait to Him or dismiss Him as merely a good teacher. No, He has done ALL things well. You can tell from the busyness of His ministry at this point in time and the amount of backlash He was receiving for His Words and Work. Yet, He was still able, as 100% man, to do all things well. That would be a difficult thing for a minister to do today."},{"kind":"para","text":"They could not keep up with the pace of the Lord. The disciples could hardly keep up. We will see they struggled as ministers with Christ. Honestly, I think because they had such a hard time keeping up, it made them all the more excellent in their ministry after the ascension of Christ. They were ready for the thick of it because they were already in the middle of it."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":37}],"verses":{"1":"Then came together unto him the Pharisees, and certain of the scribes, which came from Jerusalem.","2":"And when they saw some of his disciples eat bread with defiled, that is to say, with unwashen, hands, they found fault.","3":"For the Pharisees, and all the Jews, except they wash their hands oft, eat not, holding the tradition of the elders.","4":"And when they come from the market, except they wash, they eat not. And many other things there be, which they have received to hold, as the washing of cups, and pots, brasen vessels, and of tables.","5":"Then the Pharisees and scribes asked him, Why walk not thy disciples according to the tradition of the elders, but eat bread with unwashen hands?","6":"He answered and said unto them, Well hath Esaias prophesied of you hypocrites, as it is written, This people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.","7":"Howbeit in vain do they worship me, teaching for doctrines the commandments of men.","8":"For laying aside the commandment of God, ye hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things ye do.","9":"And he said unto them, Full well ye reject the commandment of God, that ye may keep your own tradition.","10":"For Moses said, Honour thy father and thy mother; and, Whoso curseth father or mother, let him die the death:","11":"But ye say, If a man shall say to his father or mother, It is Corban, that is to say, a gift, by whatsoever thou mightest be profited by me; he shall be free.","12":"And ye suffer him no more to do ought for his father or his mother;","13":"Making the word of God of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.","14":"And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:","15":"There is nothing from without a man, that entering into him can defile him: but the things which come out of him, those are they that defile the man.","16":"If any man have ears to hear, let him hear.","17":"And when he was entered into the house from the people, his disciples asked him concerning the parable.","18":"And he saith unto them, Are ye so without understanding also? Do ye not perceive, that whatsoever thing from without entereth into the man, it cannot defile him;","19":"Because it entereth not into his heart, but into the belly, and goeth out into the draught, purging all meats?","20":"And he said, That which cometh out of the man, that defileth the man.","21":"For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,","22":"Thefts, covetousness, wickedness, deceit, lasciviousness, an evil eye, blasphemy, pride, foolishness:","23":"All these evil things come from within, and defile the man.","24":"And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid.","25":"For a certain woman, whose young daughter had an unclean spirit, heard of him, and came and fell at his feet:","26":"The woman was a Greek, a Syrophenician by nation; and she besought him that he would cast forth the devil out of her daughter.","27":"But Jesus said unto her, Let the children first be filled: for it is not meet to take the children's bread, and to cast it unto the dogs.","28":"And she answered and said unto him, Yes, Lord: yet the dogs under the table eat of the children's crumbs.","29":"And he said unto her, For this saying go thy way; the devil is gone out of thy daughter.","30":"And when she was come to her house, she found the devil gone out, and her daughter laid upon the bed.","31":"And again, departing from the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, he came unto the sea of Galilee, through the midst of the coasts of Decapolis.","32":"And they bring unto him one that was deaf, and had an impediment in his speech; and they beseech him to put his hand upon him.","33":"And he took him aside from the multitude, and put his fingers into his ears, and he spit, and touched his tongue;","34":"And looking up to heaven, he sighed, and saith unto him, Ephphatha, that is, Be opened.","35":"And straightway his ears were opened, and the string of his tongue was loosed, and he spake plain.","36":"And he charged them that they should tell no man: but the more he charged them, so much the more a great deal they published it;","37":"And were beyond measure astonished, saying, He hath done all things well: he maketh both the deaf to hear, and the dumb to speak."}},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 08"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we get a bit of an altercation with the Scribes and Pharisees. We talked pretty deeply about the problem with religious traditions versus following the Lord in as a relationship. The religious then (and still today) were extreme hypocrites. They would talk about their life of law, but then would twist the law if they needed it to suit them. The biggest problem they faced was with their hearts. They honored the Lord by mouth and work, but not in their heart. We also got another healing account from a girl that was possesed by a demon."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get a few more miracles, including with another feeding of the multitude. I believe the feeding of the five thousand represents the ministry of Jesus as the Earth is today, and the feeding of the four thousand is a representation of the ministry of Christ as it is in the tribulation. Due to the numbers that are represented (less people in this time) the place that it happens in (a desert and dry place), and the number of baskets leftover (seven). So we can study this together and make our own decisions on it."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we encountered an altercation with the Scribes and Pharisees. We discussed in depth the issue of religious traditions versus following the Lord in a relationship. The religious leaders of that time, and still today, were extreme hypocrites. They would speak about living by the law but would twist it to suit their needs. The biggest problem they faced was with their hearts. They honored the Lord with their words and actions, but not with their hearts. We also received another healing account involving a girl possessed by a demon. In this chapter, we encounter several more miracles, including another feeding of the multitude. I believe the feeding of the five thousand represents the ministry of Jesus as it is today, while the feeding of the four thousand symbolizes the ministry of Christ during the tribulation. This is due to the numbers involved (fewer people in this time), the location where it occurs (a desert and dry place), and the number of baskets left over (seven). We can study this together and make our own decisions on it."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"Feeding 4,000","entries":[{"start":1,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The seriousness of this situation should not be missed. Jesus has already performed a miracle with food, and I assume there wasn’t anyone really complaining. They were not concerned about their own food intake; they were only concerned about following Jesus and seeing what He would do next. The multitude of people at this point was “very great.” How great? We will see in a moment. Jesus knows that they have been traveling for three days now, and it was highly unlikely that these people packed lunches sufficient for three days. Instead of telling them to go home, He would have to feed them."},{"kind":"para","text":"May I just encourage the reader at this point. Jesus will never send you away empty. He feeds you first. Whether that be physically or spiritually is not up to me, but I know the Lord never sends you away empty-handed. He gives you something to chew on. He has done a miracle like this before (showing the obvious providence of the Lord through the miracle of multiplying bread and fish), and it seems He will do it again, proving that the Father is looking out for His own. In the first miracle, the people were only with Him for ONE day before He fed them. This time, it is THREE days. That is a long time to travel without food. I believe it also shows the lengths of Jesus’ ministry versus Jesus’ judgment. One will last longer than the other."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a silly question. They have seen this miracle once; what makes them think He can’t do it again? I know it has been a little bit since they last saw this, but it should have been astounding enough for them to remember. Actually, as we will see in a moment, there are about four thousand men sitting at this particular meeting. The first meeting saw about five thousand. This time there was less than before. Still, it would take another miracle to feed this many people, and the miracle is certainly going to come. But I don’t want to just look at this (or any of the miracles for that matter) as a repeated happenstance."},{"kind":"para","text":"Every miracle the Lord performs is sufficient for a greater purpose. He did not perform miracles just to show how powerful and great He was. Rather, He performed them as a symbol of salvation and faith every single time He did it. This one will be no different, and I hope we don’t miss it."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In this instance, we have the Lord asking them how many loaves they had, and they were ready to give an answer (as it seems). The first time this happened, the Lord asked them to go find providence, and they had five loaves of bread and two fish. They will have a few fish in a moment. Just seven loaves of bread for now. Still plenty of providence, though."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first time He told the people to sit on the grass. It was a nice, lush location; they were taking a seat on it. This time He is telling them to sit down \"on the ground.\" It is much later in the year, so I assume at this time there was not much green going around on the ground. It was either very dry and prickly grass, or just rock-solid Earth. Either way, it was not nearly as comfortable this time around. It has also been pointed out that the Lord blessed the loaves before, but this time gave \"thanks\" for the loaves. He will bless the fish in a moment, though:"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bread and fish are great representations of the Lord. He will go on to say that He is \"the bread of life.\" He is our sustenance."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus said unto them, I am the bread of life: he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst.","ref":"John 6:35"},{"kind":"para","text":"But the fish were symbols of humanity. Remember, Jesus asked Peter, Andrew, James, and John to follow Him because He would make them fishers of men."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus said unto them, Come ye after me, and I will make you to become fishers of men.","ref":"Mark 1:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"The fish were blessed by the Lord, and they are still blessed today. I think it's important to note the fish and the sheep in Scripture. I think I have talked quite a bit about sheep already as we have studied (and I will talk about it more as we go along), but fish are a very important theme in Scripture as well. Peter points out fish a lot (he was a fisherman, so that is his comfort topic). Jesus calls His followers \"sheep.\" But I believe before they are sheep, they are \"fish.\" They are just swimming along with not much direction or purpose. They are just living and have no idea what they are doing. When we are called out and caught by the Lord, He makes us a \"new creature.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe we become His sheep. We become, of course, His children. We are no longer just swimming around this life willy-nilly. Rather, we are following the Shepherd. Jesus blesses the fish. If my association is in any way accurate, I believe the Lord blesses the lost. He blesses the Lord providing for the lost. He blesses those who have not been captured. He wants them to be captured, though. He is still fishing for men today. He is still desiring to change them into a follower of Him. The blessings they receive are open invitations to praise and follow the Lord. They just don’t see their blessings like that. They squander the blessings, and they simply become fish food for bigger fish. The Lord is trying to catch you before that happens, though. You just have to be willing to jump into the net."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps there is no connection here. Maybe the two animals are different themes of the same thing. We are fish, AND we are sheep. I don’t see that being the case, though, if I can be honest. Jesus doesn’t tell Peter to “feed His fish.” Let’s look at what Jesus says:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So when they had dined, Jesus saith to Simon Peter, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me more than these? He saith unto him, Yea, Lord; thou knowest that I love thee. He saith unto him, Feed my lambs. He saith to him again the second time, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me? He saith unto him, Yea, Lord; thou knowest that I love thee. He saith unto him, Feed my sheep. He saith unto him the third time, Simon, son of Jonas, lovest thou me? Peter was grieved because he said unto him the third time, Lovest thou me? And he said unto him, Lord, thou knowest all things; thou knowest that I love thee. Jesus saith unto him, Feed my sheep.","ref":"John 21:15-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"If Jesus thought of fish and sheep as the same, I believe He would have been wiser to stick to the fish metaphor. Peter understood fish. He didn't really understand sheep. Peter would have to understand sheep, though, if He was going to lead the flock of God properly."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first feeding took up twelve leftover baskets, while this one is only seven. They were filled and satisfied, though. Why seven? Well, it’s the Lord’s perfect number. The first time it was twelve, and that was God’s indication of man’s perfection. The second time it’s seven, and that is the Lord’s indication of HIS perfection. God loves to work in sevens and twelves. We see that throughout nearly all of scripture. We will talk about that more in the book of Revelation."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is why I am under the impression that this feeding is representing the providence of the Lord during the tribulation. God provides for His own today (that’s the feeding of the five thousand), and He will provide for His small remnant in the end times (that’s the feeding of the four thousand). Whether you want to agree with this ideology or not is not a make-it-or-break-it theology in my opinion, but it is an interesting thing to note, and it gives further depth to the differences between this feeding and the first feeding."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“About” is stating that it’s a rough estimate. If they were just counting the men (which was common to do), and we didn’t include the wives and children, we assume “about” four thousand was the max. I am under the impression that there were over ten thousand here this day if we included the women and children. Neither here nor there, it was a decent multitude either way, and there was no way that anyone else could feed this many with the amount of bread and fish they had, yet it was accomplished this day."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":13,"title":"Looking For Signs","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a new location, and I don’t know where it is. There are assumptions about where this place could be, but I couldn’t give any guarantees. It’s interesting that Jesus goes here, though. I believe He likely went here because it’s not very commonly known. I don’t think there were many people at this place. Yet, we will see that no matter where Jesus goes, people will find Him. And no matter how few people there are, Jesus will give them the message of salvation."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Pharisees are really trying to trip Jesus up. They are pulling out all the stops and going to the most secluded places just to make sure Jesus has no place to run. They want to back Him in a corner to pin something on Him. Most religions are guilty of this today, honestly. They are still trying to pin something on Jesus. I have given up that game long ago."},{"kind":"para","text":"I had spent a great deal of time trying to find fault in Christ when I was first saved. I could not find anything, though, and so I found that it was a wasted effort. Now I try to find flaws in God’s Word. The more “flaws” I find, the more connections His Word makes, and I overwhelmingly realize how flawed I personally am as the reader of His Word. If there is any flaw in the Word of God, I still have yet to find it. I’m writing commentaries on every single line of this book and still have not found a flaw."},{"kind":"para","text":"Religions can only find flaws in Christ because they have twisted scripture to supplement their own flaws. There is no flaw in Christ. There is nothing you can pin against Him. The only thing you can pin on Christ is His perfection and sacrifice. Otherwise, whatever you try to twist to fail Christ only makes you fail."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just like the reader at this point, they were missing the whole purpose of Christ’s coming. It was not to show “signs and wonders.” We have seen Jesus do plenty of “signs” just in this chapter alone. They are missing the point. Again, I think Peter was focused on the actions of Christ more than the words of Christ. He is telling Mark about all the things Christ did, and even in the retelling (like at this moment), I believe he thought to himself, “Man. I was just like those Pharisees. I was just looking at the signs Christ was giving but missing what Jesus was ultimately doing.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Pharisees didn’t care about a sign. They could have seen all the other miracles of Christ and been just as satisfied with those as the best signs of His acclamation. They just wanted to hurt Jesus in any way possible, and Jesus wasn’t here for that. So He gets up and leaves. That small town missed out on a blessing from the Lord because of the continual rejection and degradation of the religious sect. Perhaps that is why our small city is missing out on so many blessings today."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":21,"title":"No Bread","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Okay, let’s backtrack for a moment. Did they not just experience a miracle with multiplying food? I find this verse to be so funny. Jesus has not been really that concerned about providing food for a while now. He has continuously provided for them, and I don’t think this situation takes place very long after the feeding of the four thousand. If this reveals anything, it is likely that men are only concerned about one thing… Eating."},{"kind":"para","text":"We love food. Food is our love language. Simple dull creatures we are. They had one loaf, and that was enough for a week if Jesus had anything to do with it. Let’s notice what Jesus’ thoughts are on this though:"}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is contemplating the religious issues of humanity, particularly the traditions of sinful lineages. He is concerned that His disciples are more inclined to follow these traditions rather than seeking a genuine relationship with Him. To convey this message, Jesus uses “leaven” as an analogy, knowing that His disciples are preoccupied with thoughts of food. They are focused on sustenance, while Jesus emphasizes substance. He urges them to be more concerned about the religious lies the world is following than about their next meal."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have discussed leaven in several contexts. Leaven is a tool used to raise bread and is also a symbol of sin. This is why we do not eat leavened bread during communion and why Jews refrain from eating it during Passover. It is connected to God’s salvation, as leaven represents sin. In essence, Jesus is warning, “Beware of the sin of the Pharisees.” He cautions against the substance they are introducing into your faith, as it can rob you of what is good and undermine your relationship with the Lord. We must choose to follow the Lord either by rules or by love; we cannot do both."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When Jesus asks a question, it is never for His own understanding. He asks questions to make us think and to put us in our proper place. These questions are rhetorical. For example, the question of why they were concerned about bread was stating, “You full well know that bread is not an issue.” They don’t perceive or understand yet. Their hearts are “hardened” by their empty stomachs, and not by their empty souls. The Lord is searching the heart of man and trying to fill the soul. Yet we are so concerned about the temporal that we miss the necessary filling of the Lord. Over and over again, we see the disciples make that mistake."}]},{"start":19,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are two very obvious signs of God’s provision. The feeding was not just a miracle; it was a representation. All miracles of Christ are representations of the Lord’s providence and salvation. Jesus never did a miracle that did not lead people to greater faith or even to repentance. He did “all things well,” remember? Everything He did was for a Heavenly and eternal purpose, not just to heal someone and let them go."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are only focused on the Lord healing you and not focused on the Lord’s sacrifice for you, then you will never receive healing from the Lord. He heals those by faith before by health. He is far more concerned about your spiritual health than your physical. We are so spiritually weak, and that is the real concern we should all be taking into consideration."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":26,"title":"The Blind Can See","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn't talk any more about the bread. They continue their journey, and I assume the Lord somehow provided for them. Obviously, He did; otherwise, they wouldn't have made it. There was a blind man here, and I assume that the only reason the Lord came to Bethsaida was to reach this one man. This one man would reach many others. But Jesus doesn't do His miracle right there in the middle of town. He pulls the blind man away:"}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting way to heal this man. \"Lord, I can't see.\" \"Okay,\" Jesus says, preparing a ball of spit. Of course, if we read about this account in the other gospels, we will know that the Lord made mud from His spit; He didn't just spit in the man's face. Again, the Lord could make the man see without even touching Him because faith is the factor."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The man couldn't fully see. I don't know if you have seen men walking or not, but they usually don't look like trees. Obviously, this man has an idea of what trees look like; otherwise, he wouldn't have said that they look like trees."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Why did it take a twice-over? Does this not reflect us all? Does this not give us a very clear and obvious picture of our own faith? We are all walking through this life in spiritual blindness, unwilling to take the healing to see. When Jesus meets with us, He helps us see \"partially.\" He helps us to see, but we don't see things like they actually are. We see the temporal still, but we know that there is a spiritual realm that we cannot see. We will actually never fully see it until we are dead."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For now we see through a glass, darkly; but then face to face: now I know in part; but then shall I know even as also I am known.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we all get to Heaven, we will see very clearly both realms. We will be able to say, \"It was all worth this moment!\" Everything we might go through, all the cloudy vision we are going to face, and uncertainty we will experience will all have a clear view in light of eternity. The Lord is going to reveal everything to us when we get there. We will come into full knowledge, and we will see very clearly what we were missing this entire time. We might ask \"why\" today, but we will be saying \"I understand now\" in Heaven."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, there is something I would have a very hard time keeping my mouth shut about. There would be no way I could just keep quiet about my new sight. I couldn’t see before, but now I see clearly. Take this spiritually for you today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Does it not excite you that you can see today? Does it not bless you to know that the Lord has given you sight? What a great reason to open your mouth about the Lord. If He has given you sight and new life, how can we ever find reason to be silent about it? How could we not share that wealth with those that we claim to love?"},{"kind":"para","text":"This man likely woke up every morning with a smile on his face and a faith that is restored. He woke up and knew what the Lord had done for him because he could actually see around his house and environment. He could see the trees, the sky, the grass, the people, and it likely thrilled him every second. He would likely live the rest of his life in gratitude for his sight and for the Lord providing it."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":30,"title":"Who Is Christ?","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a good question. It's a question brought up in the gospel of Matthew in more depth. It's best saved for that book. But it's a good question for all of humanity to continually ask today. Who does the religious say that Jesus is? What are their associations with Him? How do they perceive Him? Is He their final authority and Savior? Is He absolute? Is He the finished work of salvation? Is He 100% God and 100% man? Who do men say that He is?"}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Men think Christ is simple. He's a tradition. He's a prophet. He's a teacher. He's a good person. He's an idea, a product, an example. There's more depth to Christ than any of these things. There is one thing that Christ is above all else, and that is really what we need to get into the depths of when we are reading scripture, or when we are growing in faith. Christ will ask a new question, and He makes it personal (not just to His disciples but) to all of us."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Who do YOU say that I am?\" Christ is \"I Am.\" Peter gets the answer right and says, \"Well, you are the promised Christ!\" \"Christ\" means \"The Son of God\" or \"Anointed.\" He is the Messiah and Savior of the world! He is not just anointed. He is proven and accepted by the Father."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the gospel of Matthew, Jesus has a slightly longer conversation, which would be best discussed in that gospel account. However, I want to acknowledge that the rest of the conversation seems to be absent. This is not because it isn’t important, but in the eyes of Peter, what Jesus said next was obvious because of what He said before."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Catholic Church views Peter as their first pope. If he is, and they rely on the account written in Matthew as the standard to measure his papacy, and we agree that this gospel account is handled by Peter, then why would he omit this valuable information? This further validates the idea that Jesus was talking about Himself being the rock on which He builds His church. There should be no debate about this, honestly, but this just provides more proof that the Catholics have their interpretation of scripture wrong. They should read the Greek to gain further detail. Peter is the little rock, and Christ is the foundation (big rock)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once again, Jesus tells them to refrain from telling people about this, but how could you keep something so grand a secret? The disciples seem to keep somewhat quiet about it, but I think it’s more because they didn’t fully understand yet. They won’t understand until the day He rises from the grave."}]}]},{"start":31,"end":33,"title":"How to Die","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it’s not a secret. In the Gospel of John, we will read that Jesus makes mention of His death and resurrection quite a few times. They seem to misunderstand it every time, though, because even at the cross, they are seen completely confused about what is happening. Peter seems to recognize it at this point, but doesn’t seem to put too much stake into it. This is when Peter makes a rash decision to keep the Lord from talking such nonsense (or at least Peter thinks it’s nonsense)."},{"kind":"para","text":"May it just be a note, but Jesus is not saying these things because He is expecting it to happen. He’s saying these things because He knows it will happen. This is how it has to play out (even if Peter doesn’t like it)."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was a little bit more that was said here than Mark wrote down. Peter didn't want to get into the depths of what was said, but again we have Matthew writing down the details."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Peter took him, and began to rebuke him, saying, Be it far from thee, Lord: this shall not be unto thee.","ref":"Matthew 16:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a mistake for Peter to say. He says, “Lord, this won’t happen. I won’t let it happen.” Little did he understand that it needed to happen. If the cross of Christ does not take place, all of humanity will be forever lost in their sins."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s funny because Peter says that Jesus “looked on his disciples,” but Matthew says, “But he turned, and said unto Peter…” It was only Peter getting the rebuking here. It was only Peter that would be corrected because it was only Peter that said what he said. This was a harsh comeback for Jesus to give to Peter. He referred to Peter as Satan. This is something that Satan would have said to Jesus. Satan would try to stop the salvation of the world at all costs. Satan wants company in Hell."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t think Peter was prepared for this reaction from the Lord. I can almost guarantee that it hurt Peter’s feelings. It hurts my feelings a bit if I could be honest. I do understand that the Lord was very serious about the matter of His death. There was nothing that must stop it. I don’t believe the Lord said this to try and hurt Peter either. He said it to address the seriousness of this promise. Peter was missing the messages this entire time since the start of this gospel account. He was only attracted to Jesus’ actions. In this particular example, we see that it is still Peter’s main focus. His concern was on the death of Christ. It was not on why Christ would die, but on how Christ would die."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think most religions are focused on this too. They are focused on the gruesomeness of Christ’s death rather than the reason for it. That is why Jesus had just got through asking them, “Who do YOU say that I am?” If they believed that He is the Christ, they would believe that there was a reason for His death to be this way. They didn’t ask though. I don’t think they truly believed that Christ would die. I think they believed He would be with them forever. They believed He was going to start some sort of revolt to give them freedom. They didn’t realize that they would only get freedom through His death."}]}]},{"start":34,"end":38,"title":"A Bid For the Soul","entries":[{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This might seem kind of insulting to Peter. Right after Jesus had just finished calling Peter Satan and rebuking him, He tells everyone that they will not be able to follow Him unless they “deny” themselves and pick up their cross to follow Him. That is what Peter must do. However, that is not what Peter will do. He will not pick up the cross to follow Christ. None of them will. They will all run away from the Lord in fear. It is when He resurrects that they become bold enough to pick up their cross."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the sign of genuine salvation. We do not pick up our cross to receive salvation. We pick up our cross after we are saved and accepted in Him. We carry our own cross, but we have the help of the Lord to carry it as well."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It sounds counterproductive. Saving your life would usually mean that you have… well… saved your life. But not to the Lord. If you want to gain anything for eternity, you must give up everything that is temporal."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are not concerned about gaining for the life to come, then your rewards will be here, and there will be nothing but pain and suffering for the next life. I’d rather take the suffering here and peace for eternity than to take the peace here and suffering for eternity."}]},{"start":36,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is a bid out for your soul. Rowland Hill was once preaching in the open air to a vast audience. Lady Anne Erskine, who was the Countess of Rothes, was riding by and asked who it was that was addressing the vast assembly. She was told that it was the celebrated Rowland Hill. She said, “I have heard of him; drive me near the platform, that I may listen to him.” The eye of Rowland Hill rested on her. He saw that she belonged to the aristocracy and, turning to someone, inquired who she was. He went on preaching, and all at once he stopped. “My friends,” he said, “I have got something here for sale.” Everybody was startled to think that a minister was going to sell something in his sermon. “I am going to sell it by auction, and it is worth more than the crown of all Europe; it is the soul of Lady Anne Erskine. Will anyone bid for her soul? Hark! Methinks I hear a bid. Who bids? Satan bids. What will you give? He will give riches, honor, and pleasure; yea, he will give the whole world for her soul. Hark! I hear another bid for this soul. Who bids? The Lord Jesus Christ. Jesus, what will you give for this soul? He will give you peace, and joy, and comfort that the world knows not of; yea, He will give eternal life for her soul.” Turning to Lady Anne Erskine, he said, “You have heard the two bidders for your soul, which shall have it?” She ordered the footman to open the door, and pushing her way through the crowd, she said, “The Lord Jesus shall have my soul, if He will accept it.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"What a great representation of the value of your soul. How valuable is your soul? Satan has a lot to bid for it. He has a lot of enticing riches to give for it. You can have this whole world for that soul. Or you can have eternity if you give your soul to Jesus Christ. There is nothing more important in this life than to sell your soul to the right bidder."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You can sell your soul to sin. That is selling it to Satan. It will give you riches today. But it will never leave you satisfied. You can sell it, and you will still be short-handed. There is nothing of real value in this world. It's all fool’s gold compared to what Christ can offer. I cannot fathom standing before the Lord in judgement with Christ’s head hanging low in shame over my soul. He lost the bid, and you lost eternity. There is nothing more sickening and sad than to know that the Lord has lost your soul."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":38}],"verses":{"1":"In those days the multitude being very great, and having nothing to eat, Jesus called his disciples unto him, and saith unto them,","2":"I have compassion on the multitude, because they have now been with me three days, and have nothing to eat:","3":"And if I send them away fasting to their own houses, they will faint by the way: for divers of them came from far.","4":"And his disciples answered him, From whence can a man satisfy these men with bread here in the wilderness?","5":"And he asked them, How many loaves have ye? And they said, Seven.","6":"And he commanded the people to sit down on the ground: and he took the seven loaves, and gave thanks, and brake, and gave to his disciples to set before them; and they did set them before the people.","7":"And they had a few small fishes: and he blessed, and commanded to set them also before them.","8":"So they did eat, and were filled: and they took up of the broken meat that was left seven baskets.","9":"And they that had eaten were about four thousand: and he sent them away.","10":"And straightway he entered into a ship with his disciples, and came into the parts of Dalmanutha.","11":"And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.","12":"And he sighed deeply in his spirit, and saith, Why doth this generation seek after a sign? verily I say unto you, There shall no sign be given unto this generation.","13":"And he left them, and entering into the ship again departed to the other side.","14":"Now the disciples had forgotten to take bread, neither had they in the ship with them more than one loaf.","15":"And he charged them, saying, Take heed, beware of the leaven of the Pharisees, and of the leaven of Herod.","16":"And they reasoned among themselves, saying, It is because we have no bread.","17":"And when Jesus knew it, he saith unto them, Why reason ye, because ye have no bread? perceive ye not yet, neither understand? have ye your heart yet hardened?","18":"Having eyes, see ye not? and having ears, hear ye not? and do ye not remember?","19":"When I brake the five loaves among five thousand, how many baskets full of fragments took ye up? They say unto him, Twelve.","20":"And when the seven among four thousand, how many baskets full of fragments took ye up? And they said, Seven.","21":"And he said unto them, How is it that ye do not understand?","22":"And he cometh to Bethsaida; and they bring a blind man unto him, and besought him to touch him.","23":"And he took the blind man by the hand, and led him out of the town; and when he had spit on his eyes, and put his hands upon him, he asked him if he saw ought.","24":"And he looked up, and said, I see men as trees, walking.","25":"After that he put his hands again upon his eyes, and made him look up: and he was restored, and saw every man clearly.","26":"And he sent him away to his house, saying, Neither go into the town, nor tell it to any in the town.","27":"And Jesus went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Caesarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?","28":"And they answered, John the Baptist; but some say, Elias; and others, One of the prophets.","29":"And he saith unto them, But whom say ye that I am? And Peter answereth and saith unto him, Thou art the Christ.","30":"And he charged them that they should tell no man of him.","31":"And he began to teach them, that the Son of man must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.","32":"And he spake that saying openly. And Peter took him, and began to rebuke him.","33":"But when he had turned about and looked on his disciples, he rebuked Peter, saying, Get thee behind me, Satan: for thou savourest not the things that be of God, but the things that be of men.","34":"And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.","35":"For whosoever will save his life shall lose it; but whosoever shall lose his life for my sake and the gospel's, the same shall save it.","36":"For what shall it profit a man, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul?","37":"Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?","38":"Whosoever therefore shall be ashamed of me and of my words in this adulterous and sinful generation; of him also shall the Son of man be ashamed, when he cometh in the glory of his Father with the holy angels."}},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 09"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we got a look at a few more miracles, including the feed of four thousand more people, healing blind people so they can see, the importance of your souls matter (something that Jesus will remind His people about continuously), who Christ really is, and the importance of God's Word over supernatural signs."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Jesus will give us a very important reminder of God's kingdom. We are patiently enduring this Earth until God's Power is revealed to us, but we should remember that His power has already been revealed, and we are able to see and obtain it even still today. We will see some pretty amazing miracles still, and a continual reminder that Jesus is going to sacrifice Himself and rise again from the dead."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we explored several miracles, including the feeding of four thousand more people, healing blind individuals so they could see, and the importance of our souls, a message Jesus will repeatedly remind His followers of. We also delved into who Christ truly is and the significance of God’s Word over supernatural signs. In this chapter, Jesus provides us with a crucial reminder of God’s kingdom. We are patiently enduring life on Earth until God’s power is fully revealed to us. However, it is important to remember that His power has already been revealed, and we can still see and obtain it today. We will witness some remarkable miracles and receive a continual reminder that Jesus will sacrifice Himself and rise again from the dead."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"God's Kingdom","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Oh! The atheists are screaming about this verse! They claim Jesus is a hypocrite because all these people have died. He says they won’t taste death until they have seen the kingdom of God come with power, and yet God’s kingdom still hasn’t come. I could argue a few things here."},{"kind":"para","text":"The first argument I would like to make is that a Christian, when they have passed from this life, doesn’t eternally die. We know that those without Christ suffer a “second death.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And death and hell were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death.","ref":"Revelation 20:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"We also know that when a Christian dies (physically) he lives forever in Heaven (Spiritually)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.","ref":"Romans 6:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"The argument can be made that Jesus was speaking about eternal death. However, I want to bring the argument back to an earthly understanding. I believe we can still take this statement literally. Jesus was specifically talking about their physical death. There would have been some who might have heard this message being preached who were going to die before God’s kingdom came. However, I think the moment that Jesus rose from the grave was when God’s kingdom was established."},{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew tells us a lot about the kingdom of God. Perhaps we can find a clue about when this kingdom that Jesus is talking about actually comes."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.","ref":"Matthew 6:33"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.","ref":"Matthew 4:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But Jesus said, Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven.","ref":"Matthew 19:14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I will give unto thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.","ref":"Matthew 16:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed are they which are persecuted for righteousness' sake: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.","ref":"Matthew 5:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Not every one that saith unto me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the kingdom of heaven; but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven.","ref":"Matthew 7:21"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And saying, Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.","ref":"Matthew 3:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I could go into how the kingdom of God and the kingdom of Heaven have some specifically different indications, but the application is relevant for both. Christ says His kingdom is “at hand.” If something you want is “at hand,” that does not mean it’s too far to reach. It means it’s right there! It’s in your hand. “At” means a location, and “hand” IS the location. If we look at the other verses speaking about the kingdom of Heaven or kingdom of God, we will see that Jesus was purposely doing things here so that God’s kingdom could be present with mankind TODAY. He was not waiting for a physical death to start presenting the kingdom. He was living in God’s kingdom right here on Earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"There were people against that, but they will not experience God’s kingdom with power. They will reject it and die in their sins. The moment Jesus rises from the dead, the “power” of the kingdom is displayed."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.","ref":"Matthew 28:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"So Jesus is not wrong when He states this claim. We are wrong because we don’t understand His preaching. I also believe that God’s saints will be present with Jesus when He returns to Earth to rule and reign. That’s a topic for a different time. Either way, scripture is pretty clear about the kingdom that Jesus is speaking about here."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":8,"title":"The Transfiguration","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Transfigured\" means \"to change into another form.\" This \"transfiguration\" process made Christ look different. He had a \"heavenly\" glow that radiated from His face. It is the same thing that takes place with Moses after spending time with the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when Moses came down from mount Sinai with the two tables of testimony in Moses' hand, when he came down from the mount, that Moses wist not that the skin of his face shone while he talked with him. And when Aaron and all the children of Israel saw Moses, behold, the skin of his face shone; and they were afraid to come nigh him. And Moses called unto them; and Aaron and all the rulers of the congregation returned unto him: and Moses talked with them. And afterward all the children of Israel came nigh: and he gave them in commandment all that the LORD had spoken with him in mount Sinai. And till Moses had done speaking with them, he put a vail on his face. But when Moses went in before the LORD to speak with him, he took the vail off, until he came out. And he came out, and spake unto the children of Israel that which he was commanded. And the children of Israel saw the face of Moses, that the skin of Moses' face shone: and Moses put the vail upon his face again, until he went in to speak with him.","ref":"Exodus 34:29-35"},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that is why the disciples will have a hard time telling that it’s Jesus after His resurrection. But I’m getting ahead of myself. We will notice that there are three people that Jesus took with Him for this specific event. James, John, and Peter seem to be the ones that Jesus kept in close contact with. Why? Well, I don’t know. Perhaps they were the only ones that truly desired to know more about Christ. Maybe they were the least busy? Maybe Jesus purposely singled them out for a future reason."},{"kind":"para","text":"I could understand why He chose John and Peter. They will become very useful to scripture later on (especially John). The church starts on the work of these two disciples. James becomes a prominent leader (pastor) in the church, but then is beheaded rather quickly. So why James? Why not take Matthew with Him? Or maybe Thomas the doubter? If Thomas struggled so much with doubt, why not take him? I honestly don’t know, and I could just speculate, but the Lord knows why, and He uses these three quite a bit as proofs to some of His greatest miracles."}]},{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a lot to take in. He's in the presence of God. Even Jesus' CLOTHES are shining bright white. Suddenly Elijah and Moses appear and start talking to Jesus. Once again we get a picture of Peter's ADHD. He can't stop and listen to what is going on, and so we will notice that whatever Jesus was conversing with these two about seems to be lost somewhere in the universe:"}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tabernacle\" means \"tent.\" Probably like the one they built for the Ark of God when they were traveling in the wilderness. They wanted to make three. Not specifically for worshipping, but for a place that they could lodge and stay with them. Peter didn't know what else to do with his hands, so he thought it would be a good time to start setting up camp."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a pretty high honor that they get to experience, and Peter feels like he has to be doing something because he is afraid. I don't blame him. I feel the same way when I'm either in an awkward situation, a scary situation, or a depressed situation. I need to move around and do something. Usually something that has nothing to do with the actual topic at hand. I need to think and move and I can't do that sitting and watching. Sometimes, though (and I'm preaching to myself), you have to just sit and watch. I'd say more times than not, you should sit and watch..."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we are busy with our hands, we usually miss what is being said. I think John and James were pretty good at hearing Jesus. I'm sure they had their hangups and difficulties just sitting still. I think this message was meant for the whole world, but the one that needed to follow this command to \"hear\" Jesus was Peter. If there was any remnant of doubt in the mind of Peter that this was truly God's Son, it should have been gone at this moment. Peter knows for sure what God said. Now He just needs to start listening a little closer."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They looked to the sky, heard a voice, and then looked at their surroundings. Jesus was the only one standing there in front of them. They could have thought that they were just seeing things, perhaps what they saw wasn’t real. However, there are four witnesses to it here. Jesus would not lie about it. The other two disciples were brothers, so they could tell if the other one was lying. Peter was really the only odd man out, but the testimony of the other three would lead him to believe what he saw."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is something that will also be tested later on. How? Well, he will use this memory to display the gospel to Mark, who is writing this account! Peter was certainly paying attention at this moment, and he is assured of what he saw."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"title":"Rise From the Dead","entries":[{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They knew what they saw. They were just told to “hear” Jesus from the eternal God themselves. That is quite the privilege. We would all kill to hear God talk today. We want His voice as audible as possible. We want to hear the Heavens shake and thunder from His voice. We will likely never get that, however, until we are with Him in eternity. But they had only one question… what was He talking about “rising from the dead?” We saw that Jesus was not quiet about this from our last chapter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he began to teach them, that the Son of man must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again. And he spake that saying openly. And Peter took him, and began to rebuke him.","ref":"Mark 8:31-32"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now they are starting to question it. “What is that supposed to mean? ‘Rising from the dead’ is there some sort of parable about that?” No. It is literally what Jesus was going to do. The parables were easy stories about what will come to pass, but Jesus was being upfront with His disciples. Obviously, His disciples had a different idea about what Christ was there to do, but Jesus was not there to fulfill their ultimate ideas. Christ was there to save them from their sins. That’s what the prophets were preaching on for so many years, and now it was unfolding right in front of their eyes, and they didn’t understand it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I didn’t understand it either when someone explained it to me. It took quite some time for my simple, unlearned, and ignorant brain to comprehend God’s salvation. I’m thankful it finally clicked, but I was struggling with what it all meant for a very long time. I was, perhaps, like these disciples. I understood what Jesus was saying, but I thought He was talking about something else. How could that be a good thing? How could rising from the dead benefit ME? Well, it actually does benefit us, because if He didn’t rise from the dead, we would need a new Savior. We would need a new High Priest. We would need a new source for eternal comfort, and we would never get it. Praise the Lord this is literal, and not just some sort of parable describing something else."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":13,"title":"Elijah Has Come","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, they were told not to speak about what they saw on the mount of transfiguration, and suddenly they start speaking about Elijah. Maybe this has nothing to do with just seeing Elijah not too long ago. Maybe someone was brushing up on some studying and read that tidbit about Elijah. Maybe James, John, and Peter saw it and thought, “Hey! I saw Elijah! He didn’t clean things up though. He didn’t prepare the way for Christ really, so this doesn’t make much sense… We should question Jesus.” At least they didn’t say anything in front of the other disciples."},{"kind":"para","text":"So here they are, and the answer is probably not what they were expecting. They were thinking there was a bit more from that mount experience than what they had seen, but they don’t know how that fits in with the coming of the Lord. That’s because these are two separate situations."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He came as the spirit of John the Baptist."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I say unto you, That Elias is come already, and they knew him not, but have done unto him whatsoever they listed. Likewise shall also the Son of man suffer of them. Then the disciples understood that he spake unto them of John the Baptist.","ref":"Matthew 17:12-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"John the Baptist paved the way for Christ, and that had already taken place just as it was supposed to. They are where they are today because John the Baptist set them up for it. Praise the Lord for John. They have already killed him; let’s not forget. They had beheaded him. That is not a fun picture for the disciples, and certainly not a fun one for Jesus. He died a martyr’s death. He died for the faith of the gospel. He died doing what he loved, preaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want to die doing what I love. I don’t know if I will. I have seen (with my own eyes and ears) people try to stop me from doing what the Lord has called me to do. People and pastors of the church even attend. I cannot stop what God has commissioned me to do. I love to teach, and all the powerful voices of this world can try to put an end to it, but I will die doing it whether they want me to stop or not."},{"kind":"para","text":"They had just seen Elijah and didn’t understand what had just taken place. If I’m honest, I don’t fully understand it. Why Elijah and Moses? Why not Elijah and Enoch? I suppose it could be argued that Moses was translated as well. But we are certain that Enoch was translated, so why not him? It says that Moses died here on Earth and was buried somewhere that no one knows. Enoch and Elijah were taken before death. Some have speculated that these two (Elijah and Moses) will be the last two witnesses. The last two witnesses will mimic a lot of the preaching style of these two men, so it would certainly make sense. That is certainly not a hill I’m willing to die on, though. But it would place these two characters at this moment here for a greater reason than to just showcase that they are still prominent and useful figures."},{"kind":"para","text":"What I want to know (more than anything else) is how the disciples knew that these two men were Elijah and Moses. They didn’t know what they looked like. How could they know? Were they wearing name badges? Did Jesus tell them? Did they announce themselves when they came? I believe there is some context missing, but it likely would draw away from the main theme of this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is speaking about the kingdom, let’s not forget. That’s how this chapter starts. I think this was a display of the kingdom “at hand.” The kingdom was already here when Jesus came. It’s not established until Christ raises from the dead (that’s why He tells them not to say anything until then). But the Lord’s kingdom was there because Jesus was there. He is the King. He is the King the moment He was born."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Highest: and the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of his father David: And he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever; and of his kingdom there shall be no end.","ref":"Luke 1:32-33"},{"kind":"para","text":"The angel assures Mary that this child will be King forever, and His kingdom will be established forever. There is nothing that will keep Christ from having rule and authority… forever! That is a great promise to us. He was King two thousand years ago when He was born, and He is still King today. He will remain King even at the end of the world, and after into eternity. Christ will always reign as supreme authority over all of creation."},{"kind":"para","text":"Those of us reading this today would do well to remember that. He gets to rule and reign. It is not your Pastor or ministry worker. It’s not the church leaders. It’s not this thing or that thing. No, it’s always going to be Jesus Christ as ruler and King. May we live as if He is reigning over the whole Earth."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":16,"title":"Back to Earth","entries":[{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus came back from this Heavenly picture to see the desperate needs of the people down below. I wish the whole Earth was just this moment of transfiguration. Obviously, that will not be the case until sin and death are done away with for all of eternity. It makes it hard to live a Holy life for the Lord when there is so much pain and suffering here on Earth from Satan and sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are easily distracted from Heaven the moment we enter into the world in our daily lives. The transfiguration seems to be a thing of the past because there are problems to deal with here on Earth. Now there are questions, confusion, and arguing, and Jesus must come back to reality. But the way Jesus does it is by presenting a Heavenly solution to the problems they face."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":29,"title":"Real Power","entries":[{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The main problem was that the disciples were having a hard time getting rid of a demon. The scribes were probably saying, “Isn’t Jesus your teacher? He is supposed to be this hot shot ‘Son of God’ and yet He hasn’t seemed to teach you anything. My son is in pain, and you can’t heal him?” The people were losing their patience while Jesus was gone. They had no Heavenly solution to their Earthly problem. They had no faith. That was the real problem."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was not present with them, and so they lost heart and lost faith, which caused all sorts of division and arguing. Nothing could get done because they were focused on Earthly problems. This is why it’s important that we focus on Heaven."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another great question from the Lord? A faithless generation doesn't have very long with Christ. They won't be with Him forever. Those that have faith have Christ for all of eternity. As long as we have our focus on Christ, we will have our healing and needs. \"Bring him unto me.\" A statement that should ring through our ears in every time of need."}]},{"start":20,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus’ compassion is what brings His salvation. Our faith is what fixes that salvation to us. It’s coming to Jesus and asking for Him personally that gives us access to His compassion. That was what they were lacking. They were lacking the faith to come to Jesus first rather than last."},{"kind":"para","text":"Don’t exhaust all of your other options before coming to Christ. Just come to Him first, and you won’t have to exhaust yourself at all. He will take care of you if you will let Him."}]},{"start":23,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a powerful moment. It could bring a tear to any of our eyes. He desperately wants to help his child and desperately wants to believe that Jesus is the Christ. He wants to see it. He wants to stop struggling with his faith and start trusting that Christ can do all things. Christ was in his presence, and all he had to do was trust in Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The cry of every Christian should be the same as this man today. We think we are so faithful and so strong, but we would crumble at the warfare that is happening behind the scenes. I think the Lord holds a lot of the actual fighting back from your life because He knows it would utterly destroy you if you had to face it. Even without knowing the full extent of the battle, we face some pretty serious troubles in this world that are nearly unbearable. My unbelief is what the Lord needs to work on more than anything else. What I want to see is the “kingdom of God come with power.” However, what I currently see is the kingdom of Satan robbing power. And you know what? We let him."}]},{"start":25,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is power. The people tried to stop him, and the disciples couldn’t cast out this demon. Everything seemed to be falling apart around them quickly. Jesus simply said, “Thou dumb and deaf spirit, I charge thee, come out of him…” That’s all that had to be said. Jesus simply had to insult the demon and tell him to leave. The man fell, and everyone thought he was dead. It was a bit chaotic until the man fell to the ground. Then suddenly… Silent. Everyone thought that the demon had killed the man all of a sudden. But Jesus took him…"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord needs to lift up a man who has fallen. We fall in so many ways. We fall from sin, disappointment, battle, rejection, pressure, pain, heartache, pride, greed, and selfishness. But Jesus can take our hand and lift us up. And when Jesus does it, we are always lifted up."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps the man was actually dead? We fall from death. But the Lord brings us to life when we have Him. He is a life giver, by the way. It is sin that takes your life."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.","ref":"Romans 6:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"God is always giving us life, and we are always rejecting it."}]},{"start":28,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Their first mistake was not coming to the Lord. We had mentioned that already. Jesus could have simply said, “Well, you couldn’t do it because you didn’t come to me. You didn’t have faith.” But Jesus wanted to prepare His disciples for when He would be gone in the flesh. So He says, “You couldn’t do it because sometimes you need to pray and fast for someone.” What does this insinuate? Well, I believe it insinuates the same response honestly."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we pray and fast, it is still that we are going to the Lord for help. Prayer is speaking to God, and fasting is relying on God. When we pray, we are asking in earnestness that the Lord would meet with us and address our needs. We should pray that God would be gracious. We should pray out of a true love for the Lord. I’d say ninety percent of my prayers (specifically lately) have been focused on how much I love the Lord. I haven’t been asking for much. I’ve just been telling God that I love Him. Telling Him that I need Him. I just need His presence. That is sufficient enough. There are times when I need to pray for specific requests, but the prayers are only asked to the Lord when my relationship grows with Him. We pray from a heart of love, not a heart of demanding."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we fast, we are giving everything (including our necessities) over to the Lord, so that we can have His presence alone to guard and guide us. That is what we need. It’s allowing God’s kingdom to have power here on Earth. When we fast, we are bringing His power to our needs. We allow Him to do the work, rather than take it into our own hands. We need God’s presence and power. If this chapter has taught us anything, it should have taught us at least that."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":32,"title":"They Shall Kill Him","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was currently on a mission to do more ministry, and if He had stopped in Galilee, it would have kept Him from where He needed to be the most. Therefore, He kept His presence a secret. It should be well understood that Jesus, due to the Holy Spirit of God, is capable of being everywhere all the time. People don’t even know it, but He has passed right along through them. That in itself is powerful."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He keeps hitting on this fact. It should have gotten the attention of His disciples over and over again. It was a literal thing that He was speaking of. He was preparing His disciples for that moment, but it seems that nothing was going to prepare them to see their friend and Savior hanging on that cross. It was what needed to happen. It was going to happen whether they desired (or believed) it or not. That was the only way the power of God would be manifest to this group of people."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have studied quite a bit through scripture of the times when God has displayed His power. All of those times lead to this moment in history. They all lead to the ultimate sign of power in Jesus’ death and resurrection. The disciples don’t understand any of this though…"}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s funny. They didn’t seem to be afraid to ask any other question. Perhaps they were afraid to ask because He had already said this before a couple of times to them. They didn’t want to repeat the same questions. Or maybe they were afraid to ask because they didn’t want to confront the truth of this matter. They didn’t want to think about Jesus being killed. They were afraid to even address the idea that this was going to happen any time soon. So they just stayed quiet and assumed Jesus knew what He was talking about. They would just deal with it when the time came."}]}]},{"start":33,"end":37,"title":"Who is Greatest?","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that the disciples were not really directing anything towards Jesus anymore. Instead of Jesus waiting for them to come ask Him for the answers, He is going to them and asking them questions. Jesus has made it a point to be open and readily available to His disciples, but they were (for some strange reason) not taking that seriously. What did Jesus do to make the disciples afraid to speak to Him? We can look through this entire account (of nine chapters) or any of the other gospels, and we would find no reason for the disciples to reject this great privilege. In fact, they should be taking everything to Him and talking to Him about it. Yet, they seem to think they are bogging the Lord down by questioning everything. They seem to think that the Lord is not interested in their needs and desires."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let me tell you, the Lord is certainly interested. There are many pastors and “ministers” today who are probably not interested in your questions and hurts. They don’t care about what you feel or what you need. They just want you to serve or fill a seat. That is not the Lord’s Spirit, though. The Lord’s Spirit is to know every person individually. Jesus made it a point to stay in the conversation with His disciples. He was very insistent upon keeping people in the loop and keeping Himself involved. That is an example for all ministers in the church today. We should all keep ourselves involved with other people. If we are not doing that, we are neglecting the actual calling of the Lord. You are no minister if you are not ministering to people."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The thing they were “disputing” about was who would be the “greatest” in Heaven. My daughter has recently been interested in soccer. She has some worries about it, but her biggest worry is not being the best. She wants to try and outplay all of the other players. This is coming from someone who has never played soccer before. These other kids have been playing for quite a few years, and Melody thinks she is going to somehow run circles around them. I hope she becomes a great player. I told her, however, that she must rely on her team. They are doing this together. If she hoards the glory for when the team succeeds, then she will bear the burden for when the team fails."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we put ourselves as the most important, we are then responsible for all the pressure. We have to carry the weight. No one else does. That is the problem with modern culture. It’s the problem with modern leadership and politics. Even in the church, it’s a major problem. The leaders want to take all the glory for themselves. They want to take credit for when things are working out, but when things are falling apart, it’s everyone else’s fault. They suddenly were never a part of the team when things are not working in their favor. That is a red flag for the congregants today. If your Pastor is only willing to take credit for success and reject involvement for failures, then you are not serving a “minister” of the Lord. Perhaps he is a minister… just not a minister of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s what the disciples were about to learn pretty quickly in this moment. They don’t say anything about what they are disputing, so Jesus goes ahead and gives them advice anyway because He knows what they were saying. Jesus just wanted them to realize how silly it was to even think this thought."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is not just a ministry lesson. This is a life lesson. If you want to be first, then you must be a servant. If you want to be respected, then you have to put yourself last. If you want to receive genuine love from the Lord, you must be rejecting yourself and your own pride. If you put yourself first, and you rob the Lord of glory, you will be crushed under it. The prideful will fall."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall.","ref":"Proverbs 16:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s not just fancy speech from a wise man. It is cold, hard, factual evidence from the pages and oracles of history. Show me a prideful man, and I will show you his fall. You will not see a prideful man remain standing. Pride in man is like oil in water; they just don’t mix."},{"kind":"para","text":"Who is this a picture of, though? I believe Jesus is picturing Himself. He is first in Heaven but last on Earth. He has the power of God yet served in the weakness of man. The greatest is Christ. We are nothing compared to Him. The disciples should have seen that when they started comparing themselves to each other. Comparison is the thief of joy."}]},{"start":36,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What does a child represent? Why should we be like children? Children represent innocence, growth, and learning. They embody trust and curiosity, as well as hope and newness. In contrast, adults are often seen as lacking these qualities. When we view adults, we tend to see them as evil, uneducated, untrustworthy, dull, hopeless, and old. We perceive them as past their prime, unable to go back in time and do things over. They had their chance, and this is the result."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, when we have Christ, we are given a second chance. We embrace a “child-like” faith. The moment we receive Christ, we are born again into the faith. We begin this journey as innocent, curious, trusting, hopeful, and new. If children do not represent these qualities to you, then your faith is little to nonexistent. Christ is the greatest both in Heaven and on Earth. To receive Christ, we must do so in our most humble state, as a child of the faith."}]}]},{"start":38,"end":50,"title":"The Greatest","entries":[{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Speaking of childlike faith and who is the greatest, John speaks up about a different incident here. It almost seems as if he was purposely trying to change subjects, but the subject is still the same. John has plenty of power from the Lord and great resources at his disposal, but this particular situation is taken in the wrong way. He assumes that the people who do the Lord’s work must be a disciple with the Lord. Only these twelve, in the eyes of John, have the right to minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are some churches today who believe that it was only the disciples to whom the commission to preach was given. We do not take that mantle. They were given the command, and therefore they are the only ones who have to go into “all the world and preach the gospel.” That is not true. Jesus gives the go-ahead, even here, to the rest of the world to minister and preach in His name. Jesus is the only name to minister in, by the way. He is the only one who gives the authority and power to do so. If we are preaching and ministering in any other name rather than His, we are actually only destroying the lives of people."}]},{"start":39,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If people are ministering for Jesus, it is the same as ministering to Christ. We are the image bearers of Christ. It is US, who people will read."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye are our epistle written in our hearts, known and read of all men:","ref":"2 Corinthians 3:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is Christ who we should be displaying. We don't have this kind of unity in the world today. Granted, there are plenty of religions and churches that are not solid in their doctrine and theology, and so it is nearly impossible to walk with them in affirmative truth and action."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Can two walk together, except they be agreed?","ref":"Amos 3:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, we are going to struggle walking with others who are preaching false doctrine. However, we can walk with others who believe in the sacrifice of the Lord. We can start there. It is Christ that unifies us. If we are not in unity (with anyone, whether it's a religion, a boss, or a minister), it is only because Jesus is not present somewhere. The topic is still the same, though. John might have tried to change the subject, but Jesus will use this thought to further illustrate the importance and innocence of His ministers."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You know what you are viewed as, as a minister of the Lord? You are viewed (to the Lord) as a child. If someone hurts you, or rejects you, or “offends” you, then it would be the same (in the Lord’s eyes) as hurting and ruining the reputation of a child. You would have to be a sick and twisted individual to purposely go out of your way to hurt a child, and yet that is what much of the world does today by mistreating God’s ministers. Jesus said it would be better that you go drown yourself than to even “offend” one of God’s children."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Offend” means “to entice to sin.” Basically, it’s robbing someone of their innocence. That is what our world does today. They take away people’s innocence. They especially take it away from children. Don’t even get me started on the 2024 Paris Olympics…"}]},{"start":43,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have two revelations from Christ here. One is the seriousness of sin. Jesus preached against sin, making it clear that sin is a killer. When it infects something, it will start to take over, much like cancer. It is impossible to get rid of, and so it must be the Lord that heals and removes it. The cutting off of an arm is a powerful picture, but it will not get rid of sin (I’ll talk about that more in a moment)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The second thing that is spoken about here, and needs to be addressed (and will be spoken about a few more times in this chapter), is Hell. Jesus gives us an image of Hell and is seen preaching against it. Hell is a very sad and tortuous place. It sounds like a hot place and also like a place where pain never ends. I believe, based on scripture, that Hell is a place of pain. From the moment you are put there, until… well… forever. Jesus preached against Hell, against sin, and warned people about the dangers and power of it. But the power of God is far greater, and that is what we will need to remember when we are reading about cutting off body parts in these next few verses."}]},{"start":45,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Worm” means “a kind of worm that preys upon dead bodies.” Even the maggots eating your corpse will remain with you. It seems there is nothing you can take with you out of this world but one, and that is the worms devouring your corpse. How do you keep that from happening? Well, cut off your foot! Now, that is interesting, isn’t it? I can understand how an arm can cause you to sin, but… a foot? The arm is (perhaps) a good representation of your actions. It shows that you are able to actively DO sinful things. The foot is probably a representation of your pursuits. It shows that you are able to actively PURSUE sinful things. The last one is a little more dire, I think."}]},{"start":47,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What is more valuable than your sight? Blind people would love to see, yet seeing people often take advantage of their sight to sin. If the arm represents our doing of sin, and the foot represents our pursuit of sin, then our eye must represent our longing for sin. We cannot pursue or do what we cannot see. Yet, it’s with those same parts of the body that we follow the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"You know what’s even crazier? We would run out of body parts before we gave up sin. It would be better to cut these parts off, but it will not work that way. If it did, that would certainly be a way to help. But the body does what the mind tells it to. So the only way to keep yourself from sin is to cut out your brain. I don’t know if you know this, but you will die without a brain. Unless you are a scarecrow, you are not going to last very long. But God has the power to literally forgive sin! We don’t have to cut off anything when we are the children of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.","ref":"1 John 1:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"To him give all the prophets witness, that through his name whosoever believeth in him shall receive remission of sins.","ref":"Acts 10:43"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I, even I, am he that blotteth out thy transgressions for mine own sake, and will not remember thy sins.","ref":"Isaiah 43:25"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"God forbid. How shall we, that are dead to sin, live any longer therein?","ref":"Romans 6:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"So it is only by the salvation of God that we can save all of our body parts as well as our spirits. If cutting off parts of our body saved us, we would be wise to do it. If it was of necessity that we die on a cross to get salvation, it would be wise to do it. If it was the only way to receive salvation by working for it, then it would be wise for us to do it. But that is not how it works. The disciples will learn that soon enough, but they are taking this in very carefully here. They are paying attention to this little child sitting in the arms of Jesus. He is keeping innocence as the focus here. It's not about working for your salvation. It's about killing the sin. The only way to kill sin is to cut it out. The only way to cut it out is to have the \"Great Physician.\" The only way to do that is to be born again."}]},{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s put this into a word picture here. Imagine a salt shaker. You have a delicious piece of steak in front of you, and you are going to add some salt to it with that shaker. You shake it, and little flakes of salt come out. That is proper seasoning; it helps enhance the flavor of the steak. But let’s say that you use the shaker, and fire comes out. Well, that would be an interesting thing to witness, wouldn’t it? Fire will help “prepare” the steak. We are salted by “fire” first. God prepares us for what is to come. He has spent quite a few moments in the past couple of chapters preparing the disciples for His death. That is the fire He is salting them with."},{"kind":"para","text":"Next will be salting with actual salt. He will prepare them, and then He will make them better. When Jesus removes sin, it is not taking flavor away from you. He adds something to your life to enhance you. It’s an additive to preserve and make better! That’s what salt does (whether you like salt or not). That is how God’s power works on the lives of His children."}]},{"start":50,"end":50,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Have you ever had salt that wasn’t salty? It’s a thing! If it has been contaminated, it does not taste like salt. It starts to clump up and get old, and it becomes good for nothing. The only thing you can do is pour the salt out and get new salt."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is constantly salting us with fire and salt. He is always giving us growth points. That is what is being represented here. We do not stay as children. We grow up. We are little in service, child-like in faith, but mature adults in understanding and in stature."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Brethren, be not children in understanding: howbeit in malice be ye children, but in understanding be men.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"We will constantly be seasoned and preserved. Why? Well, it brings peace to each other. The whole point of this conversation is to encourage unity with the believers. It is to lean on each other and move each other forward, not putting yourself ahead of everyone else."},{"kind":"para","text":"Going back to my daughter’s soccer skills, imagine if she learns from those other players on the field. Imagine the kind of impact she can have if they develop teamwork with each other. Imagine how much easier and smoother things can run when she is depending on and promoting others on her team. If they are not against you, they are FOR you."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the lesson we should learn in faith and ministry. We must be helping people move forward ahead of us. A pastor who puts himself in front of his congregants will never be able to unite with the flock. He should be ONE of the sheep, not one of the shepherds. We already have ONE Shepherd, and He can do perfectly fine without your leadership. He is a Good Shepherd. He can lead you AND the rest of the flock perfectly well. It would be wise for us to depend upon that, and while we are depending on that, to move each other forward."},{"kind":"para","text":"What the disciples should have been disputing was who they are going to lead ahead of them. They should have been saying, “Hey, I would love to help you lead. I would love to make you greater.” If they were humbly pushing each other up, it wouldn’t be a “dispute,” it would be an “edification.” Sadly, we have plenty of God’s children pushing their ministers forward, only to get them to the top so they can turn around and push you off the cliff… That’s what pursuing greatness gets you. It gets you to the top on the backs of the humble, and once you are there and have pushed everyone off this cliff, you are left there lonely and empty."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":38},{"start":39,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":44},{"start":45,"end":46},{"start":47,"end":48},{"start":49,"end":49},{"start":50,"end":50}],"verses":{"1":"And he said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That there be some of them that stand here, which shall not taste of death, till they have seen the kingdom of God come with power.","2":"And after six days Jesus taketh with him Peter, and James, and John, and leadeth them up into an high mountain apart by themselves: and he was transfigured before them.","3":"And his raiment became shining, exceeding white as snow; so as no fuller on earth can white them.","4":"And there appeared unto them Elias with Moses: and they were talking with Jesus.","5":"And Peter answered and said to Jesus, Master, it is good for us to be here: and let us make three tabernacles; one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias.","6":"For he wist not what to say; for they were sore afraid.","7":"And there was a cloud that overshadowed them: and a voice came out of the cloud, saying, This is my beloved Son: hear him.","8":"And suddenly, when they had looked round about, they saw no man any more, save Jesus only with themselves.","9":"And as they came down from the mountain, he charged them that they should tell no man what things they had seen, till the Son of man were risen from the dead.","10":"And they kept that saying with themselves, questioning one with another what the rising from the dead should mean.","11":"And they asked him, saying, Why say the scribes that Elias must first come?","12":"And he answered and told them, Elias verily cometh first, and restoreth all things; and how it is written of the Son of man, that he must suffer many things, and be set at nought.","13":"But I say unto you, That Elias is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.","14":"And when he came to his disciples, he saw a great multitude about them, and the scribes questioning with them.","15":"And straightway all the people, when they beheld him, were greatly amazed, and running to him saluted him.","16":"And he asked the scribes, What question ye with them?","17":"And one of the multitude answered and said, Master, I have brought unto thee my son, which hath a dumb spirit;","18":"And wheresoever he taketh him, he teareth him: and he foameth, and gnasheth with his teeth, and pineth away: and I spake to thy disciples that they should cast him out; and they could not.","19":"He answereth him, and saith, O faithless generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him unto me.","20":"And they brought him unto him: and when he saw him, straightway the spirit tare him; and he fell on the ground, and wallowed foaming.","21":"And he asked his father, How long is it ago since this came unto him? And he said, Of a child.","22":"And ofttimes it hath cast him into the fire, and into the waters, to destroy him: but if thou canst do any thing, have compassion on us, and help us.","23":"Jesus said unto him, If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth.","24":"And straightway the father of the child cried out, and said with tears, Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief.","25":"When Jesus saw that the people came running together, he rebuked the foul spirit, saying unto him, Thou dumb and deaf spirit, I charge thee, come out of him, and enter no more into him.","26":"And the spirit cried, and rent him sore, and came out of him: and he was as one dead; insomuch that many said, He is dead.","27":"But Jesus took him by the hand, and lifted him up; and he arose.","28":"And when he was come into the house, his disciples asked him privately, Why could not we cast him out?","29":"And he said unto them, This kind can come forth by nothing, but by prayer and fasting.","30":"And they departed thence, and passed through Galilee; and he would not that any man should know it.","31":"For he taught his disciples, and said unto them, The Son of man is delivered into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise the third day.","32":"But they understood not that saying, and were afraid to ask him.","33":"And he came to Capernaum: and being in the house he asked them, What was it that ye disputed among yourselves by the way?","34":"But they held their peace: for by the way they had disputed among themselves, who should be the greatest.","35":"And he sat down, and called the twelve, and saith unto them, If any man desire to be first, the same shall be last of all, and servant of all.","36":"And he took a child, and set him in the midst of them: and when he had taken him in his arms, he said unto them,","37":"Whosoever shall receive one of such children in my name, receiveth me: and whosoever shall receive me, receiveth not me, but him that sent me.","38":"And John answered him, saying, Master, we saw one casting out devils in thy name, and he followeth not us: and we forbad him, because he followeth not us.","39":"But Jesus said, Forbid him not: for there is no man which shall do a miracle in my name, that can lightly speak evil of me.","40":"For he that is not against us is on our part.","41":"For whosoever shall give you a cup of water to drink in my name, because ye belong to Christ, verily I say unto you, he shall not lose his reward.","42":"And whosoever shall offend one of these little ones that believe in me, it is better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and he were cast into the sea.","43":"And if thy hand offend thee, cut it off: it is better for thee to enter into life maimed, than having two hands to go into hell, into the fire that never shall be quenched:","44":"Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched.","45":"And if thy foot offend thee, cut it off: it is better for thee to enter halt into life, than having two feet to be cast into hell, into the fire that never shall be quenched:","46":"Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched.","47":"And if thine eye offend thee, pluck it out: it is better for thee to enter into the kingdom of God with one eye, than having two eyes to be cast into hell fire:","48":"Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched.","49":"For every one shall be salted with fire, and every sacrifice shall be salted with salt.","50":"Salt is good: but if the salt have lost his saltness, wherewith will ye season it? Have salt in yourselves, and have peace one with another."}},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Jesus opened up about the power of the kingdom of God. He demonstrated how that power was present on Earth, and gave plenty of proof for the future generations to behold that same power. We get to see that power in the world today. We actually get to be a part of using that power to encourage and serve others! We saw the transfiguration of Jesus, more warnings about Jesus' death, and the disciples arguing over who the greatest in the kingdom will be. He used a child as a great illustration of who is in power in God's kingdom."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Jesus continues with that illustration. He teaches His followers some very valuable and important lessons, and continues to prepare them for His coming death. It seems they are all still hung up on who is going to be the greatest in the eyes of Jesus, and Jesus is going to remind them of what that really means, and what that actually looks like."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Jesus opened up about the power of the kingdom of God. He demonstrated how that power was present on Earth, and gave plenty of proof for the future generations to behold that same power. We get to see that power in the world today. We actually get to be a part of using that power to encourage and serve others! We saw the transfiguration of Jesus, more warnings about Jesus' death, and the disciples arguing over who the greatest in the kingdom will be. He used a child as a great illustration of who is in power in God's kingdom. In this chapter, Jesus continues with that illustration. He teaches His followers some very valuable and important lessons, and continues to prepare them for His coming death. It seems they are all still hung up on who is going to be the greatest in the eyes of Jesus, and Jesus is going to remind them of what that really means, and what that actually looks like."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Wont To Teach","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was very specific and particular about His steps here. He has a reason for each movement He makes, each word He says, and the direction He’s going. He’s getting rather close to the death He has spoken about a few times already. He wants to prepare people even still. He has more to teach, and He wants to get what He can out there before the time comes when He is placed on that cross."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus loves to teach. It’s His passion. That is a hard thing for people to have passion in, in our era today. We have plenty of room to talk about that in coming books of the Bible, but teaching is something we should take very seriously. The people who need the most encouragement are those who love teaching. Those are, sadly, the ones that get neglected the most, however."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we should recognize that Jesus was not “preaching” to them. He is teaching. There’s a very stark difference between the two. We should know the difference. One is for changing your motives, the other is for digging deeper into those motives. One is for preparing your heart, the other is for growing your heart. One is for making corrections, the other is for knowing why we must be corrected. One is for planting seeds, the other is for watering them. Preachers and teachers should be walking together, arm in arm, but that is not a very common theme in the church today. We will see in the letter to the church at Corinth that they had a problem with this as well. But we can speak about that in 1 Corinthians."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":12,"title":"Getting Divorced","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is all the religionists are good for. They are good for trying to hurt the reputation of Jesus. That's what they wanted to do back then, and it's still what they want to do today. They want to try and back Jesus into a corner so that He makes a mistake in His answers. But we will notice that every question (no matter how difficult it might be) has been answered with absolute fact. The religionists can throw every sort of question and accusation they want at the Lord, but because He has all knowledge, it would not be difficult for Him to give a just and righteous answer. None of the Pharisees could make anything stick to the Lord. They were not as smart as they pretended to be."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great way to play into their religion. If you are questioned about a Biblical fact or religious assumption, go straight to their own religious standards. If we measured the congregants up to their own religious practices, we would find really quickly that they fall short of their own practices. They can't even keep up with their own works righteousness, so there is no way they will be able to keep up with scriptural works. He points them to Moses. They knew the law of Moses better than they knew the love of God. That is still the issue with religions today. We are so in-tune with God's law and judgement that we have lost God's love and presence."}]},{"start":4,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You know why divorce exists today? Because we have stubborn, rebellious, and hard hearts. We don't know what love is. Love is more than just marrying someone to be with them. That is something we will address over and over again through other parts of Scripture. The Apostles expound on true love. Peter will talk about it;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And above all things have fervent charity among yourselves: for charity shall cover the multitude of sins.","ref":"1 Peter 4:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul will talk about it;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And now abideth faith, hope, charity, these three; but the greatest of these is charity.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"John will talk about it;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Beloved, let us love one another: for love is of God; and every one that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God.","ref":"1 John 4:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously it is something that Jesus speaks about quite a bit;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If ye love me, keep my commandments.","ref":"John 14:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't have the church, though. Jesus knew what marriage represented, and it still had yet to exist. The ultimate example of marriage is the relationship that the church will have with the Lord. When the church is established (in the book of Acts), we will see the ultimate representation of marriage."}]},{"start":6,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s interesting that the number one debated topic of our generation is what a “male and female” are. We don’t know how to define those genders. Everything is on a spectrum now. We have over-complicated something so very specific and simple. We can no longer stand on affirmative absolutes because the world thinks that nothing is serious, yet they take that seriously. If you don’t agree with them on an issue (especially about gender and marriage), then you ARE an issue."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was very matter of fact about this. God MEANT for men to be men, and women to be women. And God MEANT for men to marry women. He MEANT it for replenishing the Earth. He MEANT it for continual generations. Even the people who believe in evolution must agree that there is value in human genders. Why? Well, if we are evolved from animals, we have evolved into very specific gender species. We could have evolved into a “spectrum” of genders, but we did not. We have a specific assigned gender, and the only way to (so-called) “evolve” into a different gender is through surgery. Even then, we are not evolved into it. We are just pretending. That is what evolution is anyway."},{"kind":"para","text":"Furthermore, the human race is the only one on this entire planet who has not become extinct. Evolutionists strongly agree that animals and dinosaurs have become extinct. They mostly agree that fish and other animals will become extinct over time as well. But they cannot agree that human beings will become extinct. They never will either because we know that humans are a superior form of creation. God made them that way. That is what He made marriage into as well. If marriage is going to be sacred and solidified, it must be done under God. Otherwise, the hardness of our hearts will put the marriage into turmoil, and it will end in shambles."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The reason that divorce exists is because human beings don't love correctly. If the marriage is fixed upon your own strength, then divorce is inevitable. If it's fixed on the strength of God, then there should be nothing that can break that marriage apart (aside from death, which belongs to the Lord)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"See now that I, even I, am he, And there is no god with me: I kill, and I make alive; I wound, and I heal: Neither is there any that can deliver out of my hand.","ref":"Deuteronomy 32:39"},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse can literally connect to anything the Lord has provided an individual. If you are joined to ministry, it should not be possible for man to put that asunder. If the Lord has joined you to salvation, it should not be possible for man to put that asunder. If the Lord has joined you to being a parent, it should not be possible for man to put that asunder. If the Lord has joined you to a friendship, it should not be possible for man to put that asunder. If the Lord has joined you to a marriage, it should not be possible for man to put that asunder. But if it is man that joined you, there is nothing keeping it together."}]},{"start":10,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Divorce is adultery. I'm not saying this from opinion; I'm saying this from biblical conviction. God's Word says it, and therefore it is the truth. Whether you want to accept it as the truth or not is between you and the Lord, but that is what His Word says. We should accept the truth at face value. It's okay if that is the lifestyle you have led. The Lord has forgiveness for you still. It's a sin like any other sin, and it must be something that the Lord works on in your life. Praise the Lord He forgives us. Praise the Lord He gives us more than one chance to live right before Him. Otherwise, we would have to be cutting off limbs, as was demonstrated in the last chapter."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"The Children's Kingdom","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus has had numerous occasions of healing the sick and the possessed. He has touched the lives of many, and healed countless. Yet, the disciples think NOW is a good time (for some reason) to stop that from happening. When did Jesus ever say that He didn't want that to happen anymore? There are certain instances when Jesus had been staying on the down low, but when He was found out and there was a need that needed to be met, He was there to meet that need. He never rejected people. Furthermore, He spoke very prominently about not rejecting children, and that is literally what the disciples are doing right here. This was a very dumb mistake..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus could have said, “Do you not remember our conversation not that long ago? How could you reject children from coming to be healed? Did you not understand when I said you would be better off tying a millstone around your neck and drowning yourself in the ocean than for you to offend these children?” Maybe they were not paying attention. I mean, obviously, one of them had to be paying attention in order for Peter to give the account in our last chapter. I guess they just thought that Jesus didn’t really mean what He said before?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead of reminding them of what was said, however, Jesus just repeats Himself. That is the majority of scripture, by the way. Scripture is just a gradual building of repeated and kept promises by the Lord. God repeats Himself over and over again, and He has done that for literally thousands of years, and yet we still get confused and forgetful. That is why it’s important that we continuously read our Bibles. We need the constant reminders from the Lord about the necessity to love Him and obey Him."}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The children are a better example than the adults in this matter of salvation and trusting. The Lord has made it clear that the children are setting the example. If the children cannot do that, then the adults are doomed to failure. If you cannot come to the Lord as a child, you cannot enter the kingdom of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, the power of God’s kingdom was talked about in our last chapter. You know what that power is? Perhaps we didn’t mention it. We might think of power as some sort of physical strength and exertion. It is, in a sense. But the power of God is actually something far bigger and better than physical strength. It is spiritual salvation. That is the power of God. It’s given to the children of God. If you are not a child of the Lord, you cannot have the power of God’s kingdom. It’s not possible. You must receive His kingdom as a “little child.” That’s why being born again is such an important topic to discuss. It’s why we must come to Him in believing. We must relearn our life and relationship with the Lord."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":22,"title":"Good Master","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This young man did what is right here. He kneeled before the Lord and referenced Him as “good,” a title that belongs to the Lord alone. He asked Him a very important question, one that should be on the minds of all who do not know Him personally. However, he answered his own question within the question itself. He must personally know the “Good Master.” We will see that in a moment by Jesus’ response to him."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s address the young man’s first statement. He called Him “Good Master.” Jesus does not say that He is NOT a Good Master, but He is confronting this young man’s assumption of Jesus being “Good” and a “Master.” This is something that the young man will have to consider when Jesus tells him this next part."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus IS a Good Master, but why is this young man calling Him that? Does this young man think of Jesus as someone that is Good, or a Master? I highly doubt it. In fact, I know this young ruler doesn’t see Him like this because of how this conversation ends, but I’m getting ahead of myself. But we must keep this in mind for the sake of the responses in the next couple of verses."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus knows this kid is smart. He says, “Hey, you know what the commandments are: don’t commit adultery, don’t kill, don’t steal, don’t lie, defraud not, and honor your parents.” Let’s pause for a moment. “Defraud not” is not a part of the Moral Law. If we read through the Ten Commandments in Exodus 20, we will not see the command to “Defraud not.” Why did Jesus throw that in there? Well, it’s a draw to the remaining commands of scripture (not coveting, not dishonoring those around you, or abusing them)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice the commandments that Jesus focused on have nothing to do specifically with serving the Lord. He chose the commands that focus on serving others. My bet is placed on the fact that Jesus knew that this young man is not good. Even if he was a perfect scholar of God’s Word, he likely mistreated the people around him. We can know that he is a bad guy by the lie he tells next."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Matthew has a little bit of a different account of the words here."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The young man saith unto him, All these things have I kept from my youth up: what lack I yet?","ref":"Matthew 19:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter says that the young man has “observed” these things. He has “looked” into them rather than keeping them. Matthew, however, states that the young man said he has “kept” them. Which is it then? I don’t know. Perhaps the word for “observed” and the word for “kept” are interchangeable in the Greek. Scholars argue over which language Matthew was written in. They believe it was written in Hebrew (Aramaic) and then translated to Greek later on. So perhaps the translation gets a little convoluted in a couple of places because it’s like a game of telephone."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that Mark’s account is a little more accurate in the translation here. That’s just my personal opinion. We can study some translational flaws in Matthew later on. Either way, we can know for sure that this young man is lying, and the Lord is not the author of confusion."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If this young man lied, Jesus didn’t hate him for it. It says, in the midst of a lie, Jesus “loved him.” He took what the young man said and wanted to test him. If this young man was honest in his keeping (or observing) of these commandments towards his neighbor, then it shouldn’t be a problem for him to remain in that claim. He should have no problem giving everything he has to his poor neighbors if he has truly kept the commandments like he says. But we know he was lying because the next verse proves it."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He was a rich boy. I’ve said it over and over again. If you are rich, you likely got there by climbing on innocent people. That’s just historically proven over and over again. Look at the rich still today and see how they got where they are. They abused someone’s kindness somewhere. If this young man truly believed that he kept the commandments, he would keep the commandments still to follow Jesus. If he truly believed that Jesus was a “Good Master” (like he started this conversation with), then he would have had no problem following his master’s advice. He spoke two lies in two sentences. I’m not even sure if he actually, and truly, believed there would even be an “eternal life.” Perhaps he was like the Pharisees, trying to tempt Him. I guarantee this young man was not nearly as sad as Jesus was at the rejection of faith here."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord was not requesting anything overly astounding from this young man. The reason Jesus points this particular young man out is because of his actual heart’s desire. We will notice that there are two commandments that Jesus continually preaches on quite a bit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Master, which is the great commandment in the law? Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind. This is the first and great commandment. And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets.","ref":"Matthew 22:36-40"},{"kind":"para","text":"These two commandments are emphasized, even to this rich young ruler. He points out that you must love the Lord and then you must love others. That is what brings ultimate fulfillment. We will recognize that there is a twofold commission here. I know of many preachers and pastors who emphasize the love of God. They say that all you must do is love the Lord. That should be your focus. Yet the Lord continuously brings up loving others as well. He wants us to love God first, yes, but we must also love others. It’s about being in unity with God and community with the children of God. This young man probably loved the Lord with all his heart. I have no doubt about that. However, he didn’t have the proper love for others."},{"kind":"para","text":"We really must evaluate ourselves here. We must look at how much we love God. We, perhaps, have an astounding love for the Lord that surpasses any love that we might have for anything else here on Earth. Or, at least we think we do. We will say that we do for a second; how does that love look in your life? Loving God does not mean we just separate ourselves from the church. No… We must love God and allow that love to bring us into connection with others who love the Lord as well. Community is a very important prospect. I think this young man wanted to follow the Lord, he just didn’t want to love others. That was what he was lacking. That is what many church leaders are lacking sadly…"}]}]},{"start":23,"end":27,"title":"The Rich Are Lost","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will discuss the problem with the rich later, but it’s important to note it here. This young man turned away from the riches of eternity because they would not be the riches of his present life. He wants his best life NOW, not his best life in eternity. You can either take one or the other. Do you want the best treasures and rewards here, and an eternity of pain and void, or do you want your treasures and rewards in eternity, and a present of pain and trials? If you get your reward here, you will rob yourself of eternal treasures. That is God’s kingdom rule. Longsuffering is a fruit of the Spirit."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He spoke to them in the last verse as disciples. He is speaking to them in this verse as children. May we not forget that the kingdom of God is on Earth today. It was on Earth the moment Jesus was born, remember? He is addressing kingdom children at the moment. These children are all poor, mind you. The only one that had riches at this moment in time was likely Matthew. But even then, I don't think he was making much money following the Lord. They are all living well below their means, and yet they are surviving by the Lord's providence. The little children need to understand this here. They are astonished that Christ would say that the rich are not the prime example of royalty in the kingdom. It's the CHILDREN who are royalty."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I can hardly put a piece of thread through the eye of a needle. I don’t know if you’ve done it before, but it is quite difficult. Now, imagine trying to put an entire adult camel through that same needle! Yet, Jesus says it would be easier to do that than for a rich man to be enticed by eternal riches. Rich people don’t want to sell out for the Lord. They want to keep their treasures here. They think a life of riches is worth an eternity of suffering. They fail to realize that eternity lasts longer. Perhaps many of them think they will just get right with the Lord on their deathbeds. That’s when they can turn to God and be forgiven. The truth is, they will be forgiven if they truly turn to the Lord at that point. But what if they never get to their deathbeds? What if they die in a car accident?"},{"kind":"para","text":"You certainly can’t ask the judge for forgiveness while you are in the courtroom. You must be innocent before then. There must be proof of your innocence if you are looking to avoid serving time. When you are in front of the judge of the universe, you will not just ask for forgiveness then. He will not forgive you. You had your chance here. A rich person will not take that chance because they think they can just pay off the judge. The judge doesn’t want anything from you, though. There is no posting bail. You are sealing your fate for all of eternity by rejecting the Lord today. God owns the whole universe, so there is nothing you can give Him to pay Him off and let you go away with your sins. You must be sinless. There will be more conversation about that in the future letters (specifically 1, 2, & 3 John). The only way you can be sinless, though, is if you have the Lord as your Savior. He literally forgives and forgets your sins."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And their sins and iniquities will I remember no more.","ref":"Hebrews 10:17"}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Did the disciples think that they were going to be saved by their riches? They were already off to a bad start if that is what they thought. None of them were rich enough. How much do you think it would cost to pay off your sin debt? The world could not contain the amount of money it would cost. The entire universe could not contain it. That is just for YOUR debt, let alone the debt of every single human on planet Earth. That is why we cannot pay our way to Heaven. We will never be rich enough, even if we are the richest man on Earth."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Guess what? It’s possible to save a rich man. It’s nearly impossible, but not impossible. They can turn to the Lord. They just have to give their lives to Him. That is what the problem was with the rich young ruler. He didn’t want to give his life to the Good Master. He didn’t truly trust and believe in who He is. He trusted his riches, and that was good enough for him for now."},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine that later on in his life, he was sitting on his deathbed and thought, “Was it worth it? Was all of this time spent getting material wealth worth giving up following Christ? Can I turn to Him now? Is it too late? Will He take me?” Hardly ever is any pursuit on this Earth worth the work. 100% of the time, working for the Lord is never wasted. I’ve never even done a failed work for the Lord that I thought was a waste of time. I would do it again, even though I failed, because I know it impacted lives, and I know that it pleased Him. To the world, it failed. To the Lord, it was a grand success."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":31,"title":"Left It All","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This version of what Peter said is cleaned up a little bit. If this was all he said, it was somewhat right. They had left everything and followed Jesus. They were in the middle of work (most of them), and they left their jobs to follow Christ. But Matthew gives us a little bit of depth to Peter's acclamation."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then answered Peter and said unto him, Behold, we have forsaken all, and followed thee; what shall we have therefore?","ref":"Matthew 19:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter asked a question, apparently. It was not just a statement. He says, \"We left everything. What are we going to get because of it?\" It's a selfish question. It's refusing to address the pride from the previous statements about who would be the greatest. You know what the people get? They get Christ. But Jesus is going to answer the question in the most truthful way possible."}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s funny. He says, “Rich men will hardly go to Heaven.” Now He says, “If you give up everything to follow me, you will be rich!” This had to be throwing a curveball to Peter and the disciples. They had to be very confused at this point. It is God who makes you rich. Any other way you get your riches is an abuse to mankind. That’s just a fact. If you want the Lord to bless you, be the last in line. If you want God to bring you up, lay yourself down. If you want God to save you, give up your life. If you want to be rich, give all you have. The kingdom of God works opposite of the kingdom of man. If the disciples were honest with themselves, they would know they hadn’t fully given up everything to follow the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is not expecting someone like Peter to completely neglect and reject his wife to follow Christ. That is not what is being said. Jesus says, “No one is going to give up everything in this world for following me. They don’t truly believe me. They don’t love me like they know they should. They call me Good Master, yet don’t believe I am good, and don’t look at me as their master.” That’s the case with all the disciples, and it’s the case with us still today. I love my wife. But do I love the Lord enough to give her up for Him? If my wife was trying to draw me away from the Lord, then it would be wise of me to follow Him and give her up rather than to follow her and give Him up. There are some families who are faced with this situation. It’s not a fun ultimatum to be in. The Lord should have both spouses’ hearts. That’s how the marriage will work. If not, then one spouse will follow after who (or what) they truly love, and the other will follow something else. They will let man divide the marriage."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":34,"title":"Amazed, and Afraid","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"They were amazed... they were afraid...\" They were emotional, that’s what they were. They can't seem to figure out how to process the actions and words of Jesus. They don't connect the two as being equal."}]},{"start":33,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Notice that every time Jesus references His death, He also brings up His resurrection. He is trying to prepare the disciples for what is to come, but they just aren't understanding it. You know who the disciples are focused on? They are focused on themselves. They are not aware that they are about to lose the Lord soon. This will be their last week with Jesus, and they have no idea what is going to come their way by the end of it. Every time Jesus brings up His death and resurrection, they turn the story back to themselves and their own greatness:"}]}]},{"start":35,"end":45,"title":"A Servant to All","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a weird thing to request from the Lord. Jesus was kind to His disciples, so He is willing to do whatever they desire if it brings holiness to them. Jesus was not willing to sin for the disciples, but He was certainly willing to serve and give everything He could for them. He will be literally giving His life as He said a moment ago, but they seem to be ignoring that for some reason."}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What can Jesus do for you? Questions that Jesus has in Scripture are questions that the world can answer. Jesus has given you His literal life. What more could you want from Him? Well, the disciples want a little more than what He is going to give, and it's an eternal focus:"}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I will say, this is a remarkable request. But it's drenched in pride. They are STILL focused on who can be the greatest. The spots that these brothers are asking for are spots of reverence. They are spots of power. They know that these are spots to be taken by someone that the Lord deems the most worthy. The other disciples were probably not thrilled about these brothers asking this. In fact, I know the other disciples didn't like it, because we are going to read that in verse 41."}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a big thing for the disciples to ask. Jesus has told them a few times now that He is about to die a very painful death, and they want to follow Him into that? They would have to face some great hardship. Greater than they even understand or know how to deal with. They are not worthy to drink from the Lord's cup, but they will be alongside the Lord as if they were worthy. The Lord makes everyone worthy of His salvation at the cross. Those that reject it are unworthy, obviously."}]},{"start":39,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are going to go through some fire, for sure. James will be killed once the first church is established. It will almost be Peter next, but the Lord rescues Peter in a miraculous way. John lives to be an old man. He is the only one of the twelve that actually lives a full life. All the other ones die by being killed. John will still go through some immense trials and will be exiled for the sake of the faith. He and James are certainly going to suffer many things for the sake of the Lord."}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everything was a competition to them. It was all about who can get the closest to Jesus. That is much of the church today. It's all a competition in the house of the Lord. Who can do the most? Who can have the most ministries? Who can teach the most lessons? Who can lead the most people? Who can have the bigger class? Who can write the most addresses? Who can knock the most doors? I'm not going to church to compete with my brothers in Christ. It's a bit of a letdown for all those that want to compete, but I'm there to serve others. Period. There's no other reason for me to be there except to serve and worship. When that is taken away, what's the point of church?"}]},{"start":42,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What John and James actually asked for was to be servants for the rest of their lives. If they wanted the two top spots by Jesus, they would have to live in the two lowest spots on Earth. Are they capable of lowering themselves below the Christ? Can they be more humble, more gentle, more loving, and more compassionate than the Lord? Can they give up their own lives for the salvation of others? That is what they will have to do to be up there with their Savior. They will give their lives, but it will pale in comparison to the ministry and longsuffering of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus says, “If they want to be great in God’s kingdom, they will have to be servants here, they will have to be ministers.” Ministers do not hold high status. I know that goes against modern culture. We think of church ministers as people who are wealthy and pious, but they are not. They have always been meant as the lowly servants of the church. The church would be in great error to neglect the poor ministers."},{"kind":"para","text":"Israel, in the Old Testament, was miserable for neglecting the Levites. The Levites were the Israelites’ ministers. They neglected them over and over again, and it led them into great trials and worldly living. They became disgusting people. That is what a church will look like when it neglects its ministers. Take care of the ministers, and the ministers would be wise to take care of you. There are some “ministers” today who think that the church owes them their full support and service. They think others serve them, not the other way around. Ministers are servants. If you are a church leader, you are a minister, and therefore you are a servant."}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is the \"Son of Man.\" He is the one that ministers to the people. He is literally God in the flesh (the Son of God), and the servant of all (the Son of Man). Jesus is a servant. If we are going to learn to mimic anything from this book, it should be that we mimic His willingness to constantly and always serve others no matter how tired He might be."}]}]},{"start":46,"end":52,"title":"Blind Bartimaeus","entries":[{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Of course! Good ol' blind Bartimaeus. I wonder what his ailment was?"}]},{"start":47,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Thou son of David\" is referencing that this man KNEW who Jesus was. He heard that Jesus had come to town, and so he cries out to Him. He begs for mercy. He does not beg to see again; he begs for salvation. He is begging that Jesus would show him, a lowly, sinful, blind man, mercy."}]},{"start":48,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have not learned their lesson from the children. Jesus will see Bartimaeus as a child. He will see him with eyes of compassion. The people, however, are seeing him as an annoying nuisance to the Lord."}]},{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"He chose you,\" the people said. He chose this man. He could have chosen any man on planet Earth, and He chose blind Bartimaeus. Jesus will show him mercy."}]},{"start":50,"end":51,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bartimaeus wanted to see. What did he wanted to see? Well, surely he wanted to see the whole world. But more so, he wanted to see his Savior. That is the first thing he will see with his eyes."}]},{"start":52,"end":52,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus said, “Your faith gives you sight! You can go now.” Instead of Bartimaeus walking home, he followed Jesus. He followed Christ the rest of the way. His new sight would behold the death of His Savior. What a terrible first sight to see. But what a glorious note that Jesus would rise again on the third day. Bartimaeus would have sight by faith, and he would walk by faith the rest of his life. It would be his trust in Christ that holds his salvation, and that alone would be his most prominent sight."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t know where Bartimaeus goes after the crucifixion. I like to believe he is one of the five hundred who see Christ alive in the resurrection. I believe he was. His physical and spiritual sight would not be wasted. God has joined him with his sight, and no one can take that away from him. It is interesting that Jesus has healed several people at this point, but not everyone has their names written down for us to read. Blind Bartimaeus does, though. His name is in the holiest book of all eternity. He is remembered and memorialized by the Lord."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":38},{"start":39,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":44},{"start":45,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":46},{"start":47,"end":47},{"start":48,"end":48},{"start":49,"end":49},{"start":50,"end":51},{"start":52,"end":52}],"verses":{"1":"And he arose from thence, and cometh into the coasts of Judaea by the farther side of Jordan: and the people resort unto him again; and, as he was wont, he taught them again.","2":"And the Pharisees came to him, and asked him, Is it lawful for a man to put away his wife? tempting him.","3":"And he answered and said unto them, What did Moses command you?","4":"And they said, Moses suffered to write a bill of divorcement, and to put her away.","5":"And Jesus answered and said unto them, For the hardness of your heart he wrote you this precept.","6":"But from the beginning of the creation God made them male and female.","7":"For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and cleave to his wife;","8":"And they twain shall be one flesh: so then they are no more twain, but one flesh.","9":"What therefore God hath joined together, let not man put asunder.","10":"And in the house his disciples asked him again of the same matter.","11":"And he saith unto them, Whosoever shall put away his wife, and marry another, committeth adultery against her.","12":"And if a woman shall put away her husband, and be married to another, she committeth adultery.","13":"And they brought young children to him, that he should touch them: and his disciples rebuked those that brought them.","14":"But when Jesus saw it, he was much displeased, and said unto them, Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God.","15":"Verily I say unto you, Whosoever shall not receive the kingdom of God as a little child, he shall not enter therein.","16":"And he took them up in his arms, put his hands upon them, and blessed them.","17":"And when he was gone forth into the way, there came one running, and kneeled to him, and asked him, Good Master, what shall I do that I may inherit eternal life?","18":"And Jesus said unto him, Why callest thou me good? there is none good but one, that is, God.","19":"Thou knowest the commandments, Do not commit adultery, Do not kill, Do not steal, Do not bear false witness, Defraud not, Honour thy father and mother.","20":"And he answered and said unto him, Master, all these have I observed from my youth.","21":"Then Jesus beholding him loved him, and said unto him, One thing thou lackest: go thy way, sell whatsoever thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come, take up the cross, and follow me.","22":"And he was sad at that saying, and went away grieved: for he had great possessions.","23":"And Jesus looked round about, and saith unto his disciples, How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of God!","24":"And the disciples were astonished at his words. But Jesus answereth again, and saith unto them, Children, how hard is it for them that trust in riches to enter into the kingdom of God!","25":"It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.","26":"And they were astonished out of measure, saying among themselves, Who then can be saved?","27":"And Jesus looking upon them saith, With men it is impossible, but not with God: for with God all things are possible.","28":"Then Peter began to say unto him, Lo, we have left all, and have followed thee.","29":"And Jesus answered and said, Verily I say unto you, There is no man that hath left house, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands, for my sake, and the gospel's,","30":"But he shall receive an hundredfold now in this time, houses, and brethren, and sisters, and mothers, and children, and lands, with persecutions; and in the world to come eternal life.","31":"But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.","32":"And they were in the way going up to Jerusalem; and Jesus went before them: and they were amazed; and as they followed, they were afraid. And he took again the twelve, and began to tell them what things should happen unto him,","33":"Saying, Behold, we go up to Jerusalem; and the Son of man shall be delivered unto the chief priests, and unto the scribes; and they shall condemn him to death, and shall deliver him to the Gentiles:","34":"And they shall mock him, and shall scourge him, and shall spit upon him, and shall kill him: and the third day he shall rise again.","35":"And James and John, the sons of Zebedee, come unto him, saying, Master, we would that thou shouldest do for us whatsoever we shall desire.","36":"And he said unto them, What would ye that I should do for you?","37":"They said unto him, Grant unto us that we may sit, one on thy right hand, and the other on thy left hand, in thy glory.","38":"But Jesus said unto them, Ye know not what ye ask: can ye drink of the cup that I drink of? and be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with?","39":"And they said unto him, We can. And Jesus said unto them, Ye shall indeed drink of the cup that I drink of; and with the baptism that I am baptized withal shall ye be baptized:","40":"But to sit on my right hand and on my left hand is not mine to give; but it shall be given to them for whom it is prepared.","41":"And when the ten heard it, they began to be much displeased with James and John.","42":"But Jesus called them to him, and saith unto them, Ye know that they which are accounted to rule over the Gentiles exercise lordship over them; and their great ones exercise authority upon them.","43":"But so shall it not be among you: but whosoever will be great among you, shall be your minister:","44":"And whosoever of you will be the chiefest, shall be servant of all.","45":"For even the Son of man came not to be ministered unto, but to minister, and to give his life a ransom for many.","46":"And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus, sat by the highway side begging.","47":"And when he heard that it was Jesus of Nazareth, he began to cry out, and say, Jesus, thou son of David, have mercy on me.","48":"And many charged him that he should hold his peace: but he cried the more a great deal, Thou son of David, have mercy on me.","49":"And Jesus stood still, and commanded him to be called. And they call the blind man, saying unto him, Be of good comfort, rise; he calleth thee.","50":"And he, casting away his garment, rose, and came to Jesus.","51":"And Jesus answered and said unto him, What wilt thou that I should do unto thee? The blind man said unto him, Lord, that I might receive my sight.","52":"And Jesus said unto him, Go thy way; thy faith hath made thee whole. And immediately he received his sight, and followed Jesus in the way."}},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Jesus has been travelling closer and closer to His crucifixion. He has been trying to prepare His disciples for that moment for a while now. They still don't understand what He means by it. He took a moment to teach about some important issues that the religionists of that day were trying to bring up to try and hurt the reputation of Christ and His followers. He used children as an example of how a believer is supposed to be, and talked about how difficult it is for a rich man to come to know Christ. The chapter ended with Jesus healing a blind man named Bartimaeus."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get the start to what is commonly referred to as \"Holy Week.\" This is the week that leads up to the crucifixion and death of Jesus Christ. It starts on a Sunday and ends on a Tuesday in this chapter. There is a lot of things that the Lord is doing this week to prepare His disciples, and prepare His enemies for what needs to take place to save mankind from their sins."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Jesus has been traveling closer and closer to His crucifixion. He has been trying to prepare His disciples for that moment for a while now. They still don't understand what He means by it. He took a moment to teach about some important issues that the religionists of that day were trying to bring up to hurt the reputation of Christ and His followers. He used children as an example of how a believer is supposed to be, and talked about how difficult it is for a rich man to come to know Christ. The chapter ended with Jesus healing a blind man named Bartimaeus. In this chapter, we get the start to what is commonly referred to as \"Holy Week.\" This is the week that leads up to the crucifixion and death of Jesus Christ. It starts on a Sunday and ends on a Tuesday in this chapter. There are a lot of things that the Lord is doing this week to prepare His disciples, and prepare His enemies for what needs to take place to save mankind from their sins."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Sunday","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We should all know that Jerusalem will be Jesus’ final destination before His crucifixion. Jesus has not been silent about that. Instead of Him going in the opposite direction of Jerusalem, He is seen walking towards it. To start off this “Holy Week,” He comes to Bethphage and Bethany."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Mount of Olives is a very unique place that I think deserves some time in study. It’s a place that is mentioned a few times throughout scripture. It’s where David went after he had the kingdom stolen from him. He was wandering around in the wilderness and came to this location to pray and stay until there was some news that he should make his way back home and speak to his son."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And David went up by the ascent of mount Olivet, and wept as he went up, and had his head covered, and he went barefoot: and all the people that was with him covered every man his head, and they went up, weeping as they went up.","ref":"2 Samuel 15:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this is also the place in which Jesus comes to pray and stay. It's also where the disciples will end up after the Lord has ascended."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then returned they unto Jerusalem from the mount called Olivet, which is from Jerusalem a sabbath day's journey.","ref":"Acts 1:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s an important geographical location that presumably can still be explored today. Granted, there might not be nearly as much to look at there now, but I imagine it was somewhat of a tranquil place back then. Jesus is preparing His disciples for what is coming, and right now He has to fulfill a prophecy that was spoken about in the Old Testament. So He’s sending two of His disciples on a task that probably doesn’t make much sense to them, but they are to listen and go with faith and trust that Jesus knows what He is doing."}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord knew about this colt far beforehand. Whether or not this was arranged, I couldn’t say. I believe it’s entirely possible that the Lord just simply knew that there would be a colt tied over there, and that there would be questions asked about taking the colt."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it’s quite a miracle that there are going to be people there who are just going to listen to the disciples and let them take the colt without much of a fight. It will seem as though the disciples are “stealing” someone else’s property. Maybe that’s a good argument as to why we could think that this was pre-arranged? Either way, the people seem to know who the Lord is and the work that needs to be done."}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They followed the instructions of the Lord. The people must have known who Jesus was in order for them to just let these disciples take the colt. They could have done more prodding and questioning, but they didn’t. It all goes down just as Jesus had said it would, and the disciples simply did what they were told to do. It went, actually, quite smoothly. The disciples were prone to mess things like this up, but they didn’t. There was always something to get in their way to make things difficult, but that wasn’t the case either. Not on Holy Week."}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Throughout this book of scripture, we have seen Jesus constantly try to keep His presence quiet to the masses. He has told nearly every person that He has performed a miracle for to keep quiet about how that miracle came to be. He repeatedly instructed people to “tell no man” about the events and then moved on to the next place. Obviously, that didn’t work most of the time, and people learned about Jesus quite rapidly."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps that was His plan all along. Jesus knows the genuine faith of a person and understands that it would be nearly impossible for people to keep quiet about such an amazing feat that just took place. In this scene, however, it is quite a display. He is not willing to hide Himself any longer. This is a display of sacrifice. It’s a meager triumphal entry. Compared to a worldly king, people would likely laugh at this display. Even still today, people would laugh. But this is the picture of the Lord. Even in His triumph, He is lowly. But the next time He comes, it will not be on a lowly colt."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse; and he that sat upon him was called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he doth judge and make war. His eyes were as a flame of fire, and on his head were many crowns; and he had a name written, that no man knew, but he himself. And he was clothed with a vesture dipped in blood: and his name is called The Word of God. And the armies which were in heaven followed him upon white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean. And out of his mouth goeth a sharp sword, that with it he should smite the nations: and he shall rule them with a rod of iron: and he treadeth the winepress of the fierceness and wrath of Almighty God. And he hath on his vesture and on his thigh a name written, KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS.","ref":"Revelation 19:11-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you missed the lowly Christ in this lifetime, it is very well certain that you will meet the Judge of Righteousness in the next one."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hosanna\" means \"pray, save us.\" These people knew what Christ was here for. They knew that He was not here to overthrow authority. Authority will certainly be given to Him (we have already spoken about that in the previous chapters), but He came here to give Himself as a \"ransom for many.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For even the Son of man came not to be ministered unto, but to minister, and to give his life a ransom for many.","ref":"Mark 10:45"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is no turning back from a public display like this. Jesus has made Himself openly recognized and known here, and He would be a very weak Master if He turned around at the last moment after making such a display in front of everyone so close to His ending city."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He came to Jerusalem but did not stay there. He took a look around and then turned back to go to Bethany to stay the night. You might look at this and think, “Well, that wasn’t much. Was that the Lord’s full day?” Yes, it was. He taught a bunch of lessons, traveled to Bethany, rode a colt, looked around the temple of Jerusalem, and then went back to Bethany. Ministry is strange, isn’t it?"},{"kind":"para","text":"That, honestly, seems to be how fast the days go for a minister. Now, I’m sure there was plenty more that happened with the Lord that day. I’m sure they ate, took breaks, prayed, and prepared for the day. But the minister’s day seems to just roll by rather quickly. If you are counseling people, the day goes even quicker. Some people can just talk for hours about their needs. That is not necessarily a bad thing; it just makes it difficult to move to the next need."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I counsel people who can talk a lot about their problems, I have a hard time saying, “Hey, I have another appointment. Do you think we can meet again and continue the conversation?” I want people to just let everything out in one sitting so they feel better as quickly as possible. To let people linger on their hurts is a very dangerous venture. At the same time, I can’t just very well allow one person to rob time from other hurting people either. So it makes it difficult to minister in these situations."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, the Lord had an eventful day, but it was a good eventful day. It was a setup for the rest of the week. The disciples perhaps didn’t fully understand what was happening, but they were likely enjoying the occasion. They knew that Jesus was going to save them. They understood that He is THE Christ. He is the Lord in the flesh. But they will be hit with a left hook by the end of the week."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Monday","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is interesting. There are only two times in scripture when it talks about Jesus being hungry. The first time is when He was fasting for forty days before being tempted by Satan:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, he was afterward an hungred.","ref":"Matthew 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"And then again right here as the second day of Holy Week. There are glimpses of Jesus' human side, and this was one of them. But He is going to use this as an illustration right now as well. It's an important illustration, and it will be of good use to us as we study it."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was excited to eat some figs. He was disappointed when He came to the tree and found nothing. This story serves as a great representation of the church today. God uses this illustration in the book of Isaiah as well, but we are getting a New Testament look at it, so we can link it to the church far better. It represents us. From a distance, people would think our church is fruitful. Up close, however, we find that there is no fruit on the tree. It just looks pretty and ready, but it’s not. We are not ready. As much as I love my church, my church is not ready. All churches are falling apart one way or another. It’s up to leadership and ministers to address those leaks and fix the ship, but we don’t see that happening very often. The fruit of ministry is to help fix the problems in the church. Instead, we are just a leafy tree with nothing to show for it."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord curses this tree. He has the ability, in His power, to completely reject and turn down a church today. He can keep it from prospering. I’ve seen many churches today, however, that are prospering all on the wrong thing. The Lord seems to be silent about them. He allows those to grow. Why? Well, I don’t know. He gives the people a false sense of hope for some reason."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites fell for the same thing. They were doing their rituals and traditional deeds for the Lord without a heart for Him, and the Lord allowed them to grow, and grow, and grow, but there was no fruit. Israel was a big beautiful tree with plenty of shade and flourishing, but it had nothing to show for it. The Lord let it grow, and then He took it all away in the blink of an eye."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what I believe happens to a church that grows too fast. God will let it grow quickly, and it will grow into its own destruction. Jesus will see it and say, “Well, this has ruined my hopes. I am going to do away with it.” Notice the tree doesn’t wither right away. Everyone heard what He said, but they won’t see the results of this until later on. Sometimes that is how it will be with a flourishing church that has no fruit."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":19,"title":"Casting Out Moneychangers","entries":[{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I believe this is why He came to the temple yesterday. He came to see how it looked and to do a survey of the temple. He saw that it was given over to thieves and had become a marketplace. That was not what the temple was for. God’s house today can be turned into that. We can easily fall into the idea that church is a place to sell things. The only sale that a pastor should be making behind the pulpit is of scripture. People will buy the truth. At least, if they were smart, they would."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we might notice there is a pretty important difference between the temple account here and the fig tree just a moment ago. Why didn’t the Lord cultivate the fig tree to produce more fruit? He is seen cleaning up the temple but destroying the tree. I think these are two views of the same problem. The temple became fruitless. Jesus has the power to grow and the power to wither His church. So why not let the temple wither? Well, He has a future purpose for it. We will see this temple being used quite mightily in the book of Acts. The first church will start right here in Jerusalem and expand outward from there."},{"kind":"para","text":"The withered fig tree is a representation of how useless a church can become. It could also be a representation of the New Covenant. This is the week where Jesus will provide mankind with His New Covenant."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In that he saith, A new covenant, he hath made the first old. Now that which decayeth and waxeth old is ready to vanish away.","ref":"Hebrews 8:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"It was what the Lord had promised for generations now, and it was coming to pass as the week progressed."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The sad part about this is not so much that the Pharisees allowed God’s house to be turned into a marketplace. That is sad, no doubt, but that is not the saddest part. The sad part is that they were the ones who helped make it that way! They had totally gotten rid of their moral code for the work of the Lord and became vessels of profit. They only did their religious ruling so they could fill their pockets. In fact, as we learn in Matthew, they were abusing innocent people to fill their own pockets. They were cashing out on the fatherless and widows. They abused the weak and poor so they could earn a wage. They mistreated their ministering."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is still a very large problem in the church today. There are a lot of so-called “ministers” who are only doing their job so they can be paid. That is the wrong way to minister. If they lost their paycheck, they would be calling it quits in ministry. Honestly, those are the types of people that you should not have involved with the leadership of your church. If you have to pay someone to get involved, they are never going to be genuine. The only reason a church should be paying someone is because they are already doing that work for free and the church wants them to be available to do it more. Otherwise, truly, you are wasting your money."},{"kind":"para","text":"This act of Jesus cleansing the temple to be what it is supposed to be upset the religionists. Everything Jesus did upset them. He was taking away their easy prey. He was giving freedom to the innocent. Evil people hate it when innocent people are relieved."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another full day passes, and He leaves Jerusalem again. Notice that He isn’t staying in Jerusalem. If that is where He is going to have His final destination, then why doesn’t He just stay there? Well, these people were godless and unfaithful. The people of Bethany, on the other hand, were mostly converts to the way. They were a large group of Christ followers. The Lord had a place with them, but He did not have a place with the people of Jerusalem. He will do His work there, but He cannot stay there. It’s the heart of mankind, honestly. Christ will do a work in every individual heart, but He will not stay in a hardened and rejectful one. He can only dwell in a saved heart."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":24,"title":"Tuesday","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It took a whole day. The tree was good for nothing now. From the roots, it means that there was no way to even save the fig tree at this point. They could not put water on it or on the ground. The roots were completely withered as well. It is absolutely dead. What a sad thought that a church could ever end up that way."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter noticed the tree was completely useless. He remembered what had happened. It was no mistake that the fig tree looked this way now. Instead of Jesus directly referencing the fig tree here, Jesus goes into a very important lesson on prayer."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When I was younger, I really loved Star Wars. My friends and I would play all the time, and we would have lightsabers that we would battle with. One of the things that I longed to have was force powers. I wanted to be able to control and move things with my mind. That was such an amazing idea to me. Obviously, those things are not real, but the Lord seems to insinuate that it can be (in a roundabout way)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think a lot of people are not moving mountains into the sea because they don’t have genuine faith. I know I don’t have enough faith to believe that this is possible. I don’t even know why I would need to move a mountain into the sea, but perhaps that is something that will need to take place for a very specific spiritual purpose. Jesus simply killed a fig tree. He uses that as an illustration of power. How do we utilize this power? He says we must use it by prayer!"}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, if I pray for a mansion, will I have it? If I pray for a really nice car (the nicest in existence), will the Lord just bless me with it? If I pray for a new family, is that how I will receive it? Of course, there are stipulations to prayer. Prayers are answered on the Lord’s terms, not on our terms. I spoke to a ministry friend about this idea of prayer. I said, “We don’t pray based on selfish ambitions and expect our prayers to be answered.” He disagreed with me. He said, “I think the Lord listens to all our prayer needs no matter what they are, and I believe He answers them all accordingly.” I replied, “They will only be answered according to His Will. It must align with what is good and righteous, otherwise people will be praying for a lot of dull materialism that will lead them further from God and deeper in sin.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the danger of praying outside of God’s Will. That is why God does not answer our prayers the way we think He should. He answers prayers, but He will only answer them in the way that is best for eternity’s sake. Sure, there are blessing prayers right here on Earth that we can receive now, but (as I’ve said before and will say again) we should not be looking for our rewards here. Our rewards here are all we get. Eternity’s rewards are… well… eternal."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":26,"title":"Forgive","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s one of those codes of praying. If we are going to pray, there is a standard. One standard is to forgive those people with whom you are not very happy. Lately, it seems that is a lot of what my prayer has been. I’ve been praying that the Lord helps me forgive. There is a reason that we should forgive, and Jesus tells us why we should do it right here. We know that the Lord has forgiven us. It only makes sense that if God can forgive us of our many evil and sinful deeds, then we are more than capable of forgiving others of smaller deeds they have done."},{"kind":"para","text":"Or, perhaps the deeds are not smaller. Maybe someone killed your dog (or perhaps did something detestable to a family member). That is a very different situation. Even then, we are to (in time, and in a way) forgive that person. We should still want justice served, but it should be done in a heart of forgiveness. Otherwise, we will be in danger of unforgiveness from our Father. I call it a danger because of what Jesus says next."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The moment we are saved is the moment we are forgiven. So is it possible to have unforgiven sin even if we are saved because we are not forgiving people? Well, it would seem so. However, we also are assured that the Lord forgets sin. How does He forget it? Through repentance!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Repent ye therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out, when the times of refreshing shall come from the presence of the Lord;","ref":"Acts 3:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.","ref":"1 John 1:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.","ref":"2 Peter 3:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I tell you, Nay: but, except ye repent, ye shall all likewise perish.","ref":"Luke 13:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"A Christian should be a man that is always in a mode of repentance. I would argue that if you are not repenting, you are likely not forgiven by the Lord. If you are not forgiven, that means you are not saved. It's impossible to be saved and never repent. It's also impossible to repent and never forgive others. These all connect together, and they are all accessed through prayer."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":33,"title":"What Authority?","entries":[{"start":27,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I mean, Jesus was doing some important moral cleaning of the temple. Jesus, honestly, didn't need authority to do it. Anyone with a strong moral code would see the temple and know that what was happening in there was wrong and distasteful. BUT, Jesus HAD authority to make these decisions because the Lord was with Him (and He IS the Lord). The chief priests didn't want to acknowledge this, but Christ has given every proof possible that He is the Savior. Their rejection is on their own heads. The goal of this was to ultimately get Jesus to confess to something so they could arrest Him and destroy His reputation."}]},{"start":29,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like an odd question to throw out there, but it was a very direct question that caused them to stumble. Honestly, the question is connected to theirs. Jesus cleansed the temple, restoring it to the place it was meant to be. John was also cleansing the Lord’s instruments through baptism. John was a Levite, and his father was a priest. That family was very well known to the people of Jerusalem."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even if John was not a Levite, he certainly made an impact through his preaching and presence. However, because John was a Levite, this question would hit very close to the religious rulers that Jesus was talking to. If John’s baptisms were of Heaven, then the authority was of God, and that is the same authority Jesus had to cleanse the temple. If they claimed that it was just the authority of man, the people would start a riot because they respected John and trusted in his preaching and baptism."}]},{"start":31,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was no right answer here. Actually, the right answer is “from Heaven.” In truth, John’s baptism was a heavenly picture. It was a picture of new life and a representation of the New Covenant. So far this week, that has been the continual representation that Jesus was giving."},{"kind":"para","text":"On Sunday, Jesus gave a picture of the New Covenant by making His sacrifice known and triumphant. On Monday, the Lord gave a picture of the New Covenant by cleansing out the temple and withering a tree. On Tuesday, Jesus gave a picture of the New Covenant by showing the direct access that man can have to God’s power if they have faith and believe."},{"kind":"para","text":"We still have the rest of the week to explore, and Jesus will make it very clear that the New Covenant is guaranteed to come. These people will have no choice but to either accept it for a home in Heaven or reject it for a home in Hell."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, by not answering the question at all, they made themselves look foolish. They would have been far wiser to just admit fault. They could have seen their lives change drastically because the Lord was with them. Instead, they rejected everything about the Lord and would rather play dumb than address their sinful hearts and arrogant spirits. That's the theme of the religionists in those days, and it's a continued ordinance by the religionists today."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33}],"verses":{"1":"And when they came nigh to Jerusalem, unto Bethphage and Bethany, at the mount of Olives, he sendeth forth two of his disciples,","2":"And saith unto them, Go your way into the village over against you: and as soon as ye be entered into it, ye shall find a colt tied, whereon never man sat; loose him, and bring him.","3":"And if any man say unto you, Why do ye this? say ye that the Lord hath need of him; and straightway he will send him hither.","4":"And they went their way, and found the colt tied by the door without in a place where two ways met; and they loose him.","5":"And certain of them that stood there said unto them, What do ye, loosing the colt?","6":"And they said unto them even as Jesus had commanded: and they let them go.","7":"And they brought the colt to Jesus, and cast their garments on him; and he sat upon him.","8":"And many spread their garments in the way: and others cut down branches off the trees, and strawed them in the way.","9":"And they that went before, and they that followed, cried, saying, Hosanna; Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord:","10":"Blessed be the kingdom of our father David, that cometh in the name of the Lord: Hosanna in the highest.","11":"And Jesus entered into Jerusalem, and into the temple: and when he had looked round about upon all things, and now the eventide was come, he went out unto Bethany with the twelve.","12":"And on the morrow, when they were come from Bethany, he was hungry:","13":"And seeing a fig tree afar off having leaves, he came, if haply he might find any thing thereon: and when he came to it, he found nothing but leaves; for the time of figs was not yet.","14":"And Jesus answered and said unto it, No man eat fruit of thee hereafter for ever. And his disciples heard it.","15":"And they come to Jerusalem: and Jesus went into the temple, and began to cast out them that sold and bought in the temple, and overthrew the tables of the moneychangers, and the seats of them that sold doves;","16":"And would not suffer that any man should carry any vessel through the temple.","17":"And he taught, saying unto them, Is it not written, My house shall be called of all nations the house of prayer? but ye have made it a den of thieves.","18":"And the scribes and chief priests heard it, and sought how they might destroy him: for they feared him, because all the people was astonished at his doctrine.","19":"And when even was come, he went out of the city.","20":"And in the morning, as they passed by, they saw the fig tree dried up from the roots.","21":"And Peter calling to remembrance saith unto him, Master, behold, the fig tree which thou cursedst is withered away.","22":"And Jesus answering saith unto them, Have faith in God.","23":"For verily I say unto you, That whosoever shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; and shall not doubt in his heart, but shall believe that those things which he saith shall come to pass; he shall have whatsoever he saith.","24":"Therefore I say unto you, What things soever ye desire, when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them.","25":"And when ye stand praying, forgive, if ye have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.","26":"But if ye do not forgive, neither will your Father which is in heaven forgive your trespasses.","27":"And they come again to Jerusalem: and as he was walking in the temple, there come to him the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders,","28":"And say unto him, By what authority doest thou these things? and who gave thee this authority to do these things?","29":"And Jesus answered and said unto them, I will also ask of you one question, and answer me, and I will tell you by what authority I do these things.","30":"The baptism of John, was it from heaven, or of men? answer me.","31":"And they reasoned with themselves, saying, If we shall say, From heaven; he will say, Why then did ye not believe him?","32":"But if we shall say, Of men; they feared the people: for all men counted John, that he was a prophet indeed.","33":"And they answered and said unto Jesus, We cannot tell. And Jesus answering saith unto them, Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things."}},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter saw the start of Holy Week. We were able to read about Sunday to Tuesday and the things that Jesus was preparing for the New Covenant promise that would be sealed through His resurrection coming the following Sunday. He is soaking in every moment of this week to prepare, train, and provide for this moment in history. This moment will be a pivotal point for Earth and eternity."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Jesus continues to teach, and uses the hate of the religionists to prove His point further. He knows what is coming, and He knows when it's coming, and He knows how it's coming, and so He is making it very obvious that He has it planned, and is ALLOWING it to happen. These religionists are not going to TAKE Jesus' life. Jesus is going to GIVE it."}],"previously":[", our last chapter marked the beginning of Holy Week. We read about the events from Sunday to Tuesday, focusing on Jesus’ preparations for the New Covenant promise, which would be sealed through His resurrection the following Sunday. He was fully immersed in this week, using it to prepare, train, and provide for this pivotal moment in history, significant for both Earth and eternity. In this chapter, Jesus continues to teach, using the animosity of the religionists to further illustrate His point. He is aware of what is coming, when it will happen, and how it will unfold. Consequently, He makes it clear that He has everything planned and is allowing it to occur. These religionists will not take Jesus’ life; He will give it."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"Back to Parables","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In case you don't remember, in the last chapter we ended on Tuesday, and the religionists try to blame Jesus for doing something good without the proper authority. Jesus asks them about John's baptism, and they play dumb. So now Jesus (in this chapter) is going back to speaking in parables. The religionists are listening here. We are starting off with a man that was planting a vineyard and preparing people to tend to that vineyard while He went His way home. The man that planted the vineyard is the owner. He is the Lord."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The servant was sent by the owner. The servant is the prophets of the Old Testament. The vineyard is God's people (Israel). They won't get this connection yet."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is what the Israelites did to the prophets over and over again. They didn’t like the words of the prophets, so they beat them and sent them away. They were supposed to produce fruit for the owner of the vineyard, and instead, they were abusing the work of the owner. They were basically robbing the owner. That is what God’s children were doing. They were robbing the Lord, and the Lord was sending people to help them get on track. Instead of taking the help, they rejected it and abused the Lord’s servant."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The proof of mistreatment would be evident to the owner at this point. This is the second servant that the workers have abused. The owner is not going to be thrilled about this. Yet, for some reason, the owner just keeps giving them chances and sending more servants."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These workers were killing the owner’s men. If I owned a company and I was expecting workers to produce a specific product, but instead, they were killing and beating the servants I sent to help them, I would have fired and gotten rid of those workers the very first time it happened. Not the Lord. He is patient. He chose these people and is trying to work with them, yet they are nothing but rejecting, hateful, and evil."},{"kind":"para","text":"Before we get too carried away with name-calling, we should understand that we were like them too. We used to be a part of that world. We rejected the Lord’s servants multiple times over before we finally accepted that the Lord’s servants were true. The church is a lot like the Israelites in that way."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The son is Jesus. We know that. The religionists don’t, though. The religionists don't really understand this parable still today. The son should be respected. They've killed the servants, but there is no way the people would kill His son..."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's look at this for a moment. The workers KNOW that this is the son. They literally say it out loud. Instead of being wise and earning because the owner has given them a job and takes care of them, they abuse the owner’s goodness and are killing everyone that the owner sends, including his own son. He only had ONE son, by the way. This was his only child that he was sending to them. He sent them because he loves these people. These people OBVIOUSLY don't love the owner, though."}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is what is happening at this moment in time. On this Tuesday of Holy Week, the men were ready to kill the Lord’s Son. They are planning and plotting for that moment. And when it happens, the owner of the vineyard is going to “destroy” the husbandmen and give the vineyard unto others."},{"kind":"para","text":"The whole world has access to the vineyard, but only the children of God will work it. God has given His vineyard over to His true, genuine children. The other people He trusted with the vineyard have run their course. The Lord came through those people, and they still reject Him. He used their rejection to save everyone who is willing to turn to Him."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The builders are the Israelites, the stone is the Lord (we talked about that a few chapters back), and He is the chief cornerstone. He is marvelous in our eyes. He is only marvelous to the eyes of faith. These religionists do not have those eyes, and so they have the eyes of rejection."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner. This is the LORD's doing; it is marvellous in our eyes. This is the day which the LORD hath made; we will rejoice and be glad in it.","ref":"Psalm 118:22-24"},{"kind":"para","text":"His rejection became the foundation of our faith."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This is the stone which was set at nought of you builders, which is become the head of the corner.","ref":"Acts 4:11"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was against them because of their eyes of rejection. The fact that they wanted to “lay hold on him” proves His parable even further. I’m willing to bet that everyone there saw the anger in their faces. They all knew that these religionists were being evil. It was about time that someone put them in their place, and they were likely all thrilled to see the Lord say something to them. No one else could without being accused of breaking some sort of law."},{"kind":"para","text":"The law of the Lord was never meant to be a burden on people. It was meant to be a connection to the Lord’s salvation, and yet the religionists were burdening the people with God’s law. That is all religions still do today. And if you dare to question them on this, they still get just as angry."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":17,"title":"Give to Who It Belongs","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They need SOMETHING to pin against the Lord. They need a way to get the people against Christ and His disciples. They despised Him so much that they were willing to fabricate false witnesses to get Him. That is a theme the religious zealots will use even through the book of Acts."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They try to flatter Jesus. Jesus isn't flattered. The goal of this is to try and get Jesus to break Roman law. They couldn't pin Jewish law on Him, so they will bring Rome against Him. Except God is smart. He invented smart. So there's nothing they can say or accuse Him of that will trip Him up."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, the Lord could have just rejected this question, but He is going to use them as an example. Jesus uses hypocrites all the time. He uses you and me today, whether we like it or not. The Lord will use these hypocrites to show His wisdom."}]},{"start":16,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What an answer! You know why Jesus could say such a thing? Because money had no hold on Him. He had more power than money had. To the religionists, money IS power. To the Christian, GOD is power. That’s why I have a difficult time with churches talking about the necessity to tithe."},{"kind":"para","text":"I will agree that tithing is a useful thing for the church. If you love your church, you either tithe money or tithe time. You should always be giving something to your church if you believe they are worthy of it. But the power is of God, not the money. If you have debts, take care of your debts first before you try and give anything to the Lord’s work. The Lord can use your presence and time better than your money and distance."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":27,"title":"The God of the Living","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At least the Pharisees have some truth. The Sadducees were just sad. They didn't believe in a resurrection of the dead. Jesus' message was constantly proving them wrong. Truly, Jesus was bringing people back to life in His literal ministry. Either way, they were throwing out all the stops possible towards Jesus on this fine Tuesday morning."}]},{"start":19,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, they don’t believe in a resurrection. This is a setup. They try to flatter Him with “Master” again. They must think very little of that title to just be throwing it around like they have been. But they are using another law to try and pin something against Jesus. Jesus knows the law better than them, though. He lives the law. They do not."},{"kind":"para","text":"This particular law was a genuine one. The Lord had set up a means to take care of the lineages of the tribes. He wanted every chance possible for a family line to be redeemed, and so He made it a priority for them to be redeemed through relatives. They are trying to use this against the Lord, but the Lord is their redeemer. All the law does is point to Him. That’s funny to me."}]},{"start":24,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"My wife and I will no longer be married in eternity. We will live like the angels live, having the ability to converse with God and bask in His holiness and presence. We will be doing this together, but we won’t be married to each other any longer because we will be in oneness with the Lord. Our marriage is to Him alone. Even in our marriage today, we made vows to the Lord, not just to each other. Those vows are trusted because the Lord is the one who has sealed them. The vows we made to the Lord will be kept for all of eternity. They thought they would try and pin something to Jesus, but the Lord is the author of marriage."}]},{"start":26,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great point! If the resurrection isn’t true, and yet they believe the Word of God is true, then why does God tell Moses (born four hundred years after Jacob) that He is STILL their God? He doesn’t say, “I WAS the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.” Rather, He says, “I AM the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.” He then says, “I AM THAT I AM.” He is a God of the living. He is STILL the God of the living. God has no use for dead men."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":34,"title":"The Greatest Commandment","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I think this was the first genuine question of the day. This scribe was amazed by Jesus, while the rest of the people were just trying to pin something against Him. Because He held so strongly to truth, the scribe had a burning question. His question is innocent and extremely useful. What is the first (top and most important) commandment? I believe this scribe really didn’t know."}]},{"start":29,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This first commandment is actually the one that encapsulates the first four commandments. The first four are about loving God properly. The last six are about loving others properly. Jesus will wrap them up into two very basic and easy-to-remember commandments."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It really is THAT simple. When you are doing anything in your ministry and walk, you must ask yourself two questions: \"How will this show love towards the Lord?\" and \"How will this show love towards other people?\" You will never make a wrong decision if you keep these two things in mind when you do something. Even if people get upset with you for doing it, keeping these in mind will help you know that you are making the right choice."}]},{"start":32,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This scribe knows scripture, but he needed the reminder from the Lord. He knows that Jesus' words are important, true, and good. There is one thing he is missing, though:"}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The scribe told Jesus that His words were good \"discreetly.\" The only reason he is not IN God's kingdom is because He has not fully trusted in Jesus. Jesus is God's kingdom. We have made mention of that quite a bit through this book of scripture. There was just one more small step for this scribe to take, and He would have eternal life."}]}]},{"start":35,"end":37,"title":"David's Lord","entries":[{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is going to go to David's own words and acclamation now. If the religionists had a problem with David's words, they would have to take that up with David."}]},{"start":36,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"David calls his lineage, specifically his great-grandson, his Lord. God made a promise of a messiah through the lineage of David, and David was able to give honor to his future grandson as being THE Lord. David placed his future promise as Lord. He believed and trusted God that much. The religionists would dispute the claim, but without any strong affirmation. Jesus has David’s own words to affirm His presence."},{"kind":"para","text":"The “common” people were glad to hear about this connection to Christ. I’m not much of a scholar. I’m not a religionist. I’m not a purist, perfectionist, or legalist. I am just a common person. But any talk of the Lord brings a smile to my face because I personally know Him. If you genuinely know the Lord, then talking about Him (even in the simplest conversations) should put a smile on your face."}]}]},{"start":38,"end":40,"title":"Religious Showoff's","entries":[{"start":38,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is describing a person who simply does God’s work for show. That is religion today. That was religion back then, and the example has not strayed. Better yet, today, just beware of religion in general. Get your information straight from the source. My job, prayer, and goal is to get you to go to God personally, rather than just learn about God through me. These notes are here to help you do that and to answer any questions you might have, but ultimately they are here to get you to learn from the Lord without me."},{"kind":"para","text":"I am not going to tell you how to worship God; I am only going to tell you what God says about worship. I am not going to tell you how to preach the gospel; I am only going to tell you what God says about preaching. I am not going to tell you how to pray; I am only going to tell you what God says about praying. Take God’s Word over mine. Please. Anyone who tells you to take their own word about it is a religious showoff that is not interested in leading you into the truth."}]}]},{"start":41,"end":44,"title":"Give By Faith","entries":[{"start":41,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was watching the people. He was not watching the amount. He knew the richer that people were, the more people were giving. But the people were what Jesus was interested in. He didn't care about the amount. He cared about the faith behind the amount. He took special notice of a poor widow. She was poor by financial standards, but rich in faith."}]},{"start":43,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Something that God makes mention of over and over again throughout His Word is giving from the heart. When we give, we don’t give what’s leftover; we give what is first. We give what is right. We give from a heart of love, not a heart of necessity. We don’t give because of religious practice; we give because of faith practice. We give out of faith, not out of fear. Churches might try to strong-arm you into giving, but if you give because you feel guilty, you are better off not giving at all. Jesus doesn’t want your gift that way."},{"kind":"para","text":"Tuesday seems to have been a very long day for the Lord. It’s a lot of conversations and lessons learned. It’s still not over either. Our whole next chapter will continue to focus on this day as well. Jesus still has some lessons to teach in this place to prepare for the coming salvation."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":35},{"start":36,"end":37},{"start":38,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":44}],"verses":{"1":"And he began to speak unto them by parables. A certain man planted a vineyard, and set an hedge about it, and digged a place for the winefat, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country.","2":"And at the season he sent to the husbandmen a servant, that he might receive from the husbandmen of the fruit of the vineyard.","3":"And they caught him, and beat him, and sent him away empty.","4":"And again he sent unto them another servant; and at him they cast stones, and wounded him in the head, and sent him away shamefully handled.","5":"And again he sent another; and him they killed, and many others; beating some, and killing some.","6":"Having yet therefore one son, his wellbeloved, he sent him also last unto them, saying, They will reverence my son.","7":"But those husbandmen said among themselves, This is the heir; come, let us kill him, and the inheritance shall be ours.'","8":"And they took him, and killed him, and cast him out of the vineyard.","9":"What shall therefore the lord of the vineyard do? he will come and destroy the husbandmen, and will give the vineyard unto others.","10":"And have ye not read this scripture; The stone which the builders rejected is become the head of the corner:","11":"This was the Lord's doing, and it is marvellous in our eyes?","12":"And they sought to lay hold on him, but feared the people: for they knew that he had spoken the parable against them: and they left him, and went their way.","13":"And they send unto him certain of the Pharisees and of the Herodians, to catch him in his words.","14":"And when they were come, they say unto him, Master, we know that thou art true, and carest for no man: for thou regardest not the person of men, but teachest the way of God in truth: Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not?","15":"Shall we give, or shall we not give? But he, knowing their hypocrisy, said unto them, Why tempt ye me? bring me a penny, that I may see it.","16":"And they brought it. And he saith unto them, Whose is this image and superscription? And they said unto him, Caesar's.","17":"And Jesus answering said unto them, Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's. And they marvelled at him.","18":"Then come unto him the Sadducees, which say there is no resurrection; and they asked him, saying,","19":"Master, Moses wrote unto us, If a man's brother die, and leave his wife behind him, and leave no children, that his brother should take his wife, and raise up seed unto his brother.","20":"Now there were seven brethren: and the first took a wife, and dying left no seed.","21":"And the second took her, and died, neither left he any seed: and the third likewise.","22":"And the seven had her, and left no seed: last of all the woman died also.","23":"In the resurrection therefore, when they shall rise, whose wife shall she be of them? for the seven had her to wife.","24":"And Jesus answering said unto them, Do ye not therefore err, because ye know not the scriptures, neither the power of God?","25":"For when they shall rise from the dead, they neither marry, nor are given in marriage; but are as the angels which are in heaven.","26":"And as touching the dead, that they rise: have ye not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush God spake unto him, saying, I am the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob?","27":"He is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living: ye therefore do greatly err.","28":"And one of the scribes came, and having heard them reasoning together, and perceiving that he had answered them well, asked him, Which is the first commandment of all?","29":"And Jesus answered him, The first of all the commandments is, Hear, O Israel; The Lord our God is one Lord:","30":"And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind, and with all thy strength: this is the first commandment.","31":"And the second is like, namely this, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. There is none other commandment greater than these.","32":"And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, thou hast said the truth: for there is one God; and there is none other but he:","33":"And to love him with all the heart, and with all the understanding, and with all the soul, and with all the strength, and to love his neighbour as himself, is more than all whole burnt offerings and sacrifices.","34":"And when Jesus saw that he answered discreetly, he said unto him, Thou art not far from the kingdom of God. And no man after that durst ask him any question.","35":"And Jesus answered and said, while he taught in the temple, How say the scribes that Christ is the son of David?","36":"For David himself said by the Holy Ghost, The Lord said to my Lord, Sit thou on my right hand, till I make thine enemies thy footstool.","37":"David therefore himself calleth him Lord; and whence is he then his son? And the common people heard him gladly.","38":"And he said unto them in his doctrine, Beware of the scribes, which love to go in long clothing, and love salutations in the marketplaces,","39":"And the chief seats in the synagogues, and the uppermost rooms at feasts:","40":"Which devour widows' houses, and for a pretence make long prayers: these shall receive greater damnation.","41":"And Jesus sat over against the treasury, and beheld how the people cast money into the treasury: and many that were rich cast in much.","42":"And there came a certain poor widow, and she threw in two mites, which make a farthing.","43":"And he called unto him his disciples, and saith unto them, Verily I say unto you, That this poor widow hath cast more in, than all they which have cast into the treasury:","44":"For all they did cast in of their abundance; but she of her want did cast in all that she had, even all her living."}},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: It has been a long Tuesday for Jesus and His disciples in these past few chapters. But Jesus is spending this day specifically preparing His people. He has prepared the people about how to battle tough questions, what will ultimately happen to Him in a few days, and has also used simple stories to get people to understand the problems."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Jesus continues to teach, but this will be spent on what happens after He has come back to life. He talks about distant future promises, as well as signs that will take place as the end comes nearer and nearer."}],"previously":["It has been a long Tuesday for Jesus and His disciples in these past few chapters. But Jesus is spending this day specifically preparing His people. He has prepared the people about how to battle tough questions, what will ultimately happen to Him in a few days, and has also used simple stories to get people to understand the problems. In this chapter, Jesus continues to teach, but this will be spent on what happens after He has come back to life. He talks about distant future promises, as well as signs that will take place as the end comes nearer and nearer."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"A Future Destruction","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I’m sure these buildings were quite impressive. Jerusalem was a central hub for the Jews in Israel, and they had a lot of royalty in and out of that location. They had a lot of wealth at one time or another. I imagine that Jerusalem, during the time of Solomon, looked even better."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is about to switch gears a little bit here. Of course, we get a bit of a deeper picture of this in the book of Matthew, but Peter seems to make a summation of everything (once again because he’s focused on the actions of Christ). The buildings were beautiful, but they will not last."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, they were great. However, nothing is greater than what you are to expect in eternity. Notice that Jesus never speaks about cleaning up anything in Heaven. We have seen that there are plenty of things to do in Heaven, but cleaning up is not something that is spoken about. Jesus talks about cleaning up quite a bit here on Earth. He talks about effort and work here that exceeds our desires and abilities."},{"kind":"para","text":"We use machines to make buildings today. We can make skyscrapers, domes, auditoriums, and all sorts of interesting-looking buildings. We could go to somewhere like LA and say, “My, what great buildings! Look at how beautiful this architecture is.” Jesus brings them to reality, though. He talks about a future event that will bring down not just these buildings, but every building."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":8,"title":"The Beginning of Sorrows","entries":[{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have Andrew involved in the conversation here. Andrew isn’t usually involved when these three are around. It’s usually just Peter, James, and John. Somehow, Andrew (Peter’s brother) came with them. This question is best addressed with all the disciples, but these four are getting to Christ before the others. It’s a fair enough question, though. “Will there be any warnings before destruction comes? How are we going to know when it’s time for the buildings to fall? Will there be a sign?”"}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That’s the business of this world. Sadly, it’s the business of some “ministers” as well. The Pharisees and the religionists of Jesus’ day were very keen on trying to deceive people. They wanted money and power over weaker people, and the best way to do that is to “deceive.” They would make up their own laws and ideas of religion so that people would have to pay a fee to keep traditions or suffer the judgment of breaking the law. So Jesus is going to tell them something before someone can trick them into believing something else. That is what Jesus is very focused on doing this Holy Week."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s not forget, we are still only on Tuesday of Holy Week. He has spent the majority of this day teaching about what is to come, and this chapter has been no different. The disciples will learn so that no one else can tell them otherwise. If they heard it from the Christ, they can take it to the bank that it will be absolutely 100% true."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"During the last days, Jesus says people will claim themselves as Christ. Now THAT is blasphemy. The Pharisees were trying to call Jesus out as a blasphemer, but Jesus had the right to call Himself the Christ because He IS the Christ. That term will just be thrown around in the future, though. We have had a few nut-jobs in our world who have tried to claim they are the Christ. There have been some who certainly don’t mean anything by it but to mock Christians and Christ specifically. There are others, though, who genuinely think they are the Christ, or some sort of variant of Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Charles Manson was one of those people. He tried to use his authority to trick people into believing that he actually was Christ in the flesh. Sadly, due to drugs and money, they believed it. They followed him, even to being commanded to murder people. Anyone who reads their Bibles, however, cannot be tricked so easily. This (Jesus) is Christ. And His second coming will be so obvious that people won’t have to question “is that the Christ?” No. Everyone will know."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of things in heaven, and things in earth, and things under the earth; And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.","ref":"Philippians 2:10-11"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There will be wars and rumors of wars before Christ returns. That is another clue. I assume there will be war like we have never seen before coming in the nearby future. Our world is primed for the return of Christ. We have seen a lot of wars popping up and causing problems in our world today. We have also gotten a lot of rumors about wars popping up as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Social media is a good source for rumors. You can hear about all sorts of fighting, killing, and political agendas right from the comfort of your own home. But even still, that will not be the final account. There are still some things that need to take place before the destruction."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The beginning of sorrows is shown in earthquakes, famines, trouble, and war. Earthquakes are nothing new to us Californians. At this point, famines and trouble are not particularly new either, with the events of COVID still causing problems for people who are afraid to get sick. I also don’t think there has ever been a time since I’ve been alive that nations haven’t faced a war of some sort in this world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Strangely enough, in the end, we get a view of nations actually supporting each other. They are helping each other so they can grow one particular city that will try and overthrow the Lord. Obviously, it doesn’t work, and the nation is dealt with pretty quickly."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he cried mightily with a strong voice, saying, Babylon the great is fallen, is fallen, and is become the habitation of devils, and the hold of every foul spirit, and a cage of every unclean and hateful bird.","ref":"Revelation 18:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"We can study that better in Revelation. We are told that the city will be quite the spectacle, though. Everyone will be surprised to learn that this city has fallen because of how glorious and powerful it was. All nations gave to that city, and then it was suddenly gone. But before that, nations will be fighting. It's sad that it will take a world of hate against the Lord to finally unite everyone for a season."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":13,"title":"Hated By All","entries":[{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a two-fold revelation. Firstly, the disciples will literally be publishing the gospel, and they will all literally be martyred for their faith. They will all be literally delivered up to councils, and they will all be literally beaten. They will literally be brought before rulers and kings just because they are following Christ by faith. They will have the opportunity to share the gospel with very high powers and will initiate the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then we have a future promise as well. There will be Christians during the beginning of the tribulation who will be suffering the same fate. I believe even here in America, we will suffer that fate. We have the freedom here to speak about these things today, but there will be a day when people will reject your freedom to do so and will cause great harm to you. It’s already started if I can be honest. They will be having public killings right on the streets of your neighborhood, and the victims will be the Christians who preach Christ. Even if your faith is personal, and you don’t go on sharing it, if they ever find out that you believe in Christ, they will attack you too. That is going to happen whether we like it or not. That is the gospel message."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus has spent some time talking about the “kingdom of Heaven.” The kingdom (at least to the Lord) is at hand. Now imagine that message today. It seems even closer today than it does back then. It will sound even closer in the future when people are being persecuted for the faith. We know that the gospel message (as people become more and more hateful) will only become more and more accurate and powerful."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are many times when people come to me with questions that I don’t know how to answer. Every single time that happens, the Lord gives me an answer on the spot. He brings something to mind. Whether it’s a verse of scripture, a story from my past, or a use-case that is sitting next to me, God always provides a means to present the gospel in some way. Things might be a little different when your life is hanging in the balance. It might seem as if all hope is lost when you are before world leaders threatening to kill you for your faith. You won’t have much time to plan out a sermon or defense. You will just have to trust that the Lord is going to give you the words to say."},{"kind":"para","text":"According to Jesus, God’s Spirit will grant that power to His children. We will all have the ability to think on our toes when we are offered up for our faith. I believe it is this particular verse that is the reason why the disciples had no problem standing before very powerful men with death following close behind. It doesn’t seem they have any nerves about it either. We could see in Acts that James is beheaded, and Peter is to be killed shortly after, and yet Peter doesn’t seem to be too worried about it. When we see Peter, he is sleeping chained to two guards. Of course, we don’t know the thoughts of the disciples during those moments, but we get the outsider’s view of it (unless the disciple actually gives detail to the thoughts). If I knew that I was going to die any moment, I would not have the ability to sleep, let alone the desire for it. But every Apostle seems to have a comfort of the Lord during their last moments (or what seems to be their last moments). Even Stephen, while people were stoning and literally biting him, found the right words to say:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When they heard these things, they were cut to the heart, and they gnashed on him with their teeth. But he, being full of the Holy Ghost, looked up stedfastly into heaven, and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing on the right hand of God, And said, Behold, I see the heavens opened, and the Son of man standing on the right hand of God. Then they cried out with a loud voice, and stopped their ears, and ran upon him with one accord, And cast him out of the city, and stoned him: and the witnesses laid down their clothes at a young man's feet, whose name was Saul. And they stoned Stephen, calling upon God, and saying, Lord Jesus, receive my spirit. And he kneeled down, and cried with a loud voice, Lord, lay not this sin to their charge. And when he had said this, he fell asleep.","ref":"Acts 7:54-60"},{"kind":"para","text":"All the other Apostles have the same testimony. Even Jesus, as we will see soon enough. If it were me, I don't know if I would be thinking very positively towards my attackers. Perhaps it will be different in the moment."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is more tragic than nation against nation. We will have family member against family member. Our current state in this country has proven how accurate this verse is. We have people so divided in America today, and the spectrum lands on two political choices. Politics is the determining factor of many familial divisions today. That’s the silliest sentence I’ve ever had to write. You would think it would be religious divisions, but it’s not. Though I could argue that most of the politics that one chooses is strongly influenced by religious ideology."},{"kind":"para","text":"My dad used to always say that every war is a “holy war.” He believed everything was a fight stemmed from a religion. He’s not entirely wrong, but not entirely right either. The liberal atheist will think that way, that is for sure. They have not been quiet about their hate for Christianity and conservatism. But that is not how the Christian thinks of it (or at least they shouldn’t). The Christian must think of it as a war on truth. One side is against the truth, the other side is for the truth. That is what will split a nation and a family. That is ultimately what is bringing division into lives today. It’s not about politics; it’s about what is absolute and what is relative truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you don’t believe that truth is absolute, you will always be on the wrong side of the battle. That is what Jesus is getting at in these past few chapters, and it’s what He is hitting home here. People should die for absolute truth. Honestly, that is the only thing that people are smart to die for. If anyone dies for personal (or relative) truth, their truth dies with them. Personal truth cannot be shared with others. Absolute truth lives on even if you are dead. The truth doesn’t depend on the person telling it. The truth depends on evidence and unwavering answers. That is what your faith should be hinged upon."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is ultimately what measures a man’s faith: his endurance to follow the Lord even to the end. Many people have given up their faith because they don’t want to deal with the pressure of being an outcast to the world. The world has a lot of riches, and that is how we started this chapter. We saw the riches of the world and its means to try and entice a person into giving up their following of Christ so they can live comfortably while they are here."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Christian has comforts, by the way. I’m not saying it’s either follow Christ or be comfortable. But if that was the only means to following Christ (if I had to let go of any and all comfort that I can have in life), then it would be worth it to follow Him. The riches of this world are only temporary. That is what Jesus is getting at. The riches of eternity are eternal; they never go away. We will be hated for following Christ; that is the bottom line. People don’t even have to know the kind of person I am to hate me. All they have to know is that I’m a Christian, and they will automatically hate me."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, I’ve had people tell me that. I told them that I loved them (that was true and genuine), and they told me that they hate me because all Christians are the same. That’s the sad reality we live in. The world’s riches and comforts are never worth giving up your faith for. If you can endure, even through the hatred of mankind, the rewards are always greater, both here and in eternity."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":23,"title":"The Abomination of Desolation","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The abomination of desolation will be a power that shouldn’t be where it is. That is Satan. He will bring peace to this world, but it’s a false peace. It will get people comfortable. He will come into the temple and claim himself as god and king. He will make himself the image of power, and that will be a very great sign to everyone who is God’s children that he is the anti-Christ. Jesus says he will stand in “the holy place.” Where is the holy place? Well, I don’t know. We only get information about a “Holy place” being present in Israel through scripture. It is likely that this will be where the holy place will be again in the future."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, the blood of it was not brought in within the holy place: ye should indeed have eaten it in the holy place, as I commanded.","ref":"Leviticus 10:18"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the priests brought in the ark of the covenant of the LORD unto his place, into the oracle of the house, to the most holy place, even under the wings of the cherubims.","ref":"1 Kings 8:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For thus saith the high and lofty One that inhabiteth eternity, whose name is Holy; I dwell in the high and holy place, with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit, to revive the spirit of the humble, and to revive the heart of the contrite ones.","ref":"Isaiah 57:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I don’t believe Jesus is speaking about the throne of Heaven. Satan will not be able to take that place. So, Satan will dwell in a holy place here on Earth. The church does not have a “Holy place” given to it. We are God’s church. We are the vessel of the Holy Spirit of God. It is not the believer that Satan will have control over. So, it must be an obvious location that Satan will be in, and the Christians will know and be assured that he is not supposed to be there."}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When this happens, we must leave. We must separate ourselves from the culture. The rest of the world is going to follow the antichrist, and the Christian will have no place to turn to but hiding. Where does a Christian run when this happens? Well, it happened once before during the Roman invasion. There were many Christians being killed, and whole cities were burned down by evil leaders. During that time, they ran to the mountains to hide. Most of them were saved when they did. That would be the only place a Christian might go. There will be nowhere else to really turn to. The mountains would be our best bet. Camping and staying low would be the safest option."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when they had brought them forth abroad, that he said, Escape for thy life; look not behind thee, neither stay thou in all the plain; escape to the mountain, lest thou be consumed.","ref":"Genesis 19:17"}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You’re not going to run very fast if you are pregnant and this happens. You also won’t be able to easily maneuver in the snow. It would be bad news to people who have to go through this realization and have to run for their lives before it’s too late. I imagine that when Satan comes into power, he is not going to be kind to Christians. He will search for them, and he will do whatever it takes to start killing them (pregnant or not)."}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I can't imagine these days. I can't imagine the pain and suffering Christians will go through. Thankfully, God will \"shorten\" those days. He will end the suffering quickly. I believe He'll end it through the rapture, but that's just my personal belief. We can speak more about that in Revelation."}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The goal for people saying this during that time is to lure an innocent Christian out of hiding. Satan will pull out all the stops to find you and attack you and your family. Don't believe them. If Christ truly has come, He will come to you. He knows where you are. He will personally take you home. You do not have to go seek Him. He will seek you."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now you know. There is no reason to question it. The Apostles will get deeper into these things later in Scripture, and it seems the Holy Spirit will reveal more than what is being written here. But this is a place for the disciples to start, and it's a lesson for them to learn about the things that are about to come. If the death of Christ isn't enough for them to take His Words seriously, His resurrection should be. If not that, their own near-death experiences should get them thinking pretty deeply about the things of eternity and the future of tribulation."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":27,"title":"The Lord Returns","entries":[{"start":24,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is how the rapture will occur. There will be some beginning pains. People will have a very obvious sign that the Lord is coming. The sun will be darkened, and the moon will not shine like it did before. Stars will start falling, and everything will be very different. The air will just feel different. But before those things come to pass, the rapture will take place. There will be people being killed by the antichrist, and then the rapture occurs, and “after that tribulation” that’s when the sun and moon lose their lights and the rest comes to fruition."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That's the comfort. That's what makes endurance worth it to the Christian. That is our victory sight. Satan has the world’s throne, but the Lord has the throne of a Christian’s heart. He is our ruler, not anyone on this Earth. Satan may kill many of us, but the Lord will return for those that are here and take down the ruling of Satan. Jesus will eventually throw down Satan's power and foothold, and lock him up for a time."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will be gathered by the Lord before the “Great Tribulation.” The Great Tribulation is given to those who do not know the Lord before the rapture. Again, this is something we can discuss in Revelation, but Jesus provides us with some pretty valid details about what to expect. We are going to face the beginning pains of tribulation, but we are not going to be a part of the “Great” Tribulation. That is reserved for the godless."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, this portion of scripture is not talking about the rapture. It is discussing when Jesus returns to reign for a thousand years. He will gather the remaining Christians, those who were saved during the Great Tribulation era, and they will rule with Him at that time. It is after this that Christ will put an end to sin and evil forever."}]}]},{"start":28,"end":31,"title":"Signs to Come","entries":[{"start":28,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You will see obvious signs of the Lord’s return. It still baffles me that there are plenty of people today who try to reject scripture as being relevant and accurate. Everything that scripture says will happen has already started taking place. We have seen it come to fruition in one way or another, and it will only get worse. Scripture, in fact, tells us it will get worse."},{"kind":"para","text":"If I were not already a Christian today, I would certainly be looking at current events and thinking to myself, “Something is wrong. There is some sort of supernatural force behind all of this, that is obvious.” I’m not that smart. If I can figure it out, I have no doubt an atheist can figure it out."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The believers will see the pains. I know the Apostles saw pains. The moment the Holy Ghost was given to the Apostles, the world entered into the \"Last days.\" We are living in the last days. How long the last days will be, I cannot say. But we have not seen the signs of everything yet to know whether or not the Lord is ready to call us home. We are still waiting on some of these signs to come to pass."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is another comforting thought to the Christian. This thought also gives us endurance. His Word lasts forever. Our actions, thoughts, and presence will not last forever, but God's Word does. That is something we can depend on for all of eternity."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":37,"title":"Watch","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Only God knows when the signs will start to be evident. We must wait for His approval of it. He has everything planned for a specific time and reason, and that is to be respected. We cannot rush it along, and we shouldn't even want to. I'd rather wait for the Lord to make the decision than try and just get it over with. I am confident that He knows what is best."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is because we don’t know when that we should be more earnest and mindful about eternity and sharing Christ. That is another sign of endurance. If we knew when the Lord was going to return, we probably wouldn’t worry about staying the course with Him nearly as much. We could just wait to do anything worth it for the Lord because we know He is coming soon. But because we don’t know, it is possible that He may not come again during our lifetime."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a wasted life it would be if we just sat and waited for the Lord and did nothing to serve Him. It would be a wasted life if we ignored His commission to us and just lived shallow, dull, and selfish lives thinking that He will return any moment so we shouldn’t bother. We must be pretty sick and twisted to believe in the reality of Hell and yet not have a love or compassion for people enough to tell them about it. We don’t know when He will return, and that should give us fervency to reach as many people as possible in case He comes tomorrow. In fact, that might be a good way to look at it. Jesus is coming tomorrow. That’s how we should live our lives. We should know that Jesus is coming tomorrow."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This draws us back to the vineyard from our last chapter. Jesus is the owner of the field and the Son who was killed. He is the Son who will die by His own kind, and He is the owner that will leave to a far country. He can be both because He will rise again three days after His death."}]},{"start":35,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus speaks to us as well. He is telling every human being to watch. Whether we believe the words of Jesus or not is irrelevant. The Lord has commissioned mankind to watch, and that is something we should be taking seriously. We should be watching. We should be paying attention. If we are smart people, we would take the already obvious signs of the Lord's coming to heart today and use that as a very serious and sobering command to clean up and turn to God."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":37}],"verses":{"1":"And as he went out of the temple, one of his disciples saith unto him, Master, see what manner of stones and what buildings are here!","2":"And Jesus answering said unto him, Seest thou these great buildings? there shall not be left one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down.","3":"And as he sat upon the mount of Olives over against the temple, Peter and James and John and Andrew asked him privately,","4":"Tell us, when shall these things be? and what shall be the sign when all these things shall be fulfilled?","5":"And Jesus answering them began to say, Take heed lest any man deceive you:","6":"For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Christ; and shall deceive many.","7":"And when ye shall hear of wars and rumours of wars, be ye not troubled: for such things must needs be; but the end shall not be yet.","8":"For nation shall rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom: and there shall be earthquakes in divers places, and there shall be famines and troubles: these are the beginnings of sorrows.","9":"But take heed to yourselves: for they shall deliver you up to councils; and in the synagogues ye shall be beaten: and ye shall be brought before rulers and kings for my sake, for a testimony against them.","10":"And the gospel must first be published among all nations.","11":"But when they shall lead you, and deliver you up, take no thought beforehand what ye shall speak, neither do ye premeditate: but whatsoever shall be given you in that hour, that speak ye: for it is not ye that speak, but the Holy Ghost.","12":"Now the brother shall betray the brother to death, and the father the son; and children shall rise up against their parents, and shall cause them to be put to death.","13":"And ye shall be hated of all men for my name's sake: but he that shall endure unto the end, the same shall be saved.","14":"But when ye shall see the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet, standing where it ought not, (let him that readeth understand,) then let them that be in Judaea flee to the mountains:","15":"And let him that is on the housetop not go down into the house, neither enter therein, to take any thing out of his house:","16":"And let him that is in the field not turn back again for to take up his garment.","17":"But woe to them that are with child, and to them that give suck in those days!","18":"And pray ye that your flight be not in the winter.","19":"For in those days shall be affliction, such as was not from the beginning of the creation which God created unto this time, neither shall be.","20":"And except that the Lord had shortened those days, no flesh should be saved: but for the elect's sake, whom he hath chosen, he hath shortened the days.","21":"And then if any man shall say to you, Lo, here is Christ; or, lo, he is there; believe him not:","22":"For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.","23":"But take ye heed: behold, I have foretold you all things.","24":"But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,","25":"And the stars of heaven shall fall, and the powers that are in heaven shall be shaken.","26":"And then shall they see the Son of man coming in the clouds with great power and glory.","27":"And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.","28":"Now learn a parable of the fig tree; When her branch is yet tender, and putteth forth leaves, ye know that summer is near:","29":"So ye in like manner, when ye shall see these things come to pass, know that it is nigh, even at the doors.","30":"Verily I say unto you, that this generation shall not pass, till all these things be done.","31":"Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.","32":"But of that day and that hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels which are in heaven, neither the Son, but the Father.","33":"Take ye heed, watch and pray: for ye know not when the time is.","34":"For the Son of Man is as a man taking a far journey, who left his house, and gave authority to his servants, and to every man his work, and commanded the porter to watch.","35":"Watch ye therefore: for ye know not when the master of the house cometh, at even, or at midnight, or at the cockcrowing, or in the morning:","36":"Lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping.","37":"And what I say unto you I say unto all, Watch."}},
    "14": {"chapter":14,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we finally get the conclusion of Tuesday's lessons. He spoke very strongly about the tribulation period, and what to expect when the end is coming near. God's children will be killed, and Satan will take power in places that he should not be. He thinks he will somehow overthrow the Lord's leadership, but it will be proven how weak he really is when God stops him quickly."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we move on to the rest of the week. They eat the passover, and then Judas comes to betray Jesus. We finish up this chapter with Peter weeping bitterly over his denial of the Savior, and a very bleak look at Jesus' trial at the temple in front of the religionists who are looking to try and kill Him."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, we finally get the conclusion of Tuesday's lessons. He spoke very strongly about the tribulation period, and what to expect when the end is coming near. God's children will be killed, and Satan will take power in places that he should not be. He thinks he will somehow overthrow the Lord's leadership, but it will be proven how weak he really is when God stops him quickly. In this chapter, we move on to the rest of the week. They eat the Passover, and then Judas comes to betray Jesus. We finish up this chapter with Peter weeping bitterly over his denial of the Savior, and a very bleak look at Jesus' trial at the temple in front of the religionists who are looking to try and kill Him."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Wednesday","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Passover always takes place on the fourteenth day of the \"first month,\" which correlates to April in our modern calendars. It was a day of salvation. A day of remembrance for the Jews. A day that should be focused on the Lord and gratitude towards Him. It has been a day that was lost since the Jews had given up their hearts for God's presence and only lived for God's law. That is the reason that many religionists still observe the Passover today. It all has to do with law, not with love."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't want to deal with the hassle of people on a \"feast\" day. So they would have to figure out a way to murder an innocent man before that day. They would have to kill Jesus before their day of rest. The feast day would be on the fifteenth day. They didn't care about killing a man, they just cared about what the people might think. This was a sacred time for the Jews. They would be traveling from all around to come worship and celebrate the Lord's salvation, and so the streets of Jerusalem (specifically) would be littered with all walks of life. There would certainly be a lot of people to witness this occurrence."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":9,"title":"Anointing Jesus","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"According to John, this took place six days before the Passover. So it's likely that this happened on the first day of the week, which would have been Sunday."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Jesus six days before the passover came to Bethany, where Lazarus was which had been dead, whom he raised from the dead.","ref":"John 12:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Mark placed this here to give more depth to the situation that is about to take place (namely, with Judas). This is a lighthearted moment, and it was another obvious symbol of preparation. It still seems as if no one understands what is about to happen to Jesus, but Jesus keeps giving them reminders."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John says that it's Judas primarily that makes this a bigger deal than it needed to be. Peter tells us that there were \"some\" of the people who didn't agree with this. I assume that Judas was probably the one behind pointing it out at first, and other people just followed behind him in agreement."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had no intention of giving this money to the poor. They knew that. Jesus knows that. They wanted the money for themselves. Money is a big problem in our world. That is everything that people think about. The reason they go to work is for money. The reason they have children is for money. The reason they try and serve at their church (if I can be so bold) is for money. That is not a good reason to live. Money fails. They were going to regret this dispute and murmur in a few days."}]},{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This lady did \"a good work\" for the Lord. She did it out of her heart’s desire, not out of necessity. That is what makes it a \"good\" work. There are a lot of very meaningful, good-hearted servants of the Lord in the church today, yet they are always pushed into the background. Sadly, far more often than not, the ministers push them to the back because the ministers are not the ones getting recognition. What a sleazy and shameful thing for any leader to do."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Listen, I am not the brightest and best. I don’t know what I’m doing half the time in ministry. I’m learning as I go. All we can do is our “best.” This lady did what she could. She is anointing the Lord’s body, and I’m not even 100% sure if she knows what that really means. We know what it means because we know what is to come. But the disciples still haven’t figured it out yet. All we can do is the best we can do. That’s really all the Lord looks for."},{"kind":"para","text":"People want to do great big things, but the Lord doesn’t care about that. He just cares about you doing what you can. A church that stops its ministers from doing what they can is no church at all. Again, I say, all we can do is what we can do."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"She is part of the gospel message according to Jesus. Most people ignore this small bit of Christ being anointed for His burial. Yet, it’s something that is brought up in the gospel accounts. She is part of helping fulfill this ministry of salvation by the Lord. That is what we all should be a part of. We should all be helping to fulfill the gospel ministry of the Lord, not trying to rob people who are doing it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t see anyone encouraging people to anoint the Lord anymore. I see them act the part, but they aren’t really interested in doing it. They are more like Judas, just complaining about something that he doesn’t actually even care about. It’s her ointment; she can do what she wants with it."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"title":"Ready To Betray","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This takes place on Wednesday. We started off the day with the religionists already trying to figure out a way to go after Jesus. They needed to pin something on Him. The best thing they could do was pin false accusations on the Lord, because the Lord did nothing wrong. There is a massive crowd of people that would testify against what these religionists might accuse Jesus of, but if it's a close friend to Jesus... Well, that's a different story. It could be argued that the reason Judas suddenly felt this way was because of the incident with the box of ointment. He didn't get his money, so he figured he would get it a different way."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Conveniently betray him.” That’s rich. When you are betraying someone, hardly ever is it convenient for either side. The only thing that is convenient in betrayal are the lies that are told. You won’t have to guess which one is lying. Obviously, it’s the one doing the betraying. This was good for the religionists because it gave them a way in. It gave them a testimony against the Lord. The crowds might deny what the religionists say against Jesus, but if a close friend agrees with them, it turns the table. Judas was only in this for the money. That is a common theme with false ministers today as well. If they are satisfied with their paycheck at the church and don’t have to deal with the actual “ministering” part, then you know they are betrayers. They will have no problem throwing you under the bus so they can fill their pockets. Never trust a man who looks for a paycheck to minister. This ends their Wednesday. This is a sad Wednesday. The rest of the week is all downhill from here…"}]}]},{"start":12,"end":16,"title":"Thursday","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps we should get the events of the Passover in our noggins before continuing forward."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And this day shall be unto you for a memorial; and ye shall keep it a feast to the LORD throughout your generations; ye shall keep it a feast by an ordinance for ever. Seven days shall ye eat unleavened bread; even the first day ye shall put away leaven out of your houses: for whosoever eateth leavened bread from the first day until the seventh day, that soul shall be cut off from Israel. And in the first day there shall be an holy convocation, and in the seventh day there shall be an holy convocation to you; no manner of work shall be done in them, save that which every man must eat, that only may be done of you. And ye shall observe the feast of unleavened bread; for in this selfsame day have I brought your armies out of the land of Egypt: therefore shall ye observe this day in your generations by an ordinance for ever. In the first month, on the fourteenth day of the month at even, ye shall eat unleavened bread, until the one and twentieth day of the month at even. Seven days shall there be no leaven found in your houses: for whosoever eateth that which is leavened, even that soul shall be cut off from the congregation of Israel, whether he be a stranger, or born in the land. Ye shall eat nothing leavened; in all your habitations shall ye eat unleavened bread.","ref":"Exodus 12:14-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the same routine that will follow here. Except it will be Jesus that fulfills the Passover fairly shortly here to come. Tomorrow is not going to be a joyous day for the disciples nor for the world. The world will literally get darker from what is about to happen."}]},{"start":13,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s once again possible that Jesus had already prepared this beforehand. I like to believe He didn't. He just simply knew who would be accepting of the Lord and His disciples. He knew there would be a man carrying some water, and that was the man that the disciples would be able to talk to. This man would be welcoming. There were some outside of the disciples who truly believed in the Lord and were willing to prepare a place for Him. That's still a rare thing today. Not many are preparing a place for Christ. That is a very sad thought, especially when we get into Friday."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It happened just as Jesus said. That is a good rule of thumb for us to live by. If Jesus says it will happen, then you are assured it will happen exactly as He has said. There should be no doubting that Jesus knows everything."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":21,"title":"Eating The Passover","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a bittersweet last supper. Their meal is a representation of Christ’s sacrifice, which we will discuss in a moment. They may not remember a great deal of what Jesus said, but they will certainly remember Jesus’ last meal. I believe that is why the Lord came and died in this way. He didn’t just say a bunch of Heavenly things and then go home. No, He PROVED those same Heavenly things. He PROVED that the kingdom of God is at hand. He lived His life as if He truly meant that God was there. Everyone saw it and believed it because it was so obvious and evident to them. It was impossible to deny the cold hard facts right in front of their faces."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is no longer hiding this. I don't think Jesus ever really did hide the idea that one of them would betray them. But it was this night that the betrayal would actually take place. Actually, the betrayal had already taken place. Judas just needed to finish a few things before he could collect his money and get Jesus arrested. He had to stay close to Jesus until the time was right."}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a fair question to ask ourselves. I know a lot of us are pretty confident in our abilities to stay on par with the Lord. We are consistent. Perhaps we have been for years. But we are not invincible. We could easily fall away like Judas if we are not careful."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I don’t believe Judas was ever saved. I honestly think that Judas had an idea about what salvation was, but he never really understood it. He never actually took it to himself. He might have tricked himself into believing he did, but he never actually believed the Lord. He saw the miracles, yes, but he also saw evil in this world. He let his greed keep him from loving the Lord. He loved money more than God. That is the same trick that everyone on Earth seems to fall for."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"One could argue that the Lord allowed the birth of Judas just for this specific reason. I don’t believe so. I believe the Lord allowed Judas hundreds of chances to turn to Him, but Judas never did. I believe Jesus deeply loved him and wanted more than anything for Judas to see what it was that he was about to do. There would be someone who had to betray Jesus. No matter what, honestly, I believe that Jesus was going to go to the cross. Judas didn’t have to be the one to betray Him in order for Him to get there, but the Lord knew that this was all going to come down to this."},{"kind":"para","text":"If I were Judas and I heard the Lord say this, I would second-guess my decision to betray the Lord. I would go back on my word about turning Him over. That would, however, make Judas a traitor and it would have gotten him arrested instead. David gives a picture into the mind of Christ in this moment. This is what Jesus was thinking:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea, mine own familiar friend, in whom I trusted, which did eat of my bread, hath lifted up his heel against me.","ref":"Psalm 41:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, there are many \"friends\" in our world today who have no problem betraying you to get what they want. They would easily throw you under the bus to keep their position or to fill their pockets. We must be careful with the people we love the most; they are the ones that can hurt you the most."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":25,"title":"The Bread and Cup","entries":[{"start":22,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Every year now, when they observe the Passover, this will be the memory they think of until their own deaths. It's a connection between the promise of salvation in the Old Testament and a promise of salvation in the New. I think there is ample reason to speak about this in 1 Corinthians later on, but I want to focus on one particular thing, and that is the cup:"}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus says that he will not drink from the \"fruit of the vine\" until He is in Heaven. We understand that this isn't water they were drinking. There are a lot of scholars who believe this is wine. I won't debate them on it. I don't know what it is, but it certainly isn't just water. I believe it is not fermented, though. There was no leaven in the bread, so I don't believe there was leaven in the grapes. That is intentional. Leaven is a symbol of sin."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A little leaven leaveneth the whole lump.","ref":"Galatians 5:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"That was why they are not adding it to the Passover. The Israelites were in too much of a hurry to sin at that moment in time. They had to prepare their bread and drinks in a hurry so they could leave."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":31,"title":"Laying Down Their Lives","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very sweet moment as well. A somber moment. I don't know what hymn they sang, but I could bet any money that it was referring to the Lord's death. I think that for a few reasons. One reason is that He is about to be betrayed, and everything in this chapter (thus far) and on this day (Thursday) has been a reminder of what is looming on the horizon. The other reason is that He continues to talk about it in the next verse:"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If it's a happy hymn, Jesus just killed the whole mood. He has been in a constant reminder to them about what is coming, and they would do well to take heed to His words here. They, of course, don't, and they will learn the hard way that Jesus is serious about the events that are about to unfold."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He makes it clear that He will rise again. He is going to die. It will be a painful death. But He never leaves the gospel message there. He always finishes it with \"I will rise again.\" That is important for us to note, because we do a disservice to witnessing to others if we are leaving that very important note out. We must not just preach about the death of Christ. We must preach about the resurrection of Christ, which is the power of the Kingdom of Heaven (that is at hand)."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Bold! Peter certainly means well here. I think we should get that straight. He is not saying this in defiance. He is saying it in love and passion for the Lord. He genuinely loves the Lord and is willing to stand by Him. At least that is what He thinks. It will not just be Judas who betrays Jesus on this night. They will ALL be offended and run. Judas will initiate the betrayal, but they will all run in confusion because of it. There is a little more to this conversation than what Peter is leading on here, though."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another. By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another. Simon Peter said unto him, Lord, whither goest thou? Jesus answered him, Whither I go, thou canst not follow me now; but thou shalt follow me afterwards. Peter said unto him, Lord, why cannot I follow thee now? I will lay down my life for thy sake. Jesus answered him, Wilt thou lay down thy life for my sake? Verily, verily, I say unto thee, The cock shall not crow, till thou hast denied me thrice.","ref":"John 13:34-38"},{"kind":"para","text":"According to John, Peter said he was going to literally lay down his life for the Lord. Mark seems to leave that note out here, though. I think Peter was trying to figure out how to leave this part of the story out, but it will ultimately lead to the greater testimony of Peter. So he gives the bare minimum details about it here. The important part, anyway, is the prediction that Jesus makes in the next verse."}]},{"start":30,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They all pay tribute to Jesus, but Jesus knows that they do not understand what they mean. They think that Jesus is going to be completely okay. They are under the impression that He is going to walk away from this with power and vengeance. Little do they know that their Savior is about to be placed on the cross and killed right in front of their eyes. They will not be laying down their lives for the Lord. They will have to receive the Lord laying down His own life for them. They will not be able to make the sacrifice they think they will. It will only be Christ who is sacrificed here. They should have realized that at the supper, or even in the hymn they sang. There is a connection to salvation here, and they were all missing it. Jesus says that Peter will remember this moment, though. There will be a ringing rooster that will catch Peter’s attention."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":42,"title":"A Pressing Prayer","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gethsemane\" means \"An oil press.\" It's a place where the Lord is going to surely feel pressed about the events about to come. This is placed right near the Mount of Olives. It must have been a beautiful place to pray because the Lord keeps coming back here. That is a pretty common theme in scripture, by the way. Gardens are a place of meditation and comfort to the Lord. Perhaps that is how we should ALL view gardens."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His inner three: Peter, James, and John will be testimonies to this specific situation and prayer. The Lord was feeling the pain of the cross before He was sent there. He knows what is coming."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Have you had such a heavy soul that you felt like you could die? I've had that feeling a few times, but not like the Lord is experiencing here. My troubles and trials of exceeding sorrow seem so small in comparison. That's with thoughts of suicide and depression in my background. The Lord is carrying a heavier burden than the whole world could bear together. He carries it on His own."}]},{"start":35,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a powerful prayer. Jesus knows the pain that is coming, and He asks His Father if there is another way. Yet, no matter what, He is going to do what the Father knows is best. It's GOD'S Will. Our prayers should be God-focused too. We should be praying in the same way. \"Lord, not my will today, please. YOUR Will today. May it be YOU who controls every movement and circumstance, whether I like it or not. Let me depend on YOU.\""}]},{"start":37,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He speaks to Simon specifically. Or at least that is what Peter says. Obviously, they have had a very long trip these past three years, and this whole week has been a heavy week. The Spirit really is ready, but our flesh will always fight against the Spirit. Sadly, our flesh seems to win out more than our Spirit. We must be considerate of this, otherwise, we will fall into \"temptation\" or \"trial.\" Their flesh wins over while the Lord goes to pray:"}]},{"start":39,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, one of two things had to happen here. Either they heard a very small portion of the prayer, and that is why they know that He said the same thing three times, and they just fell asleep before hearing the rest. Or the Holy Spirit has revealed the prayer to them. I find it funny if it’s the second, and this is the length of Christ’s prayer. This would mean that they fell asleep almost instantly three times. Peter was pretty good at falling asleep, though. It seems like every time we see him, he finds a place to take a nap. Obviously, that is not the case. Some of the prayer that is prayed is missing for sure. I know this to be true because Jesus asked, “Couldest not thou watch one hour?” So obviously, Jesus had an hour’s worth of prayer, three times. So Jesus prayed for three hours. But this is the moment. There is no more delay, and no more waiting. We are coming to the night of betrayal."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The disciples still didn't get it. But they were about to understand right now."}]}]},{"start":43,"end":45,"title":"Kiss the Son","entries":[{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"May we be reminded that Judas is considered “one of the twelve.” He has been considered that from the very beginning. He was one of them, a chosen man by the Lord. Yet, he is about to betray all of them as if he never actually belonged to them. They likely all never would have believed it was Judas who could do this. This had to be a bit of a whirlwind moment for the disciples, especially Peter. Peter was the one who claimed to lay down his life for the Lord, and he sees Judas with the religionists who have been well known at this point to have hated the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"This had to hurt everyone, but I think it genuinely hurt Peter. Of course, it hurt the Lord. The Lord knew it was coming, though. Peter didn’t, and he had to be so confused. He also knows that the Lord could get out of this pretty easily. But that’s not what Jesus is going to do. Peter will be disappointed in multiple ways this night."}]},{"start":44,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a sad moment. Once again, this \"Master\" title is misused. Multiple people have called Jesus Master at this point, and all of them have rejected Him as their actual Master. That title seems to have lost its meaning. Judas betrayed Jesus with a kiss. He could have chosen any means possible to point Jesus out, but for some reason, Judas chose to give Jesus one last bout of affection. He chose to give Jesus one of the most innocent forms of affection. A simple kiss."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Faithful are the wounds of a friend; but the kisses of an enemy are deceitful.","ref":"Proverbs 27:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Kisses aren't usually known for being evil, but this particular time marks (perhaps) the most evil betrayal the world has ever seen. Unknowingly, Judas helps to fulfill a prophecy by doing this. Furthermore, David speaks about a genuine kiss:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Kiss the Son, lest he be angry, and ye perish from the way, when his wrath is kindled but a little. Blessed are all they that put their trust in him.","ref":"Psalm 2:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, that is not how Judas kisses the Son... I believe Jesus would have rather been tortured by the religionists than to be betrayed by a kiss from His friend. It should also be noticed that Judas does not call Jesus \"Lord\" but rather \"Master.\" I believe that to be another stab at what he really thinks of Christ."}]}]},{"start":46,"end":50,"title":"A Cut To Hearts and Ears","entries":[{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Or at the very least they thought they \"took\" the Lord. Really, He just freely went with them."}]},{"start":47,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is funny. Which one of them cut off this man's ear? Well, Mark seems to omit the culprit. But John isn't afraid to call out who it is!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Simon Peter having a sword drew it, and smote the high priest's servant, and cut off his right ear. The servant's name was Malchus. Then said Jesus unto Peter, Put up thy sword into the sheath: the cup which my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?","ref":"John 18:10-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"I love that John and Matthew poke these little jabs at Peter every once in a while. Jesus heals the man's ear, but that doesn't seem to be mentioned here either."}]},{"start":48,"end":50,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The prophets spoke about this moment in history. This was meant to happen in this way. It was going to happen no matter what. They didn't need to make such a big scene; they could have just asked Jesus to go with them, and He would have. This was the moment for Him to give His life over into the \"hands of sinners.\" All of the disciples \"forsook\" the Lord and ran away at this moment. They did the opposite of what they said they would do if this moment came."}]}]},{"start":51,"end":52,"title":"A Certain Young Man","entries":[{"start":51,"end":52,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s some levity to a very bleak situation. What was this “certain young man” doing with them wearing nothing but a linen cloth? What was he doing moments before this? This is the only time this is mentioned, by the way. There are large debates over who this person could have been. I like the idea that it was John Mark (the one writing this account). He doesn’t give a name, but it’s a very interesting and odd detail to add to this recount. He ran somewhere with nothing on after this whole scene takes place. And that’s really all we need to know about that, I guess. I’m sure there’s a sermon somewhere here, but I can’t think of it. I suppose without the Lord, we are just as vulnerable as this certain young man. You can either follow the Lord with nothing or reject the Lord and have eternity with nothing."}]}]},{"start":53,"end":54,"title":"Good Friday","entries":[{"start":53,"end":53,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They think they are something special. Their titles mean a lot to them. They are known as their title, not as their names. They are the “chief priests,” “elders,” and “scribes.” That is who they are. That is their life. That is their purpose. That is their whole reason to exist. It’s sad and empty if I can be honest. From an outsider’s perspective, their job seems like a whole lot of work for nothing worthwhile. They get power, sure. They get money, okay. But they don’t have joy or love from the Lord. There is nothing more empty than that."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have come to Friday morning. It has been a long night for the Lord and His disciples. None of them has had any sleep. They won’t be getting any sleep either today…"}]},{"start":54,"end":54,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter was close by, but not too close as to be caught. He doesn't want to end up in jail with Jesus. He doesn't want to get into trouble. He did before, but he seems to have forgotten that at the moment. I will give him credit though. He is still concerned about his friend. He's concerned about his savior."}]}]},{"start":55,"end":59,"title":"False Witnesses","entries":[{"start":55,"end":55,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, of course, they found none. They were wasting their time. So they will have to lie about something. They will have to break their own religious law to kill Christ. They have had no problem doing that anyway."}]},{"start":56,"end":56,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you’re going to lie, you need to be good at it. They were not on the same page, though. I’m sure they specifically weren’t doing any of the actual lying in this scene. They probably just hired some people to do it. The problem is, they didn’t have matching stories, and their lies were just drawing confusion as the conclusion rather than proof and evidence. Of course, they had no proof, so this whole thing can’t stick."},{"kind":"para","text":"Surely Jesus will just be let go at this point; if it happened to anyone else, they would just give up and let them go. Not with Jesus, though. They truly and genuinely hated Him. The world’s religion still hates Him today. I wonder if these were “Jehovah’s Witnesses” trying to witness against Christ. (That’s a joke, of course.)"}]},{"start":57,"end":58,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, He did actually say this. These false witnesses probably weren't there when He said it, though. They are just trying to throw something at Him. Even this didn't stick because there was no actual law that was broken. Saying something and doing something are two very different things. And of course, we know that Jesus was speaking about Himself;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus answered and said unto them, Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up. Then said the Jews, Forty and six years was this temple in building, and wilt thou rear it up in three days? But he spake of the temple of his body.","ref":"John 2:19-21"}]},{"start":59,"end":59,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were throwing in half-truths to try and make their lies agreeable, but not even THAT was working. The Lord is innocent. But they won't let up. The world will not let up on you today either. They will throw whatever they can to choke you up and hurt you. That is not just pessimism, that's the gospel truth. If they did it to Christ, they will gladly and more easily do it to you."}]}]},{"start":60,"end":65,"title":"He Is the Christ","entries":[{"start":60,"end":61,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus didn’t have to say anything. They were making themselves look like fools on their own. Jesus was just standing and listening. He knew that what they were saying was false, and what was He going to do against false witnesses? The proof of what was true was found in the three years of ministry that Jesus had been doing with His disciples and thousands of other eyewitnesses. The religionists weren’t going to be able to do much against the many people who experienced new life from the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"But this specific question catches Jesus’ attention. He cannot deny who He is. So He is going to answer their question to progress this whole conversation further. Jesus was going to go to the cross. They didn’t need to speak any more lies. They could just do what they were going to do anyway. So Jesus gives the truth one last time, and it’s this truth that He speaks next that gives them what they need to kill Jesus."}]},{"start":62,"end":62,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a bold acclamation. It is also the truth. He has never been quiet about His part in the Godhead. People say that Jesus doesn't ever say that He is God, and yet here we have Him openly saying it without a worry. He is the Christ! He has made that clear quite a bit through this gospel account. It was the evidence that Peter mentioned a few chapters back. It's the rock on which the Lord will build His church. He builds it on this truth. It will be this truth that seals His death as well."}]},{"start":63,"end":65,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At this point, the religionists turn on the Lord. They spit on Him, cover His face, hit Him, and mock Him. Let’s remember that these are religionists. You know why I have a problem with modern religion? Well, it hasn’t changed much from what it was at this moment in history with the Lord. They are all still guilty of accusing Christ of being someone He is not. They forsake the actual purpose of His coming, which is to be crucified and to rise on the third day. That is the gospel. That is salvation. That is truth. Whether a religion wants to agree with that or not doesn’t matter. The Apostles and Prophets all agree that Jesus is the Christ and that He has saved the world from their sins if the world is ready to turn to Him."}]}]},{"start":66,"end":72,"title":"Peter's Denial","entries":[{"start":66,"end":66,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's not forget Peter is witnessing this mistreatment from a distance. He is probably feeling pretty disgusted and sick about the way in which they were treating his Lord at the moment. He was also probably wondering why Jesus wasn't stopping them from going any further. A maid comes over and sees him as he's warming himself by the fire."}]},{"start":67,"end":67,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When you come to know the Lord, it is impossible for you to reject it ever again. It's impossible for that Holy Spirit's glow to just disappear. If people cannot recognize that you have been with Christ because you are so silent about Him, then you probably never actually had Christ to begin with. Judas would not have gotten this same question. Only Peter does."}]},{"start":68,"end":68,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think the rooster was a background noise at this moment. Peter heard it, but didn't pay much attention to it. He was more worried about getting caught and being mistreated just like the Lord was. So he is seen denying the Lord by this maid who could do nothing against him anyway."}]},{"start":69,"end":70,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Deny it all he wants, they could tell that Peter was one of the Lord's disciples. You can never really quit ministry, by the way. Trust me, I've tried. Somehow, the Lord leads me back into it and gives me new vigor. I don't know why. I've had pastors and \"ministers\" tell me that I was no good for ministry. Some have not even directly told me that, but they push me away from it, insinuating it. They may even give me a different title that they think fits me better in their own eyes. Yet, it's the Lord that keeps pulling me into ministry HIS way. It's never done in my way or the leaders’ way. It's always the Lord doing it. Peter can deny it all he wants, but he will be pulled into it no matter what because that is how the Lord works."}]},{"start":71,"end":71,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Cursing and swearing would certainly take them by surprise. They would likely think, \"With a mouth like a sailor, perhaps he really wasn't ever with the Lord.\" Peter was doing whatever he could to reject his calling now."}]},{"start":72,"end":72,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He didn't just weep. Peter has cleaned this up a bit. Luke gives us a better picture of this situation."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Peter went out, and wept bitterly.","ref":"Luke 22:62"},{"kind":"para","text":"He was crying \"bitterly.\" \"Bitterly\" means \"with poignant grief.\" It was unbearable, and uncontrollable crying. He actually hurt himself by his reaction to this lady. At one point, he was standing up for the Lord, willing to die for the Lord, and giving everything he had for the ministry of the Lord. Now he's rejecting the Lord and turning away from following Him. If you are not in ministry, you are either burdened or weeping bitterly. This is just the start to this Friday. We have a lot more to cover in the next chapter. Unless you have no heart at all, you will likely be weeping bitterly with Peter as we read on to what happens next."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":38},{"start":39,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":43},{"start":44,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":46},{"start":47,"end":47},{"start":48,"end":50},{"start":51,"end":52},{"start":53,"end":53},{"start":54,"end":54},{"start":55,"end":55},{"start":56,"end":56},{"start":57,"end":58},{"start":59,"end":59},{"start":60,"end":61},{"start":62,"end":62},{"start":63,"end":65},{"start":66,"end":66},{"start":67,"end":67},{"start":68,"end":68},{"start":69,"end":70},{"start":71,"end":71},{"start":72,"end":72}],"verses":{"1":"After two days was the feast of the passover, and of unleavened bread: and the chief priests and the scribes sought how they might take him by craft, and put him to death.","2":"But they said, Not on the feast day, lest there be an uproar of the people.","3":"And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.","4":"And there were some that had indignation within themselves, and said, Why was this waste of the ointment made?","5":"For it might have been sold for more than three hundred pence, and have been given to the poor. And they murmured against her.","6":"And Jesus said, Let her alone; why trouble ye her? she hath wrought a good work on me.","7":"For ye have the poor with you always, and whensoever ye will ye may do them good: but me ye have not always.","8":"She hath done what she could: she is come aforehand to anoint my body to the burying.","9":"Verily I say unto you, Wheresoever this gospel shall be preached throughout the whole world, this also that she hath done shall be spoken of for a memorial of her.","10":"And Judas Iscariot, one of the twelve, went unto the chief priests, to betray him unto them.","11":"And when they heard it, they were glad, and promised to give him money. And he sought how he might conveniently betray him.","12":"And the first day of unleavened bread, when they killed the passover, his disciples said unto him, Where wilt thou that we go and prepare that thou mayest eat the passover?","13":"And he sendeth forth two of his disciples, and saith unto them, Go ye into the city, and there shall meet you a man bearing a pitcher of water: follow him.","14":"And wheresoever he shall go in, say ye to the goodman of the house, The Master saith, Where is the guestchamber, where I shall eat the passover with my disciples?","15":"And he will shew you a large upper room furnished and prepared: there make ready for us.","16":"And his disciples went forth, and came into the city, and found as he had said unto them: and they made ready the passover.","17":"And in the evening he cometh with the twelve.","18":"And as they sat and did eat, Jesus said, Verily I say unto you, One of you which eateth with me shall betray me.","19":"And they began to be sorrowful, and to say unto him one by one, Is it I? and another said, Is it I?","20":"And he answered and said unto them, It is one of the twelve, that dippeth with me in the dish.","21":"The Son of man indeed goeth, as it is written of him: but woe to that man by whom the Son of man is betrayed! good were it for that man if he had never been born.","22":"And as they did eat, Jesus took bread, and blessed, and brake it, and gave to them, and said, Take, eat: this is my body.","23":"And he took the cup, and when he had given thanks, he gave it to them: and they all drank of it.","24":"And he said unto them, This is my blood of the new testament, which is shed for many.","25":"Verily I say unto you, I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine, until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of God.","26":"And when they had sung an hymn, they went out into the mount of Olives.","27":"And Jesus saith unto them, All ye shall be offended because of me this night: for it is written, I will smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered.","28":"But after that I am risen, I will go before you into Galilee.","29":"But Peter said unto him, Although all shall be offended, yet will not I.","30":"And Jesus saith unto him, Verily I say unto thee, That this day, even in this night, before the cock crow twice, thou shalt deny me thrice.","31":"But he spake the more vehemently, If I should die with thee, I will not deny thee in any wise. Likewise also said they all.","32":"And they came to a place which was named Gethsemane: and he saith to his disciples, Sit ye here, while I shall pray.","33":"And he taketh with him Peter and James and John, and began to be sore amazed, and to be very heavy;","34":"And saith unto them, My soul is exceeding sorrowful unto death: tarry ye here, and watch.","35":"And he went forward a little, and fell on the ground, and prayed that, if it were possible, the hour might pass from him.","36":"And he said, Abba, Father, all things are possible unto thee; take away this cup from me: nevertheless not what I will, but what thou wilt.","37":"And he cometh, and findeth them sleeping, and saith unto Peter, Simon, sleepest thou? couldest not thou watch one hour?","38":"Watch ye and pray, lest ye enter into temptation. The spirit truly is ready, but the flesh is weak.","39":"And again he went away, and prayed, and spake the same words.","40":"And when he returned, he found them asleep again, (for their eyes were heavy,) neither wist they what to answer him.","41":"And he cometh the third time, and saith unto them, Sleep on now, and take your rest: it is enough, the hour is come; behold, the Son of man is betrayed into the hands of sinners.","42":"Rise up, let us go; lo, he that betrayeth me is at hand.","43":"And immediately, while he yet spake, cometh Judas, one of the twelve, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and the scribes and the elders.","44":"And he that betrayed him had given them a token, saying, Whomsoever I shall kiss, that same is he; take him, and lead him away safely.","45":"And as soon as he was come, he goeth straightway to him, and saith, Master, master; and kissed him.","46":"And they laid their hands on him, and took him.","47":"And one of them that stood by drew a sword, and smote a servant of the high priest, and cut off his ear.","48":"And Jesus answered and said unto them, Are ye come out, as against a thief, with swords and with staves to take me?","49":"I was daily with you in the temple teaching, and ye took me not: but the scriptures must be fulfilled.","50":"And they all forsook him, and fled.","51":"And there followed him a certain young man, having a linen cloth cast about his naked body; and the young men laid hold on him:","52":"And he left the linen cloth, and fled from them naked.","53":"And they led Jesus away to the high priest: and with him were assembled all the chief priests and the elders and the scribes.","54":"And Peter followed him afar off, even into the palace of the high priest: and he sat with the servants, and warmed himself at the fire.","55":"And the chief priests and all the council sought for witness against Jesus to put him to death; and found none.","56":"For many bare false witness against him, but their witness agreed not together.","57":"And there arose certain, and bare false witness against him, saying,","58":"We heard him say, I will destroy this temple that is made with hands, and within three days I will build another made without hands.","59":"But neither so did their witness agree together.","60":"And the high priest stood up in the midst, and asked Jesus, saying, Answerest thou nothing? what is it which these witness against thee?","61":"But he held his peace, and answered nothing. Again the high priest asked him, and said unto him, Art thou the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?","62":"And Jesus said, I am: and ye shall see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.","63":"Then the high priest rent his clothes, and saith, What need we any further witnesses?","64":"Ye have heard the blasphemy: what think ye? And they all condemned him to be guilty of death.","65":"And some began to spit on him, and to cover his face, and to buffet him, and to say unto him, Prophesy: and the servants did strike him with the palms of their hands.","66":"And as Peter was beneath in the palace, there cometh one of the maids of the high priest:","67":"And when she saw Peter warming himself, she looked upon him, and said, And thou also wast with Jesus of Nazareth.","68":"But he denied, saying, I know not, neither understand I what thou sayest. And he went out into the porch; and the cock crew.","69":"And a maid saw him again, and began to say to them that stood by, This is one of them.","70":"And he denied it again. And a little after, they that stood by said again to Peter, Surely thou art one of them: for thou art a Galilaean, and thy speech agreeth thereto.","71":"But he began to curse and to swear, saying, I know not this man of whom ye speak.","72":"And the second time the cock crew. And Peter called to mind the word that Jesus said unto him, Before the cock crow twice, thou shalt deny me thrice. And when he thought thereon, he wept."}},
    "15": {"chapter":15,"intro":[],"previously":["We have been progressing through the week in rapid succession in our last chapter. We ended it with Friday night (likely past midnight) as the religionists were prepared to pin Jesus for saying a bunch of different lies that could not be provenly true. They ask Him if He is the Christ, and He admits the truth. That sets them off, and they use that as a tool to justify their lies in today's chapter. They are taking Jesus to Pilate to get Him crucified. We will see that Pilate doesn't agree with their reasons for this, and tries to put a stop to it from getting too far out of hand, but he is not able to do much of anything with so many people starting to revolt. So in order to keep chaos at bay, he gives them what they want. They want the crucifixion of Christ. This is a deep and difficult chapter, so prepare yourself with tissues (that is the case for every time the crucifixion is talked about in the gospels)."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"In the Morning","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This must have been pretty early in the morning. They thought that Jesus’ acclamation was something important to pursue. They were going to kill a man, not just based on who He said He was, but also based on the fact that He proved it. How disgustingly evil our world is. The reason they went to Pilate with this cause was because they could not execute Jesus. They took Him to the Roman court so they could put Him to death. The crazy part is, what they were accusing Jesus of was not at all illegal to the Roman government. The Romans wouldn’t care about who Jesus said or didn’t say He was. So the Jewish religionists had to make up some sort of story so that it became a Roman problem as well."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus is not denying this accusation. He agrees to Pilates’ assessment, but He won't agree to the other charges set against Him. He won't disagree with them either, which is what catches Pilates’ attention:"}]},{"start":3,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What is Jesus going to say against His accusers? Well, actually, He could have easily stood up for Himself here. He could have also proved His power and might over them pretty easily. I don’t think they would have stood a chance against the Lord at all. Fire literally could have rained down on them. He could have turned them all into ducks. Let your imagination run wild with ideas! The Lord is capable of doing anything."},{"kind":"para","text":"But what the Lord chooses to do is remain quiet against His accusers. Why? Well, this is the only way He will be put to death. You see, He HAS to die in this moment. He has to sacrifice His life for the sins of the world. That is what puts Him on the cross after all. It is the world’s sin that crucifies Jesus. So He says nothing because He knows what needs to happen. He does not want to save people merely by sight. He wants to save people by faith. That won’t happen if He just shows them His power. He MUST sacrifice Himself. Blood is the atonement for sin."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.","ref":"Leviticus 17:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"It was their atonement in the Old Testament sacrifice, and it is our atonement today. Jesus gave His perfect blood, though. He completed the law, and no other blood could do that."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":14,"title":"The Replacement","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, it seems the Romans are about to show some pity on a man that was not ACTUALLY innocent. It was a custom for them to release a prisoner. I assume they only do this on feast days? Either way, Pilate is going to go out and ask who they want released."}]},{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's a multitude crying here. I assume the multitude consisted of religious zealots. They were mostly priests and scribes standing in this moment. That is who brought Jesus to Pilate this day. Take that into consideration at this moment as we read what is happening here. The religionists are the ones that are \"crying\" aloud as Pilate asks who they want him to release."}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pilate is not stupid; he is a politician. You can only trust a politician so far. But everything he is doing for the crowd right now is for the sake of his own image. It has nothing to do with his actual kindness for the Jews. He is actually hoping they will let this Jesus fellow go. He was a politician, but he was still trying to be fair. In Pilate’s eyes, Jesus had done nothing wrong, and yet the Jews were still trying their hardest to pin something on Him. They threw up all sorts of lies just to make sure that Pilate would kill Him. But Pilate is going to keep trying to free Jesus."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The chief priests, who were the religionists, controlled the crowd and persuaded them to turn to Barabbas and let him go. Remember, Barabbas was a literal murderer. The crowd was willing to allow someone guilty of actual murder to be released so that Jesus, who was guilty of nothing, could be killed instead. Barabbas, whether he knew it or not, was just saved by the Lord. Barabbas serves as a picture of us. We are guilty and deserve our punishment. The Lord takes our place for us, and we are set free. We are so undeserving…"}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pilate is still trying to free Jesus. There was no reason to let Barabbas go and Jesus die. I think it should be well known that the Israelites were all to live for the Lord in a certain way that would attract their neighbors. Those who don’t know the Lord should have a desire to turn to the Lord because of the life that the Israelites were leading. That didn’t happen very often."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see it come to light in the book of Ruth, but that is really it. There are a few other instances, but the Israelites didn’t seem to like living for the Lord most of the time. Here, with a ruler in the Roman government, the Jews had a chance to convert a government official. They don’t see it that way, though. They are under the impression that anyone who is not a Jew cannot be converted to following the Lord. Even if they did believe it, they were about to lose their chance because of the way they were acting and treating one of their own innocent people. I do believe that Jesus has gotten Pilate’s attention, though. Otherwise, Pilate would have no problem just giving them Jesus to be crucified."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn’t even respond to Pilate’s question. They just cried out, “Crucify him.” What a heart-wrenching cry. These people were so full of hate over a man full of love and compassion for the lost. He reached people that the religionists wouldn’t, and that is why they hated Him. They hated Him because He was actually doing righteous work without a profit, and they only did it to get one. They couldn’t control Him because nobody was in the Lord’s pocket. He was provided for over and over again without having to even work, and that was really irritating them. They needed a way to control Jesus, and seeing as they couldn’t find one, they killed Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the state of all sinners by the way. If they can’t figure out how to control the Lord, they will just reject Him and do away with Him. They will pretend like He doesn’t exist because they can’t do anything with Him anyway. It should be the other way around. We should be willing to be controlled by the Lord rather than to try and control Him. That is the only way we can have salvation."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":21,"title":"Pick Up Your Cross","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Scourging was another custom of the Romans. They would beat their prisoners nearly to death before they brought them to a crucifixion. At this point, Jesus would already be close to death. But the pain does not just end here."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Praetorium\" means “headquarters.\" It would be the dwelling of the commander-in-chief. It was probably used to give criminals their sentences. Kind of like a courtroom today. Except there are no lawyers, no jury, and no defense. It would just be sentencing, and then rolling out that sentence immediately. Jesus has already started the long, brutal process to His crucifixion; and it's about to get worse:"}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were mocking Him and pretending like He was a king. This was the Roman soldiers doing this, by the way. They thought this was a funny little game they were playing with an innocent man's life. The religionists wouldn't care either. They would make the same mocking as Christ carries His cross and is crucified. Once He leaves this area, it will just be constant, brutal, wicked, mocking, lying, hitting, spitting, and hate on Jesus. I am so thankful that this will never happen again."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Imagine being one of the religionists or soldiers in the crowd as this is happening. Imagine being the one who hit Jesus or spit on Him. Imagine as if you are there watching this all unfold, and you are happy to mock the guy who has done nothing but love people and heal them. That is what the world does to Him today. When people reject Him, or ignore Him, or debate against Him, they are purposely spitting in His face, hitting Him with their hands and reed, and mocking Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a shame that they think this is good mature behavior. What a shame that they could treat ANY human being this way, but especially one that has literally done nothing wrong. They had no problem treating Him this way because He said things they didn’t like. He lived perfectly, and that made them mad. They couldn’t pin anything on Him, and that made them angry. How shallow and selfishly minded."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus had already been carrying His cross, as the other gospels say. I assume Jesus carried His cross as far as He could, but when He reached the city gates, He had lost all strength. This Simon fellow happened to be in Jerusalem at the time and was called out of the crowd to help Jesus the rest of the way. Whether or not this Simon man was sympathetic to Christ is not understood here. I assume he may have been, or perhaps he was just scared to be hurt by the crowd or the Roman soldiers if he didn’t do what he was told. He was probably thinking, “I should have just stayed in Cyrene.” But thankfully, he didn’t. Now his name is penned in this holy book for the rest of history."},{"kind":"para","text":"I like to believe that this moment changed the heart of Simon. He probably didn’t know much about the Lord until this moment, and he felt convicted by Christ. He literally picked up the Lord’s cross and followed Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then said Jesus unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me. For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: and whosoever will lose his life for my sake shall find it. For what is a man profited, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?","ref":"Matthew 16:24-26"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Lord is about to give up His life for this man. He is giving it for every single person there. He’s giving it for the people who hit Him, for the people who spit on Him, for the soldiers who scourged Him, for the religionists who lied about Him, for the disciples who forsook Him, for the prophets who preached about Him, for the people who denied Him, and for the people who are still to come after Him. He gave it for the church that was about to be built from this moment. He gave it so that you and I reading today could have forgiveness from our unbelief, forgiveness for our sins against Him, and so that we can know Him just as personally as the disciples did. He gave His life so we don’t have to die for our sins. He gave His life to Barabbas, who deserved to die. He gave His life for you, who deserves death as well. Praise the Lord that He was willing to bear the pain of all eternity so that we can have life with Him and through Him!"}]}]},{"start":22,"end":28,"title":"The Place of a Skull","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, this place was called this because the hillside actually looked like a skull. What a gruesome place to die! It's on this symbol of death that we are going to receive new life."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This drink was to help ease His pain a little bit. He refused it, though. He had said at His last supper that He is not going to be drinking from the vine until He drinks it new in Heaven. He stuck to that, even if it meant it would help aid His pain from this excruciating death. I do find it interesting that it's mixed with myrrh, though. That was a gift that was given to Him when He was a baby."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when they were come into the house, they saw the young child with Mary his mother, and fell down, and worshipped him: and when they had opened their treasures, they presented unto him gifts; gold, and frankincense, and myrrh.","ref":"Matthew 2:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now it's a gift that was given to Him when He was at His death; I believe the symbol in BOTH cases was the same. The myrrh represented His sacrifice for mankind even as a gift to Him as a baby. The wise men knew what they were giving when they gave it."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's not miss the fact that crucifixion required driving railroad spike-sized nails through one’s hands and feet. He has already been beaten every which way, and now they hammered nails into His body. The pain is STILL not over. And that is just a small glimpse of it. None of the writers go into any greater detail about the crucifixion. We go based on proven historical facts about what that was to look like. They nailed Him to a cross, and then they stripped Him naked, and \"cast lots\" with His garments. That was a prophecy they didn't even realize they were fulfilling as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"They part my garments among them, and cast lots upon my vesture.","ref":"Psalm 22:18"}]},{"start":25,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"His accusation was that He was “The King of the Jews.” What a strange reason to kill a man. It’s an extremely brutal and evil reason to kill a man. There are a lot of people out on the streets today who claim to be Christ reincarnate. I don’t think anyone’s first thoughts are to fix them to a cross and kill them. Most people just think they are out of their minds on drugs and alcohol (and that is likely true about them). They wouldn’t go so far out of their way to try and lay the death penalty on them."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was different with Jesus, though, because He could prove that He is God in the flesh. They did not kill Him because He was “accusing” Himself of being God. They killed Him because He had proven it. That is how people reject Him today. They don’t just reject faith without evidence. They reject proof. They accept science without evidence, but they don’t accept the evidence of God. It really does baffle our minds. But we were just like them once. We were all lost sinners at one point in our lives. We were just as dull and incompetent."},{"kind":"para","text":"When it says “the third hour,” it is meaning nine o’clock in the morning. I’m sure none of these people had a very good and safe night’s rest. It was still a long day ahead as Jesus hangs there in His crucifixion."}]},{"start":27,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He was treated as just another common criminal. The scripture was fulfilled from Isaiah. Isaiah 53 (all of it) depicts this very moment in history. It's the promise of our Savior, and it's coming to fruition right in front of the whole world’s eyes just in the way that Isaiah said it would happen."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore will I divide him a portion with the great, and he shall divide the spoil with the strong; because he hath poured out his soul unto death: and he was numbered with the transgressors; and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors.","ref":"Isaiah 53:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"I highly recommend going to Isaiah 53 and reading the whole chapter at this moment before proceeding onto the rest of this chapter in Mark. It will be beneficial to really seal in this circumstance. The gospel writers have given us basic details about the crucifixion, but Isaiah gets deeper."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":32,"title":"A Continual Mocking","entries":[{"start":29,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He does not need the saving. He is the perfect Son of God. He is saving others by giving His life for them. That’s what this moment is for. It’s not for Jesus. It’s for you. He had you in mind on that cross. That is the only reason He goes through it. He did it because He loves you."},{"kind":"para","text":"Do you love someone enough to give your life in suffering for them? I believe I would for my wife. I would not want anything to happen to her. If it had to be me suffering or her suffering, I would always choose me in a heartbeat. My wife was suffering from gallstones for years. It was the most pain I had ever seen a person in. We kept going to the doctors, and they just kept telling her to lose weight. We didn’t know how serious it was until we moved to Texas, and they said that it could cause cancer if they don’t remove it quickly. Every time she was experiencing pain, she would be in the bathroom for hours crying and throwing up. There was nothing I could do. I just sat in the living room and prayed and cried because no doctors wanted to help. My prayer was that God would take her pain away. If He needed someone to feel pain, that He could place that pain on me. I begged that He would just give me the pain so she wouldn’t have to feel it. Melody and I would both pray for her."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus took the pain for us. He sacrificed His life for us. He did not want you to go through an eternity of pain, so He gave you a way to put the pain on Him. That is the picture being presented here. “He saved others…” He saves everyone that comes to Him in faith and believing."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have already seen Christ prove Himself, and they would not take it. This will look bleak to the world. It will look like a loss to the disciples. But the Lord will (yet again) prove Himself in three days. We know they remembered the remark about the temple (in verse 29), but they seem to ignore the “three days” part. But we know that even with Christ coming back from the dead, no amount of proof of the Lord will be good enough for a nonbeliever."},{"kind":"para","text":"God will literally be sending balls of fire from Heaven, and people will still curse Him and turn from Him. He will make the sun and moon stop shorter, and people will still find reason to reject Him. No matter what, mankind WANTS to reject God. They WANT to reject Him because He doesn’t live up to their standards and desires. He doesn’t need to live up to OUR standards. His standards are far above ours. We should not want to dumb God down to our finite boxes we set up for ourselves. We should want to dumb ourselves down to accept the power and largeness of God. I think that is the problem. We think we are too smart for our own good, and our own logic gets in the way of truth."}]}]},{"start":33,"end":38,"title":"Death's Sting","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"From twelve to about three in the afternoon, the land was quite dark. The scene was dark, but the sun was not shining the way it usually does at this time. Six hours of hanging on this cross was painful. But I think I should point something out here. Every hour (nay, every second of every minute), Jesus thought it was worth it. He suffered with joy! Why? Well, it certainly was because the Lord was with Him and gave Him the ability to endure this suffering. But it was also because He has so much love for you that He found it a joy to suffer for you. You are worth it to Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God.","ref":"Hebrews 12:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The people down below were mocking Him and trying to make this experience as unpleasant as possible, but the Lord above was casting a shadow over Christ and providing presence for Him. The heat of the sun was on Christ for the first three hours, and the clouds covered that the last three hours. God was still there, I think we should recognize that. He was with Christ. He IS Christ. Jesus was always God. He never stopped being God. Even while He was on the cross, He was God. He purposely allowed Himself to feel the full extent of the cross to prove His love for you."},{"kind":"para","text":"He could have numbed His pain in this process. No one would have blamed Him for it. He needed this death experience, though, to finalize His love for you. You cannot say that Jesus does not love you. He felt every bit of pain on that cross, and He did it so that you can never have an excuse to reject God’s love for you. Make up any excuse you want to; there is no doubt that Jesus loves you."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe we should take a moment to stop here and read Psalm 22. It will also help set the tone of this moment. But we should at least read the first verse:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me? why art thou so far from helping me, and from the words of my roaring?","ref":"Psalm 22:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"God was not far from Jesus. The religionists mocking the Lord would know exactly what Jesus is quoting from. But the strange part is the next verse:"}]},{"start":35,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Why Elijah? Well, I think it’s because of what was said earlier. The disciples questioned Jesus about Elijah coming to pave the way for Him, and Jesus referred them to John the Baptist. The religionists didn’t see John the Baptist as the symbol of Elijah. So they were somewhat expecting Elijah to somehow come to the rescue for Christ. This would certainly be proof that Jesus is who He says He is. Jesus doesn’t want them to simply be saved by forceful proof. He wants them to come to Him in faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is how all good things come to us, by the way. If you are a man of science, let me give you the eyes of faith. When you want to do a study on something (scientifically), your first step is to question something. You know what you WANT to be true, but you don’t know how to prove it. So you question it. Then you move on from there to testing it. You don’t know how it will work, but you know what you want the answer to be, so you test it a million different ways possible until you get the results you were looking for. Sometimes you don’t get the results, and you have to give up on that, but not before trying every possible test that you can fathom. No true scientist will give up testing if they truly believe something can be proven. Then, once you find a test that works, you PROVE it. You actually do what you have tested, and it works every time."},{"kind":"para","text":"Friend, that is faith. That is the same thing Christians do. They don’t know what is true until they have tested it. Faith is knowing that there is an answer to a question, and then testing it to see if it’s true. Once they have done enough tests to find the truth, they can then prove it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Prove all things; hold fast that which is good.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason the Lord was not going to call Elijah down to prove them was not because He needed to prove Himself. Rather, they needed to prove Him. They needed to have faith first. They needed to test Him first. Instead, they jumped straight to discrediting Him and crucifying Him. Even then, Jesus continuously gave evidence to His claims. They were never going to receive it, though, no matter what evidence Jesus showed them. Even if Elijah came down, they still wouldn’t believe. They would have still left Jesus on the cross. The burden of proof lies on your shoulders, not the Lord’s. God has given you ample evidence of the truth, and it’s up to you to prove it."}]},{"start":37,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The veil ripped from the top first. We will discuss the veil in a different setting. I assume the priests serving in the temple at that time were completely confused as to what was happening. The tearing of the veil and the death of the Lord are all representations of the same thing. Jesus died to give us full access to salvation. The veil was there to keep the sacrifice and the sinner separate. Jesus opened it to give us access to His holy sacrifice. We were now no longer separated by a veil. We are in full view of the Lord’s promises and presence. We no longer need a veil when we have Christ. We can study that better in Hebrews."}]}]},{"start":39,"end":41,"title":"Believers","entries":[{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This moment made a centurion soldier a believer. Again, unless they were Jewish, the culture back then kept Romans and Gentiles separate from each other. I assume this centurion was likely not Jewish. He was very much against the Jewish code. He had no problem killing and crucifying people before. But Jesus changed his entire outlook on eternity and faith. He truly believed that Jesus must have been genuinely the Son of God. Whatever happens to him after this, I don’t know. Scripture doesn’t tell us. But I like to believe he lived a completely changed life from this moment forward."}]},{"start":40,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus’ own disciples could not stay. I believe John was there for a good portion of this crucifixion (John says he was in his own gospel account), but we get an acclamation here that the women stayed with Him. They had followed Him for a very long time. I don’t know all the women who were there, but we are under the assumption based on these verses that there were quite a few who remained faithful to Him from the beginning to the end. They did not quit on the Lord. I believe they were still believers even into His death. I don’t think they ever stopped believing. I think after He was crucified, they thought He would just come back to life once He was taken off that cross. I think they were waiting for Jesus to prove Himself because they knew He was more than capable of doing it. Especially Mary Magdalene. She had personal experience with Jesus’ miracle work. They all did. Yet, the Lord was not doing anything. He was letting Himself die. The next three days are going to be devastating to them."}]}]},{"start":42,"end":47,"title":"A Burial Place","entries":[{"start":42,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have another believer. This Joseph character has not been mentioned before. He has observed from a distance but was not afraid to connect himself to the Lord, even after His death. He asks to take Jesus’ body. He has a place for it rather than the open pit that the Romans would likely put all of the other criminals’ bodies."}]},{"start":44,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I like to believe that the centurion called over was the same one who had just turned to Christ. Jesus seems to have died quickly compared to others who suffer crucifixion. Most cases of crucifixion would last for literal days, but Jesus died in hours. I’m glad the Lord did not suffer for any longer than those six hours on the cross."},{"kind":"para","text":"Two different words are used here for “body” in the Greek. Joseph uses the Greek word for “body” to express a life, as if the body was valuable and useful. Pilate uses the Greek word for “body” to express a corpse, lifeless and useless. Joseph saw the Lord’s body and knew that there was more to Him than just this death. There has been a promise made for quite a while now that Jesus would rise from the dead. Joseph likely believed it, and that is why he is offering his own tomb to bury the Lord."}]},{"start":46,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Two women (named Mary. For some reason were the persistent ones to follow Jesus. Maybe because they knew His mother’s name was Mary. Mary seems like a very common name used back then. They kept tabs on what happened with the body of Jesus. They were curious about the Lord's resurrection too. In fact, it was Martha (a different Mary's sister) that Jesus said He is the resurrection:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Martha saith unto him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection at the last day. Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live:","ref":"John 11:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"They were all certain about what He said, but they were not too confident that it was true. Boy did they want Christ's Words to be provenly true. And on that, we leave off on another cliffhanger. We are left off at the end of Friday. They had to sleep through the night knowing that their Christ had just been crucified. They would then spend the Sabbath in gloomy contemplation of Christ's death. It will not be a fun Saturday. I believe that is why we are going to skip Saturday. Peter doesn't think there is anything worth talking about that happened on Saturday. Our next chapter will start immediately on Sunday morning."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":34},{"start":35,"end":36},{"start":37,"end":38},{"start":39,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":41},{"start":42,"end":43},{"start":44,"end":45},{"start":46,"end":47}],"verses":{"1":"And straightway in the morning the chief priests held a consultation with the elders and scribes and the whole council, and bound Jesus, and carried him away, and delivered him to Pilate.","2":"And Pilate asked him, Art thou the King of the Jews? And he answering said unto them, Thou sayest it.","3":"And the chief priests accused him of many things: but he answered nothing.","4":"And Pilate asked him again, saying, Answerest thou nothing? behold how many things they witness against thee.","5":"But Jesus yet answered nothing; so that Pilate marvelled.","6":"Now at that feast he released unto them one prisoner, whomsoever they desired.","7":"And there was one named Barabbas, which lay bound with them that had made insurrection with him, who had committed murder in the insurrection.","8":"And the multitude crying aloud began to desire him to do as he had ever done unto them.","9":"But Pilate answered them, saying, Will ye that I release unto you the King of the Jews?","10":"For he knew that the chief priests had delivered him for envy.","11":"But the chief priests moved the people, that he should rather release Barabbas unto them.","12":"And Pilate answered and said again unto them, What will ye then that I shall do unto him whom ye call the King of the Jews?","13":"And they cried out again, Crucify him.","14":"Then Pilate said unto them, Why, what evil hath he done? And they cried out the more exceedingly, Crucify him.","15":"And so Pilate, willing to content the people, released Barabbas unto them, and delivered Jesus, when he had scourged him, to be crucified.","16":"And the soldiers led him away into the hall, called Praetorium; and they call together the whole band.","17":"And they clothed him with purple, and platted a crown of thorns, and put it about his head,","18":"And began to salute him, Hail, King of the Jews!","19":"And they smote him on the head with a reed, and did spit upon him, and bowing their knees worshipped him.","20":"And when they had mocked him, they took off the purple from him, and put his own clothes on him, and led him out to crucify him.","21":"And they compel one Simon a Cyrenian, who passed by, coming out of the country, the father of Alexander and Rufus, to bear his cross.","22":"And they bring him unto the place Golgotha, which is, being interpreted, The place of a skull.","23":"And they gave him to drink wine mingled with myrrh: but he received it not.","24":"And when they had crucified him, they parted his garments, casting lots upon them, what every man should take.","25":"And it was the third hour, and they crucified him.","26":"And the superscription of his accusation was written over, The King Of The Jews.","27":"And with him they crucify two thieves; the one on his right hand, and the other on his left.","28":"And the scripture was fulfilled, which saith, And he was numbered with the transgressors.","29":"And they that passed by railed on him, wagging their heads, and saying, Ah, thou that destroyest the temple, and buildest it in three days,","30":"Save thyself, and come down from the cross.","31":"Likewise also the chief priests mocking said among themselves with the scribes, He saved others; himself he cannot save.","32":"Let Christ the King of Israel descend now from the cross, that we may see and believe. And they that were crucified with him reviled him.","33":"And when the sixth hour was come, there was darkness over the whole land until the ninth hour.","34":"And at the ninth hour Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying, Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani? which is, being interpreted, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?","35":"And some of them that stood by, when they heard it, said, Behold, he calleth Elias.","36":"And one ran and filled a spunge full of vinegar, and put it on a reed, and gave him to drink, saying, Let alone; let us see whether Elias will come to take him down.","37":"And Jesus cried with a loud voice, and gave up the ghost.","38":"And the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom.","39":"And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of God.","40":"There were also women looking on afar off: among whom was Mary Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James the less and of Joses, and Salome;","41":"(Who also, when he was in Galilee, followed him, and ministered unto him;) and many other women which came up with him unto Jerusalem.","42":"And now when the even was come, because it was the preparation, that is, the day before the sabbath,","43":"Joseph of Arimathaea, an honourable counsellor, which also waited for the kingdom of God, came, and went in boldly unto Pilate, and craved the body of Jesus.","44":"And Pilate marvelled if he were already dead: and calling unto him the centurion, he asked him whether he had been any while dead.","45":"And when he knew it of the centurion, he gave the body to Joseph.","46":"And he bought fine linen, and took him down, and wrapped him in the linen, and laid him in a sepulchre which was hewn out of a rock, and rolled a stone unto the door of the sepulchre.","47":"And Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses beheld where he was laid."}},
    "16": {"chapter":16,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Not started"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Our last chapter was a difficult one. We finally come to the crucifixion of Jesus Christ. It was long and painful, and difficult for us to read, and certainly difficult for people to watch (especially His followers). The Lord was set on display for the world to mock, and boy did they do it. At the end of the chapter it seemed as if all hope was lost. Thankfully it doesn't just end there."},{"kind":"para","text":"In our chapter today, hope comes rushing back into the picture as we get the proof of Jesus' resurrection. He didn't just stay in that grave, and this is the proof that the disciples had (namely Peter who is likely the author of this account) to continue ministry and build the church."}],"previously":["Our last chapter was a difficult one. We finally come to the crucifixion of Jesus Christ. It was long and painful, and difficult for us to read, and certainly difficult for people to watch (especially His followers). The Lord was set on display for the world to mock, and boy did they do it. At the end of the chapter, it seemed as if all hope was lost. Thankfully, it doesn't just end there. In our chapter today, hope comes rushing back into the picture as we get the proof of Jesus' resurrection. He didn't just stay in that grave, and this is the proof that the disciples had (namely, Peter, who is likely the author of this account) to continue ministry and build the church."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Resurrection Sunday","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Friday was gloomy, Saturday passed quickly, and Sunday was just another day. Despite this, the two Marys could not stop thinking about Jesus. They were coming to the grave to pay Him a visit and “anoint” His body. His body was never anointed for the burial; it was by a different Mary for His sacrifice, but not for His burial. They couldn’t anoint Him the day of His burial because they were still reeling from what had just happened, and they couldn’t do it on the Sabbath without being accused and ridiculed by the religionists, so they waited until Sunday. At that point, the body would start to decay and smell. It is scientifically proven that a body takes three days before the decaying process begins, although it differs by body size. It is usually the maximum amount of days before it occurs. They were likely going into a tomb that would not smell very good."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They came as early as possible. They probably would have come earlier if they could have seen. They waited for the sun to come up so they had light. I believe these women did not sleep these past three days. They have been constantly thinking about the Lord. They were thinking about this visit today. They were ready and waiting to go see Him."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were eager to reach the grave, but on their way, they realized it might not be possible to actually get inside. A large stone was rolled over it, and there were guards watching, although that detail is not mentioned here. They saw the stone over the grave and knew they couldn’t move it when they arrived. However, since they were already halfway there, they continued on, likely praying for a way to reach Jesus’ body. Perhaps the Roman guards would help them move the stone if they asked nicely."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hey! Their prayers were answered! They probably got excited to see that. They thought, \"Well, gee. That was easy! But why?\" They were still ready to anoint the body of Jesus, though, and so they went into the grave."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was not the person they were expecting. They must have put the wrong address into their navigation."}]},{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are assured that this is an angel. We are also assured that they go to tell Peter and the other disciples. I find it interesting that the angel specifically points out Peter here. Either Peter just added that in, or Mary had told him that the angel called for him by name."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think Peter felt very disconnected from the Lord. I think he felt unworthy to be considered a disciple anymore. I think he doesn’t believe he will be forgiven. He probably feels like he was just as guilty of betraying the Lord as Judas was. He feels as if he put Christ on the cross by not sticking up for Him like he said he would. He even denied knowing Him. In Peter’s eyes, that was the same as rejecting salvation."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But whosoever shall deny me before men, him will I also deny before my Father which is in heaven.","ref":"Matthew 10:33"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter felt like he was now a lost cause, but the Lord did not want to leave him feeling that way."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, we know that Mary tells the disciples. We will get to that in a moment. But at the present, they are not sure if they know what they saw. Is it true? Is Jesus alive? You might be eager to know what happens next. Well, if you read more modern translations, they will tell you that this chapter (and book) ends right here in this verse. What a terrible way to end this gospel account. There is nothing fulfilling about it. There is no proof of the Lord’s resurrection, no commission of the Lord’s work to form the church, and no assurance to the believers. If it ends here, it would seem (in the eyes of Peter) that all was pretty hopeless."},{"kind":"para","text":"The reason that modern translations leave out the rest is because “some” of the earliest manuscripts don’t have the rest. Yet the majority of the manuscripts do! Seeing as we don’t have the original copy, I’m going to take the advice of the majority and assume that the remainder of this chapter is meant to be kept in."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":11,"title":"The Disciples Don't Believe","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Mary Magdalene is given assurance. She saw the tomb but didn’t see the Lord. She didn’t go to tell anyone but instead wandered around, wondering what to do next. Then Jesus appeared to her and gave her the ability to go and speak to the other disciples."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is an interesting note added here about Mary. We were not told that she had “seven devils.” She was cleansed of being possessed and stayed persistent with the Lord all the way through. She was proof of His miracle work. She was proof that faith in the Lord is the cure for a sin-sick lifestyle. She was genuine proof of a changed life. No one would be able to deny her testimony, and therefore when she went to tell them the truth, they should have had no problem believing her."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Okay, well I guess they will have a problem believing her. That is sad. They are grieving, though. In John, we are told that Mary specifically goes to Peter:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then she runneth, and cometh to Simon Peter, and to the other disciple, whom Jesus loved, and saith unto them, They have taken away the Lord out of the sepulchre, and we know not where they have laid him.","ref":"John 20:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The other disciples were present, but the angel instructed her to go see Peter along with the other disciples. Peter, however, did not believe he could be redeemed again. He thought he had lost his chance at salvation. Clearly, Jesus was not done with him yet. If we believe this is Peter’s account of the gospel story, we can be assured that Peter changes course and lives in forgiveness. However, none of the disciples believe at this moment. They are not going to take Mary’s word for it. But something sparked in Peter and John, and they are seen getting up to go check the grave."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then arose Peter, and ran unto the sepulchre; and stooping down, he beheld the linen clothes laid by themselves, and departed, wondering in himself at that which was come to pass.","ref":"Luke 24:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter doesn't understand what was happening. How did this take place? What happened to Jesus' body? What is he supposed to do now? John's account gives a slightly different viewpoint here:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Peter therefore went forth, and that other disciple, and came to the sepulchre. So they ran both together: and the other disciple did outrun Peter, and came first to the sepulchre.","ref":"John 20:3-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"John says he outran Peter to the gravesite. I'll let the Lord judge the winner. Either way, they were not sure what to make of this yet."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"title":"Two More Witnesses","entries":[{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have TWO MORE witnesses. We are not sure who these other two witnesses are really. We know from Luke that one of them was a man named Cleopas. We are not sure who the other person is, though. Either way, the disciples still didn't want to believe that Jesus was alive."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":18,"title":"The Great Commission","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus shows Himself to them. Again, the burden of proof falls on our shoulders. Jesus doesn’t need to prove Himself; we must search Him out and find the truth. We should be digging with everything we can to see what is true and what is not. Testing our questions and finding answers is what the faith of God is all about. That is how we can grow a genuine and deep connection with Him. It makes a relationship with Him so much more worth it."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are using our energy and every effort to search the Lord, we are showing our love for Him. We will do that for someone we love. We will do whatever it takes to find them. If (God forbid) one of my daughters ever went missing, you can bet any money that I would search for them unto the ends of the Earth. I would spend all of my time for the rest of my life looking for them until they were found. Why? Well, it’s because I love them. I could not just give up on them that easily."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now that they have seen the Lord alive and have been assured of His resurrection, they are sent out to preach this message to the world. We are not preaching a message of gloom and confusion. It’s not a message of doom and despair. We don’t preach a message of self-righteousness and clean living. We preach a message of a perfect Savior, who gave Himself for our sin and proved Himself through His resurrection."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have wholeheartedly believed the account of the Apostles here. I might have questions and I might have doubts, but I don’t doubt that Jesus rose from the dead. I do not doubt that He has been building my faith since 2014. I have seen Him do it. I have gone through battle, trial, pain, and hurt for His name’s sake. I have experienced the turbulent waves and a sea of tribulation that befalls sinful man, and I can see Him in the middle of it. I can see His presence every step of the way. I might lose sight of that like Peter, but also like Peter, I always come back to the Lord ready to try again."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The topic of baptism is something we can discuss in John, Acts, or even Romans. There are plenty of opportunities to talk about baptism. The important note to make here is that we are not saved by baptism. We are saved by believing. Notice it says, “but he that believeth not shall be damned,” not, “but he that believeth not and doth not get baptized shall be damned.” It’s by belief alone. Baptism is a representation of that belief, not a requirement. It’s proof rather than solidity. It’s the same with circumcision, but that is not something I will be talking about here. As I’ve said before, we can review that in Acts."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will see the disciples casting out devils, speaking in tongues, and performing miracles. Paul will pick up a serpent and not die from its bite. The Apostles will be taken care of repeatedly, even in the midst of shipwrecks and beatings. They will be healers and miracle workers to initiate the church. The Apostles will possess these abilities, but the church age today does not. I know that some religions believe we have “some” of these spiritual gifts today. However, we cannot just take “some” of them and try to explain away the others. We either take all of them and pretend we have them today, or we take none of them and prove why they don’t exist. J. Vernon McGee said, “I’ll be glad to prepare a formaldehyde cocktail if you think you can drink it.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s not to say there are no spiritual gifts anymore. We have some gifts like these still today, but not in the same way. The power of these gifts was only to initiate the church alone. Even in the early church, these gifts disappeared. We have people who can speak in tongues today, but they cannot speak in dual tongues at once. Actually, some people are double-tongued. They are able to say flattering things while simultaneously stabbing you in the back."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"title":"The Miracle","entries":[{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, Peter is having this written much later in the future. He has seen the signs work and has personally experienced what the Lord was talking about here. He can be assured that the Lord was true to His Word and that the church was the purpose of these gifts. Jesus commissioned the Apostles to preach and build churches, and thankfully, the Apostles did. They did because Jesus was proven, and the burden to preach was the burden of their proof. That is our burden today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should feel burdened to preach. We should feel a strong necessity to speak to everyone about the Lord, not just because Jesus commanded it, but because we know His love. We know and are assured of the truth. That is what John will focus on both in his gospel account and in the letters he writes to his churches. May that be the theme we get from this. Don’t miss out on the love of God because we are too focused on His actions. Focus on God’s love. Don’t miss Christ’s Words because you demand miracles. That is the real miracle anyway. Belief is a miracle."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20}],"verses":{"1":"And when the sabbath was past, Mary Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James, and Salome, had bought sweet spices, that they might come and anoint him.","2":"And very early in the morning the first day of the week, they came unto the sepulchre at the rising of the sun.","3":"And they said among themselves, Who shall roll us away the stone from the door of the sepulchre?","4":"And when they looked, they saw that the stone was rolled away: for it was very great.","5":"And entering into the sepulchre, they saw a young man sitting on the right side, clothed in a long white garment; and they were affrighted.","6":"And he saith unto them, Be not affrighted: Ye seek Jesus of Nazareth, which was crucified: he is risen; he is not here: behold the place where they laid him.","7":"But go your way, tell his disciples and Peter that he goeth before you into Galilee: there shall ye see him, as he said unto you.","8":"And they went out quickly, and fled from the sepulchre; for they trembled and were amazed: neither said they any thing to any man; for they were afraid.","9":"Now when Jesus was risen early the first day of the week, he appeared first to Mary Magdalene, out of whom he had cast seven devils.","10":"And she went and told them that had been with him, as they mourned and wept.","11":"And they, when they had heard that he was alive, and had been seen of her, believed not.","12":"After that he appeared in another form unto two of them, as they walked, and went into the country.","13":"And they went and told it unto the residue: neither believed they them.","14":"Afterward he appeared unto the eleven as they sat at meat, and upbraided them with their unbelief and hardness of heart, because they believed not them which had seen him after he was risen.","15":"And he said unto them, Go ye into all the world, and preach the gospel to every creature.","16":"He that believeth and is baptized shall be saved; but he that believeth not shall be damned.","17":"And these signs shall follow them that believe; In my name shall they cast out devils; they shall speak with new tongues;","18":"They shall take up serpents; and if they drink any deadly thing, it shall not hurt them; they shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover.","19":"So then after the Lord had spoken unto them, he was received up into heaven, and sat on the right hand of God.","20":"And they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following. Amen."}},
  },
};
